hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6001","5097 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih and Abu Razin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it - except that he said: Let him wash his hand twice or thrice."
"6002","5098 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ammar ibn Abi 'Ammar, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you wakes from his sleep, let him not dip his hand in the vessel until he washes it thrice, for he does not know upon what his hand spent the night. Abu Ja'far said: So we considered this hadith to stand upon what is intended by it, Allah willing, and found those addressed by what is in it used to urinate and not clean with water, sufficing with wiping with what they wiped with, and defecate and not clean with water, sufficing with cleansing with stones. And it was not secure from them that they sweat in their sleep, so their hands fall upon the place of urine of them and upon the place of stool of them, so their hands become impure thereby. So they were commanded to wash them before putting them into the vessels in which is the water with which they seek purification for their prayers, to put them into them upon knowledge from them of their purity. Even if they were, in the worship they were made to devote with, upon the purity which they may be certain of until they know with certainty its leaving that to its opposite. Of that is what was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding one to whom it is imagined while in prayer that he finds something, of his saying to him: Let him not leave until he finds a smell or hears a sound."
"6003","5099 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id, and from 'Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a man to whom it is imagined that he finds something in prayer. He said: Let him not leave until he hears a sound or finds a smell. So like that is one who slept upon a certain purity of his hand: that he not leave it to its opposite except by what he knows its leaving to that, a certain leaving. So it bore that what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in these reports was upon preference, not upon obligation. So one said: Abu Hurayrah was opposed in that by what he sought refuge from the evil of his opposer with, when there was no proof in it with him - for he repels what opposed him in it."
"6004","5100 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When one of you wakes from his sleep, let him not dip his hand in the vessel until he washes it thrice, for he does not know upon what his hand spent the night. So Qayn al-Ashja'i said to him: When we come to this mihras (stone water-trough) of yours at night, what shall we do? So Abu Hurayrah said: I seek refuge in Allah from your evil, O Qayn; thus I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying. Abu Ja'far said: This opposer of Abu Hurayrah with what he opposed him in this hadith had missed the meaning of what Abu Hurayrah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is the meaning we mentioned in this chapter. And what he commanded him was upon preference, not upon obligation, since there was no necessity calling him to contrary to that. So what he commanded him was worthier for him, because it is certainty, and what is besides it in it is doubt. And when he is driven to performing ablution from the mihras with which preference which he was commanded in the first hadith is not possible for him, he is excused in leaving the preference, and is upon his first certainty of the purity of his hand, as he is upon certainty of the purity of the water into which he puts it, until he knows with certainty what has taken his hand out of that purity it was upon, and what necessitates the impurity of the water into which he puts it. And there was nothing worthier for these reports than what we carried them upon, since that necessitates negating contradiction from them; and what seeks the opponent of that is carrying them upon what necessitates their inconsistency and contradiction. And we seek refuge in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from this doctrine, and we ask Him for success."
"6005","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the martyrs: who are they? 5101 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu al-'Ajfa, or from Ibn Abi al-'Ajfa, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And another thing you say in these campaigns of yours for one killed or wounded: Such-and-such was killed a martyr - and perhaps he had filled the lining of his saddle or the back of his saddle with gold or silver, seeking the world. So do not say that, but say as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies in the way of Allah, or is killed, is in Paradise."
"6006","5102 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu al-'Ajfa al-Sulami, who said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people, then he mentioned the like of it. So in this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing the people that whoever is killed or dies in the way of Allah is the martyr who deserves what the martyr deserves, not others than him of those killed in campaigns whose aim is other than the way of Allah. So one said: You have reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the drowned is a martyr, and that the burned is a martyr, among things of this kind - so he intended martyrdom for them due to what befell them of that, not due to other than it."
"6007","5103 - And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Jabir ibn 'Atik, from 'Atik ibn al-Harith ibn 'Atik - and he is the grandfather of 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah, the father of his mother - informing him: that Jabir ibn 'Atik informed him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came visiting 'Abdullah ibn Thabit and found him overcome, so his daughter said: By Allah, I had hoped you would be a martyr, for you had completed your preparation. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has made his reward fall according to his intention. And what do you count as martyrdom? They said: Being killed in the way of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Martyrdom is seven besides being killed in the way of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: the one who dies of plague is a martyr, the drowned is a martyr, the one with pleurisy is a martyr, the one with abdominal illness is a martyr, the burned is a martyr, the one who dies under rubble is a martyr, and the woman who dies in childbirth is a martyr. So in this hadith was what indicated that the people of martyrdom are those mentioned in it, by the meanings by which they were mentioned in it. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's - Majestic and Mighty - success and aid, was: that those mentioned in this hadith are those with whom are of their intentions what makes them deserve martyrdom, to the exclusion of others than them of their likes of those with whom is no praiseworthy intention by which they deserve martyrdom. And of what indicates that is what is in this address, of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressing the daughter of 'Abdullah ibn Thabit when she said to him what she said to him of what is mentioned in this hadith - Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has made his reward fall according to his intention - so that indicated that his reward which he deserves is only deserved by intention, and likewise the reward of others than him of those mentioned in this hadith is only deserved with an intention like it, and Allah knows best, and we ask Allah for success."
"6008","5104 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah al-Hadrami: that he heard Ibn Hujayrah informing, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five - whoever is taken in any of them is a martyr: the one killed in the way of Allah is a martyr, the drowned in the way of Allah is a martyr, the one with abdominal illness in the way of Allah is a martyr, the one who dies of plague in the way of Allah is a martyr, and the woman in childbirth in the way of Allah is a martyr. So what is in this hadith indicated that those mentioned in the hadith we mentioned before it with martyrdom, of the people of these things, are those who are in the way of Allah - and the ways of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, are His obediences. So whoever was in any of them, and something of what is in these reports befell him, was of the people of martyrdom whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, promised upon it what He promised them. And whoever was contrary to that was not of them. And that was confirmed and its meaning uncovered by what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than these hadiths."
"6009","5105 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Wa'il, from al-Ash'ari, who said: A man said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A man fights for spoils or for booty, a man fights for remembrance, and a man fights to be seen in his place - so who is in the way of Allah, Mighty and Majestic? He said: Whoever fights so that the word of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, be highest, is in the way of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that the fighter does not deserve martyrdom by his fighting until there is with him of his intention that the word of Allah be highest, as mentioned in this hadith. And that was also strengthened by his other hadith, and it is his saying: Actions are only by intention, and to a man is only what he intended."
"6010","5106 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi, that he heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab upon the pulpit saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Actions are only by intention, and to a man is only what he intended. So whoever's emigration was to Allah and to His Messenger, his emigration is to Allah and to His Messenger. And whoever's emigration was to a world he attains, or a woman he marries, his emigration is to what he emigrated to."
"6011","5107 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6012","5108 - And as Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Ashhab ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us, from Malik, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"6013","5109 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-'Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, who said: I heard 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6014","5110 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, who said: I heard 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6015","5111 - And as al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from 'Umar, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6016","5112 - And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, who said: I heard 'Umar addressing the people, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6017","5113 - And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that actions are only by intention, and that to a man is only what he intended. Then he informed regarding emigration with what he informed in it, and it is emigration to him, so he informed that what is sought by it is not deserved except by intention for that, because it is itself. So like that is what is besides it of these things mentioned in these reports: they are not deserved by the things mentioned in them until there is with them the intention which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed is deserved by. And there was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what enters into this meaning also."
"6018","5114 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to me, that Sahl ibn Abi Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated to him, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks Allah for martyrdom truthfully, Allah conveys him to the ranks of the martyrs, even if he dies upon his bed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that whoever has with him intention in his wishing martyrdom is thereby of its people, even if killing by it does not befall him, nor other than it of the things mentioned in these reports. And in that is proof of what we mentioned of what we carried the reports we mentioned in this chapter upon. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"6019","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The best of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it. 5115 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both saying: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The best of you is he who teaches the Qur'an and learns it. Abu 'Abd al-Rahman said: And that is what seated me in this seat. He said: And he used to teach the Qur'an."
"6020","5116 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi and Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. He said: And Abu 'Abd al-Rahman used to teach in the lifetime of 'Uthman until the time of al-Hajjaj, saying: That is what seated me in this seat of mine. And the wording is Abu al-Walid's."
"6021","5117 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim and Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"6022","5118 - And Yazid, Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, 'Ali ibn Shaybah, and Musa ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, saying: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it."
"6023","5119 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, both saying: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Alqamah ibn Marthad informed me, he said: I heard Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him - Shu'bah said: I said to him: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - who said: The best of you is he who teaches the Qur'an and learns it. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Shu'bah narrated this hadith, and al-Thawri differed with him in it, dropping Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah from its chain, so he was not mentioned in it."
"6024","5120 - As Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan al-Thawri narrating from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most excellent of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it."
"6025","5121 - And as Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Alqamah, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"6026","5122 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Alqamah, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: I heard 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6027","5123 - And as Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, both saying: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Alqamah, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Uthman, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The most excellent of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it - or teaches the Qur'an, so teach. Abu Ja'far said: Thus all people narrate, of those who narrate from al-Thawri, this hadith, not mentioning Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah in its chain - except Yahya ibn Sa'id, for he narrated it from Sufyan, mentioning Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah."
"6028","5124 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Ali informed us, he said: Yahya - meaning Ibn Sa'id - narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah and Sufyan narrated to us, both saying: 'Alqamah ibn Marthad narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The choicest of you is he who teaches the Qur'an or learns it. I said to Yahya: They do not say: from Sufyan, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah. He said: I heard it from Sufyan, then Sufyan narrated it to us, and I did not deny it."
"6029","5125 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami and Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, both saying: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Nu'man ibn Sa'd, who said: I heard 'Ali, upon him be peace, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The choicest of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it."
"6030","5126 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain."
"6031","5127 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shaybah al-Juddi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The choicest of you is he who recites the Qur'an and causes it to be recited. So we considered the meaning of this hadith to stand by it upon the meaning by which one who learns the Qur'an and teaches it deserves being choicest over others than him of his likes. So we found the community of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the best of communities, and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had preferred the generation he was sent among of it over its rest, then preferred the generation after it over its rest after it, by his saying: The best of my community is the generation I was sent among, then those after them. And we mentioned that with its chains in what has preceded from us in this book of ours. And in this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's informing the people what they become by of the choicest of the generation they are of, and that they are those who learn the Qur'an and teach it. And since they are thereby choicest, having excelled others than them of the people of the generation they are of - and they in themselves may be of differing excellence, so some of them be more excellent than others by an added meaning beyond the mentioned meaning in this hadith, of knowledge of the rulings of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, which are in His Book and which He conveyed upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of those whose rest are not in it likewise - so of that in him be more excellent than others than him of the people of his generation he is of. Then they be thus whenever they rise by a meaning of these meanings, and by other than them of the things they are praised upon, until whoever is thus excels others than him of those in his rank, so whoever is thus is the choicest of that rank, and they are thus rank after rank, until that ends to the one highest of them in all those meanings, so he is the best of them. And what we mentioned in the first generation of the community of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is in the second generation of it likewise, and in others than it of the generations in his community, generation by generation, likewise also. And we ask Allah for success."
"6032","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The child is for the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone - is there found contradicting it what was reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in negating the child by li'an? 5128 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that the child is for the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone."
"6033","5129 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us. And al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, both saying: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Ya'qub, from al-Hasan ibn Sa'd - al-Rabi' said in his hadith: the freedman of al-Hasan ibn 'Ali - from Rabah, who said: I came to 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that the child is for the bed."
"6034","5130 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child is for the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone."
"6035","5131 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"6036","5132 - And al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. He said: A party of the people of knowledge went to that the child born upon a man's bed, when he denies him, is not negated from him by li'an with him, nor by other than it - because he was born upon his bed. And of those that was reported from, of those mentioned, is one who had opposed al-Sha'bi in that, in a hadith reported from al-Sha'bi."
"6037","5133 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Ibrahim, Ibn Ma'qil, and Musa differed with me regarding the child of li'an, saying: We attach him to him. So I said: Shall I attach him to him after four testimonies by Allah that he is of the truthful, then at the fifth that the curse of Allah be upon him if he is of the liars? So they wrote regarding it to Madinah, and they wrote that he be attached to his mother. And what those who went to what we mentioned argued with, of the reports we reported, is no proof for them with us - because it may be that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by this saying mentioned from him in these reports the claimants of the children of the slave-women of others, as they used to claim them in ignorance until Islam came upon them while they were upon that. So there was from 'Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas regarding the son of the slave-woman of Zam'ah what there was, of what his brother Sa'd mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about him, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him what is mentioned from him in these reports. And we mentioned that with its chains in what has preceded from us in this book of ours. As for negating the children of wives, it is not of that in anything, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in that with mutual cursing (mula'anah), and returned the child denied by li'an to his mother, to the exclusion of the one born upon his bed."
"6038","5134 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invoked curses between a man and his wife, separated between them, and attached the child to the woman."
"6039","5135 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr and Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, both saying: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. So one said: Did anyone agree with Malik upon this hadith from Nafi', when others of the companions of Nafi' reported it and did not mention in it this phrase - and the group is worthier than the one? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, was: that Malik is a preserving, firm imam in his narration, of those if he reported a hadith alone with it, it would be accepted from him. And when he is thus, when he adds an addition in a hadith it is accepted from him - along with that we found this meaning regarding the child denied by li'an from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than the hadith of Ibn 'Umar."
"6040","5136 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah al-Himsi narrated to me, from 'Umar ibn Ru'bah, from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn 'Abdullah al-Nasri, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The woman secures three inheritances: her freed slave, her foundling, and the child upon whom she invoked curses."
"6041","5137 - And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb informed us, he said: 'Umar ibn Ru'bah narrated to us, he said: I entered with Abu Salamah al-Himsi upon him, and he narrated to us from 'Abd al-Wahid al-Nasri, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith as Baqiyyah narrated it, exactly. So in this hadith was the woman's securing the inheritance of her child upon whom she invokes curses, and in that was what indicated the severance of his lineage from the one she cursed with him, to her. And in it also is a chapter of jurisprudence, and it is her inheriting him by the return of his lineage to her and its severance from the one she cursed with him - beyond what she would inherit from him for a child she curses with. So in that is what indicates inheriting by kinship when the deceased has no agnates, and his mother is one of fixed share, so she inherits what remains of his inheritance thereby. And we ask Allah for success."
"6042","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying after his invoking curses between the two spouses between whom he invoked: Perhaps she will bring him black, curly-haired - and that she brought him thus. 5138 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hakim ibn Sayf narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, who said: Ibn Mas'ud said: A man stood in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday night and said: Do you see, if a man finds with his wife a man - if he kills him you kill him, and if he speaks you flog him, and if he is silent, he is silent upon severe rage? O Allah, judge. So the verse of li'an was sent down. He said: 'Abdullah said: So he was tried with it, and he was a man of the Ansar. He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and invoked curses with his wife. When his wife took to invoke, the Messenger of Allah said to her: Wait. When she turned away, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Perhaps she will bring him black, curly-haired. So she brought him black, curly-haired."
"6043","5139 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn 'Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-A'mash, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, letter for letter. Abu Ja'far said: The people of knowledge differed regarding a man denying the pregnancy of his wife: some said li'an is invoked between him and her upon it, as li'an is invoked between him and her upon it if it were born before that and he denied it - and it is the saying of Malik and al-Shafi'i, and Abu Yusuf had said it once, and it is not well-known from him. And others said li'an is not invoked between them upon it, because it may be that it is not a pregnancy in reality. And with them it is alike whether it is born after that, so it is known thereby that she was pregnant with him then, or it is born at what does not allow that she was pregnant with him then. And of those who went to that was Abu Hanifah. And others said li'an is not invoked between them upon it in the state of pregnancy with him until his mother gives birth to him at a time by which it is known that she was pregnant with him when the denial was from the one she was pregnant by upon his bed. And of those who said that of them was Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, and it is well-known from Abu Yusuf. And those who went to invoking li'an by pregnancy argued for what they said of that with a hadith reported by 'Abdah ibn Sulayman, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abdullah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invoked li'an by pregnancy. And that hadith's origin was only the two hadiths of 'Isa and Jarir which we reported from al-A'mash in this chapter, and there is in them no mention of invoking li'an by pregnancy - rather in them is mention of invoking li'an, not other than it. And it may be that it was invoking by accusation, not by pregnancy. So one said: In this hadith, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Perhaps she will bring him black, curly-haired - is what indicated that what was invoked with is that child about whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps she will bring him thus. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's - Mighty and Majestic - success and aid, was: that if the li'an were by that child, the ruling in it would not have differed - whether his mother brings him black, curly-haired, or brings him contrary to that - since the li'an had negated him from the one invoking with him. And there is not, after resemblance of the child invoked with to the one who invoked with him, what verifies that he is not from him, nor does nearness of resemblance to him verify that he is from him. And we ask Allah for success."
"6044","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning. 5140 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn 'Abbas: that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have had no contact with my wife since we pollinated the palms, and I found with my wife a man - and her husband was yellowish, thin-haired, lank in hair, and the one she was accused with tended to blackness, curly, tightly curled. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make clear. Then he invoked curses between them, and she brought him resembling the one she was accused with. So what was in this hadith was like that in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, and there is no proof in it that the li'an was by that child, or by the accusation to the exclusion of him."
"6045","5141 - And al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, who said: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invoked li'an between al-'Ajlani and his wife, and she was pregnant. So her husband said: By Allah, I have not approached her since we pollinated the palms - and al-'afr is watering the palms after leaving them from watering after pollination by two months. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make clear. So they claimed the woman's husband was thin in forearms and shanks, reddish-blond in hair, and the one she was accused with was the son of al-Sahma. So she brought a black boy, wide in forehead, curly, tightly curled, thick in forearms, fleshy in shanks. Al-Qasim said: Ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had said: O father of 'Abbas, is she the woman about whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I were to stone anyone without proof I would stone her? So Ibn 'Abbas said: No, but that woman had publicized in Islam."
"6046","5142 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Qasim, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its meaning. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith also had in it no mention of invoking li'an by pregnancy nor for other than it, so it is as we mentioned before it."
"6047","5143 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Mutual cursing was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so 'Asim ibn 'Adi said a saying in that, then left. Then a man of his people came to him complaining to him that he found with his wife a man. So 'Asim said: I was tried with this only due to my saying. So he took him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of what he found his wife upon. And that man was yellowish, scant in flesh, lank in hair, and the one he claimed he found with his family was dark, fleshy, thick. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make clear. So she gave birth resembling the man whom her husband mentioned he found with her. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invoked li'an between them. So a man said to Ibn 'Abbas in the gathering: Is she the one about whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I were to stone anyone without proof I would stone this one? So Ibn 'Abbas said: No, that is a woman who displayed evil in Islam."
"6048","5144 - And Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Kathiri al-Madini - and he is Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Kathir ibn al-Salt al-Kindi - narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father al-Qasim, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he said: The two who invoke curses were mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the rest of the hadith of Yusuf, letter for letter."
"6049","5145 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Yahya, who said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrating from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Mutual cursing was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of the two hadiths before it. So in the chain of this hadith was the entering of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim between Yahya ibn Sa'id and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad. And in its text was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invoking li'an between those two spouses after the placing of that pregnancy. So thereby it is negated that there be in this hadith proof for one who makes li'an obligatory by pregnancy. And the saying regarding pregnancy when denied is that there is no li'an by it until it is placed, due to what is known that she was pregnant with him when he was denied - then li'an be by him after that, as Abu Yusuf and Muhammad said. And with Allah is success."
"6050","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning. 5146 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated to us - and they claimed he is al-Busri - he said: Ibn 'A'idh narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Yazid informed me, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather: that a man of the Ansar of Banu Zurayq accused his wife, so he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he repeated that four times to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Allah sent down the verse of mutual cursing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is the questioner? A grave matter has come down from Allah. So the man refused but to invoke curses with her, and she refused but to ward off punishment from herself. So they invoked curses. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If she brings him small, yellowish, slight, thin-boned, he is for the invoker; and if she brings him black like the dark camel, he is for other than him. So she brought him black like the dark camel. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for him and assigned him to his mother's agnates. He said: Were it not for the oaths that have passed, there would have been for me in him such-and-such. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith, as we reported it before it, is of what has in it no clarification that what was invoked with was the pregnancy or the accusation - except that in it is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned the born child to his mother's agnates. So in that is what necessitates that the li'an was by him. And when it is established the li'an was by him, disagreement fell in it between 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas and 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr: one said it was before his mother placed him, and the other said after - and the reconciling view is that li'an is after birth, and Allah knows best, and we ask Allah for success."
"6051","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning. 5147 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Anas ibn Malik: that Hilal ibn Umayyah accused his wife with Sharik ibn Sahma. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch her; if she brings him white, lank, defective-eyed, he is for Hilal ibn Umayyah; and if she brings him dark, curly, thin-shanked, he is for Sharik ibn Sahma. So she brought him dark, curly, thin-shanked."
"6052","5148 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Makhlad ibn Husayn, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik: that Hilal ibn Umayyah accused Sharik ibn Sahma with his wife, so he raised that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: Bring four witnesses, otherwise a hadd upon your back. He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah knows I am truthful. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying to him: Four, otherwise a hadd upon your back. So he said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah knows I am truthful - saying that repeatedly - and Allah shall send down upon you what clears my back of flogging. So the verse of li'an came down: {And those who accuse their wives}. So he called Hilal, and he bore four testimonies by Allah that he is of the truthful, and the fifth that the curse of Allah be upon him if he is of the liars. Then the woman was called, and she bore four testimonies by Allah that he is of the liars. When at the fifth, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For it is binding. So she hesitated until we had no doubt she would confess. Then she said: I shall not disgrace my people the rest of the day. So she went on upon the oath. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch; if she brings him white, lank, defective-eyed, he is for Hilal ibn Umayyah; and if she brings him curly, thin-shanked, he is for Sharik ibn Sahma. So she brought him dark, curly, thin-shanked. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not for what preceded of the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, there would have been for me and for her a matter. He said: Al-qadi' of the eyes is long in the eye-slit, not open-eyed."
"6053","5149 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Imran ibn Yazid informed us, he said: Makhlad ibn Husayn al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, then he mentioned the like of it - except that he added at its end: not open-eyed, protruding them. And what we mentioned in what preceded of these chapters suffices us from repeating it in this chapter, since what is in it is of the form of what is in them. And we ask Allah for success."
"6054","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning. 5150 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi: that 'Uwaymir came to 'Asim ibn 'Adi and said: Do you see, a man who finds with his wife a man and kills him - will you kill him for him? Ask, O 'Asim, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So 'Asim came, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked the question and faulted it. So 'Uwaymir said: By Allah, I shall come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he came, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had sent down contrary to the saying of 'Asim. So he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has sent down regarding you two a Qur'an. So he called them, and they advanced and invoked curses. Then he said: I have lied against her, O Messenger of Allah, if I keep her. So he parted from her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command him to part from her. So it ran as a practice among the two who invoke curses. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch her; if she brings him red, short, like a waharah (small lizard), I see him only having lied against her; and if she brings him black, wide-eyed, with buttocks, I deem him only having told truth against her. So she brought him upon the disliked matter."
"6055","5151 - And al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, both saying: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, exactly. So in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: If she brings him thus, I see him only having told truth against her; and if she brings him thus, I see him only having lied against her. So in that was what indicated it was not from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) an establishing of lineage by a practice, nor a negating of it by its opposite of the practice - and that was only upon what falls in hearts in the like of this meaning. And that indicated that what preceded, of what we mentioned in the chapters we mentioned before this chapter, of what their reporters mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - if she brings him thus, he is for so-and-so; and if she brings him thus, he is for so-and-so - that is of what they were opposed in by what we reported from Sahl in it. And what we reported from Sahl in it is worthier than what we reported from them in it, due to the addition Sahl preserved and they fell short of. And in that was what indicated there was in that no establishing of lineage, nor negating of lineage. And we ask Allah for success."
"6056","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what the people of knowledge disputed after that regarding the obligatoriness of li'an by the denied pregnancy, and the falling of li'an by it. Abu Ja'far said: Some of those who went to establishing li'an by denying pregnancy before his mother places him argued for what they went to in that against their opponents in it, with the saying of Allah regarding divorced women: {And if they are with pregnancy, spend upon them until they place their burden}. So he says: Allah has assigned for the carried one, whose mother is divorced, upon the one she was made pregnant by before his mother places him, spending upon him - and it is the nourishment his mother is nourished with until there reaches him of it what his life is by. And the one she was made pregnant by is taken by that, judged upon with it, as he is taken by it if the carried one were placed before that. He said: And when that is thus, his deserving what we mentioned his deserving of it upon her divorcer was likewise that he deserves by it before her placing him, his invoking li'an when he denies him from her. So of our proof against this arguer with that for his opposition in it, by Allah's - Mighty and Majestic - success and aid, was: that the matter in that is not as he mentioned in it, nor is the spending which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, assigned in that upon the divorcer for the pregnant divorced woman due to her child, to be that counted for him - rather it is spending for the divorced woman herself, because she is in waiting-period from her divorcer, not because she is pregnant by him. And of what indicates that: that we saw the carried one, if he had wealth he inherited from a maternal brother who died while his mother is pregnant with him, that does not lift spending from his father upon his mother, and spending is upon his father as it was if he had no wealth. So that indicated that the spending judged upon the father is for the mother in her waiting-period, not for the carried one. And of that is that the divorcer is commanded to spend upon the divorced pregnant woman until she places, whether the carried one lives or dies, which indicates the spending is for the waiting-period, not for the child. So there is no proof in the verse for establishing li'an before birth, and the saying is that of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad: no li'an until birth, and with Allah is success. And this arguer also said: Of what indicates what we went to in establishing li'an by pregnancy is the established sunnah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his judging in the blood-money of quasi-intentional killing with the forty khalifah camels in whose bellies are their young. And he mentioned in that 5152 - what 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Qasim ibn Rabi'ah ibn Jawshan, from 'Uqbah ibn Aws al-Sadusi, from a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed on the day of the conquest of Makkah, and said in his address: Behold, the killing of mistaken-intentional with the whip, the stick, and the stone - in it is an aggravated blood-money, a hundred camels, of them forty khalifah in whose bellies are their young."
"6057","5153 - And what Isma'il ibn Hamdawayh al-Bikandi narrated to us, he said: 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man, Musaddad ibn Musarhad, and Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Qasim ibn Rabi'ah ibn Jawshan, from 'Uqbah or Ya'qub al-Sadusi, from 'Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it - except that Musaddad and al-Himmani did not doubt, and said in their hadiths: from al-Qasim ibn Rabi'ah, from 'Uqbah ibn Aws, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This sayer said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with khalifat (pregnant camels) in blood-money - and they are the pregnant - and if pregnancy were unknown and unperceived, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have judged with it, nor imposed it upon anyone. And in that was what indicated that pregnancy is perceived, and that judgment is applied in it before his mother places him as it is applied in it after her placing him. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's - Majestic and Mighty - success and aid, was: that there is no proof for him in what he argued with of that for what he argued for with it, because what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned of these camels, he assigned by the outward of what those camels are upon, and by what falls in hearts by what is witnessed of them that they are thus - not by verification of that of them. And the proof of that is that it is not rejected that a she-camel be with people pregnant by what they see of her of what custom runs with their seeing it in their likes, then it becomes clear she is not pregnant. And likewise the daughters of Adam may be seen thus, then it becomes clear that what was seen of them was not pregnancy. And when that is thus, li'an is only by what its reality is stood upon, not in what conjecture is used in with which is no reality. And of what indicates that is that if a man says to his slave: If my slave-woman is pregnant, you are free - while the outward of her affair is that she is pregnant - then the slave's master dies before she places, and he comes demanding his inheritance, he is not judged for that in the saying of all of them, since what appeared of that slave-woman may not be pregnancy, and he is not, by the saying that was from his master, freed a freeing by which he deserves his father's inheritance. And when that is thus in inheritances, it is in negating pregnancies likewise. And what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged of the khalifat is of that meaning also: that he verify by their placing what he knows they were pregnant with on the day the one they were upon paid them to the one they became due to - he has taken in full what became due to him, and if it becomes clear they were then contrary to that, he returns them and demands pregnant ones. And in what we mentioned is what negated that this arguer have proof in what he argued with, of what we mentioned, for what we described - along with that he wronged his opponent in all he argued against him with, of what we mentioned, because his opponent claims that spending in the waiting of irrevocably divorced women is upon their divorcers for the waiting-periods they are in, not for pregnancies if in them; and that blood-money in quasi-intentional has no khalifat in it, rather it is with them a hundred camels: of them twenty-five hiqqah, twenty-five jadha'ah, twenty-five banat labun, and twenty-five banat makhad - except Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, for he used to say with khalifat. And in what we argued with in that is what repels that there be against him proof in what this opponent argued against him with, of what we mentioned. And in what we mentioned in these chapters is what indicated that the sound saying in negating pregnancy is what we mentioned in it from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, with what Abu Yusuf agreed with him upon. And we ask Allah for success."
"6058","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever greets his brother then meets him after that, with a tree or wall having come between them, let him greet him. 5154 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih said: And 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Bukht narrated to me, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever meets his brother, let him greet him; and if a tree or wall or stone comes between them, then meets him, let him greet him."
"6059","5155 - And Fahd and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, both saying: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding the renewing of greeting among people upon one another when some of them are absent from others by what is mentioned in this hadith. And that is the best of manners, and most connecting for what is between people. And likewise the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do after him."
"6060","As 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zubalah al-Madini narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to me, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit and Humayd, from Anas, who said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to walk together; when a tree or mound met them, they separated right or left, and when they met passing by it, some of them greeted others. He said: And in that was what indicated their holding to the manners of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his commands - may Allah's pleasure be upon them. And we ask Allah for success."
"6061","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what Anas reported of what they used to think of the Messenger of Allah in his lengthening standing after raising his head from bowing, and in his lengthening sitting between the two prostrations, that he had erred. 5156 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, who said: Anas used to describe to us the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he raised his head from bowing, stood until we said: He has forgotten."
"6062","5157 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"6063","5158 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, with the like of it, and added: He said: And he used to sit between the two prostrations until we said: He has erred. So we considered what is in this hadith of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lengthening standing after raising his head from bowing until they saw him as having erred. So we found him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - it was reported from him that he used to say after raising his head from bowing."
"6064","5159 - What al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to me, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when he raised his head from bowing he said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth, and filling what You will of anything after."
"6065","5160 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun informed us, from al-Majishun and 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from al-A'raj, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6066","5161 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi and 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, both saying: al-Majishun narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl and from his uncle al-Majishun, from al-A'raj, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"6067","5162 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us. 5163 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, both saying: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, he said: My uncle al-Majishun informed us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and did not mention 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl."
"6068","5164 - And what al-Hajjaj ibn 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: My father informed us, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"6069","5165 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from 'Ata, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6070","5166 - And what Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd Abu al-Hasan narrated to me, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Awfa narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So these things were what he used to say in that until they saw him as having erred, and Allah knows best. So one said: That would only be when custom before it had run upon contrary to it. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's - Mighty and Majestic - success and aid, was: that may be he used to say that once and leave it once. And may be he used to employ in that extending his voice with it, as he used to employ in what he said after his salutation from his witr: Glory be to the King, the Holy - lengthening his voice with the third of that, for he used to say it three times. And when that is thus, what was possible for him in it of time differed, so his Companions thought in that what they thought in him. And it was reported from him also that he used to add to that."
"6071","5167 - What Malik ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sayf al-Tujibi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Tanukhi narrated to us, from 'Atiyyah ibn Qays al-Kilabi, from Qaza'ah ibn Yahya, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of what is in the hadith of 'Ali, Ibn 'Abbas, and Ibn Abi Awfa, and added: O people of praise and glory, the truest what the servant says - and all of us are Your servants: There is no preventer of what You give, and the fortune of the fortunate does not benefit him against You. Abu Ja'far said: So he would say this once and leave it times, so they thought of him what his Companions thought of him in it. As for what we mentioned of what he did between the two prostrations, it may also be he did likewise due to what he said in it of what we mentioned from him of what has preceded from us in this book of ours, of his saying in that: My Lord, forgive me, my Lord, forgive me. So he would lengthen it in some of them, exceeding what his custom in it had run upon, until it was thought of him that he had erred. And it was reported from him in that also."
"6072","5168 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Sharik, from Abu 'Umar, from Abu Juhayfah, who said: Fortunes were mentioned before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So some people said: The fortune of so-and-so is in camels, and some said: in horses. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent. When he stood to pray and raised his head from bowing, he said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise, filling the heaven and filling the earth, and filling what You will. There is no preventer of what You give, no giver of what You prevent, and the fortune of the fortunate does not benefit him against You. And we ask Allah for success."
"6073","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying regarding the army of commanders: The commander is Zayd; if Zayd is killed, the commander is Ja'far; if Ja'far is killed, the commander is 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah - and deriving what is in it of jurisprudence. 5169 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn al-Fadl al-'Ataki narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Ya'qub, from al-Hasan ibn Sa'd, from 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army and appointed over them Zayd ibn Harithah, saying: If Zayd is afflicted before that or martyred, your commander is Ja'far; if he is killed or martyred, your commander is 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah. So Zayd took the banner and fought until he was killed, may Allah be pleased with him. Then Ja'far took the banner and fought until he was killed, may Allah be pleased with him. Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner and fought - and he did not mention that he was killed, and I see that dropped from Ibn Abi Dawud and from others than him of the reporters of this hadith - then Khalid ibn al-Walid took the banner, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gave victory upon him. So their news came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he went out to the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Your brothers met the enemy, and Zayd took the banner and fought until he was killed or martyred. Then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib took the banner after him and fought until he was killed or martyred. Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner and fought until he was killed or martyred. Then the banner was taken after him by a sword of the swords of Allah, Mighty and Majestic - Khalid ibn al-Walid - and Allah gave victory upon him. Then he gave respite to the family of Ja'far - he did not come to them, then came to them and said: Do not weep for my brother after today; call for me my brother's sons. So we were brought as if chicks, and he said: Call for me the barber. So the barber was brought and shaved our heads. Then he said: As for Muhammad, he resembles my uncle Abu Talib; and as for Awn, he resembles my creation and my character. Then he said: O Allah, succeed Ja'far in his family, and bless Abdullah in the deal of his right hand - three times. So our mother came and mentioned our orphanhood, and he said: Do you fear poverty for them? I am their guardian in this world and the Hereafter. And in that was the Prophet's permitting Khalid's taking command without his appointing him, and his approving him upon it - indicating the permissibility of one taking command in war without appointment when the appointed commander is lost, and Allah knows best."
"6074","5170 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Sumayr narrated to me, who said: 'Abdullah ibn Rabah narrated to me, who said: Abu Qatadah narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the army of commanders and said: Over you is Zayd ibn Harithah; if he is afflicted, then Ja'far; if Ja'far is afflicted, then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah. So Ja'far leapt and said: O Messenger of Allah, I would not have gone with your putting Zayd before me! He said: Proceed, for you do not know which of that is better. So they went and remained as Allah willed. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit and called: Prayer in congregation. So the people hastened to the Messenger of Allah, and he said: I inform you of this invading army of yours: they went until they met the enemy. So Zayd ibn Harithah took the banner and fought until he was killed a martyr - and he sought forgiveness for him. Then Ja'far took the banner and pressed upon the people and fought until he was killed a martyr, may Allah have mercy on him - so he bore witness for him of martyrdom and sought forgiveness for him. Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner and planted his feet until he was killed a martyr - and he sought forgiveness for him. Then Khalid ibn al-Walid took the banner, and he was not of the commanders - he appointed himself. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hands and said: He is a sword of Your swords, so You aid him. So from that day Khalid was named the Sword of Allah. Then the Messenger of Allah said: Go forth and reinforce your brothers, and let none of you stay behind. So they went forth in severe heat, on foot and mounted. While we were traveling a night upon the road, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dozed. And he stopped upon this of the hadith. So in this is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approved Khalid's appointing himself after the killing of the commanders he appointed, and made that a proof for the permissibility of such - and in this is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made some of the commanders a governor after the killing of others of those he mentioned of them, so whoever of them was thus was a governor by a risk appointed upon it. And in that is what indicated that it is allowed for the imam to say: When such-and-such is, I have appointed so-and-so to such - and when that is allowed in governorship, the like of it is allowed in agency, as Abu Hanifah and his companions say regarding a man saying: When such is, so-and-so is my agent in that - though they have in it opponents of the people of fiqh. And in this hadith also is what was from Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, without appointment, due to what he saw of need for that. So in this is what indicated that what occurs of the Muslims' affairs that busies their imam from appointing upon it, it is allowed for one to undertake standing with it - rather upon him is standing with it, and upon people is hearing and obedience in it. And 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, upon him be peace, exemplified that in the Eid prayer when 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was besieged from it and prevented, so he prayed with the people. And Allah knows best, and we ask Allah for success."
"6075","As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu 'Ubayd - freedman of Ibn Azhar - who said: I witnessed the Eid with 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, upon him be peace, while 'Uthman was besieged. So he came and prayed, then left and addressed. Abu Ja'far said: That was from 'Ali, upon him be peace, when he feared there would be no Eid prayer for the people that day. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan had said - and of his root is that Friday is not established except with a ruler - had said regarding the ruler whom a matter of what he fears missing of the Muslims' affairs busies from it, with none present from before him of those who can undertake it: that whoever of the people is able to undertake it, undertakes it - so his undertaking it is like his undertaking if he undertook it by the command of the ruler to whom undertaking it belongs. And the people besides him must follow him in that. And Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf differed with Muhammad in what he said of this, and the saying with us in that is as he said, not as they said. And there was reported in this chapter of what enters into what was from Khalid of what is mentioned in it."
"6076","5171 - What Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed and said: Zayd took the banner and was afflicted, then Ja'far took it and was afflicted, then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took it and was afflicted, then Khalid ibn al-Walid took it without command, and Allah gave victory upon him. He said: And his eyes were shedding tears. He said: And it would not please me that they be with us - or he said: It would not please them that they be with us - Ayyub doubted. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"6077","Chapter explaining the difficulty in interpreting the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {Then what is with you regarding the hypocrites, two parties} - the verse - by what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. 5172 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Umayyah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: The hypocrites were mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So one party said: We kill them, and a party: We do not kill them. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: {Then what is with you regarding the hypocrites, two parties} - the verse. So this hadith with us was of what Shabbabah did not precisely retain from Shu'bah, because what is in it is that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were regarding the hypocrites two parties: a party saying We kill them, and a party saying We do not kill them - and Allah sent down this verse in that. And the hypocrites in the residence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah were not exposed from before the Messenger to killing nor to other than it. And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to carry them upon their outward, and upon what they displayed to him of their affairs, even if he had stood from their inward upon contrary to that of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, informed him of them, and what He indicated to him by His saying in what He sent down upon him: {If the hypocrites and those in whose hearts is disease and the agitators in Madinah do not cease, We shall urge you against them, then they shall not neighbor you in it except a little} - and He, Mighty and Majestic, did not urge him against them, nor was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against them, after his knowledge of what Allah - Mighty and Majestic - had informed him of them, of the disbelief they were upon which they concealed, by His saying: {When the hypocrites come to you, they say: We bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah - and Allah knows that you are His Messenger - and Allah bears witness that the hypocrites are liars}. Then He followed that with His saying: {They are the enemy, so beware of them - may Allah fight them, how they are deluded}. And by what Allah - Mighty and Majestic - sent down upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding them, of His - Mighty and Majestic - saying: {And do not pray over any of them who dies, ever, nor stand over his grave; they disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger} - the verse. And of His informing of their fate to which they go in the Hereafter, by the saying of Allah - Mighty and Majestic: {The hypocrites are in the lowest depth of the Fire} - the verse. And in what we mentioned is proof of the distance of this hadith from the meaning with which Zayd ibn Thabit narrated it regarding them. Then we looked into the narration of other than Shabbabah of it from Shu'bah: how is it?"
"6078","5173 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd ibn Thabit: that a people went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Uhud, then returned. So they differed regarding them: one party said We kill them, and a party said We do not kill them. So there came down: {Then what is with you regarding the hypocrites, two parties, while Allah has overthrown them for what they earned}."
"6079","5174 - And we found Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa had narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Uhud, some people of his Companions returned. So the people were two parties: of them who said We kill them, and of them who said We do not do. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down regarding them: {While Allah has overthrown them for what they earned}. So in this hadith was what indicated to us the meaning due to which the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were regarding them two parties - a party saying We kill them, and a party: We do not kill them - and that was due to their leaving the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after going out with him to fight his enemies at Uhud, and their returning to other than it. So killing them became lawful thereby, and they became thereby at war with Allah and His Messenger and the believers. Then we sought to know the place to which they returned - which place is it?"
"6080","5175 - So we found Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Some people turned back from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud. So some people said: We kill them, and some said: We do not kill them. So Allah sent down: {Then what is with you regarding the hypocrites, two parties}. Zayd ibn Thabit said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It expels men as fire expels silver - meaning Madinah. So we understood thereby that their return was to other than it, not to it. And we found the Qur'an indicating that, by the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, right after this verse: {So do not take of them allies until they emigrate in the way of Allah} - and the emigrant was only to Madinah, not from Madinah to other than it. Then we looked: was there reported anything indicating the place to which they returned? So we did not find in that other than."
"6081","5176 - What Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih - and he did not go beyond him with it - and Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya had narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi said to us: All I narrated to you from Warqa is from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid - : {Then what is with you regarding the hypocrites, two parties}: a people who went out from Makkah until they came to Madinah claiming they are emigrants, then apostatized after that. So they sought permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to go to Makkah to take merchandise of theirs to trade in. So the believers differed regarding them: one saying They are hypocrites, and one saying They are believers. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, clarified their hypocrisy and commanded fighting them. So this is what we stood upon of the interpretation of this verse. And we ask Allah for success."
"6082","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in interpreting the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {Rivalry distracted you, until you visited the graves. Nay, you shall know; then nay, you shall know}. 5177 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Minhal ibn 'Amr, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from 'Ali, upon him be peace, who said: We used to doubt regarding the punishment of the grave until there came down: {Rivalry distracted you, until you visited the graves. Nay, you shall know; then nay, you shall know}. Abu Ja'far said: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Harawi saying: Ahmad ibn Hanbal said: al-Firyabi never narrated a hadith better than this hadith - meaning this hadith of Qays. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith was affirmation of the punishment of the grave, and there were reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reports of his seeking refuge from it, successive - of them."
"6083","5178 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, who said: I heard Mus'ab ibn Sa'd narrating from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He used to command us with this supplication, and narrate it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Allah, I seek refuge in You from miserliness, and seek refuge in You from cowardice, and seek refuge in You from being returned to the worst of age, and seek refuge in You from the trial of the world, and seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave."
"6084","5179 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, who said: Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, used to teach us these words, and mention them from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned what is in this hadith. And there was reported another hadith which some people mentioned, from 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and some of them shortened it from him - and due to that we did not place it at the beginning of this chapter."
"6085","5180 - And it is what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us. 5181 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, both saying: Isra'il informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from five: from cowardice, miserliness, evil of age, trial of the breast, and punishment of the grave."
"6086","5182 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from his father, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, who said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from five, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq and his son Isra'il reported this hadith from Abu Ishaq; as for Shu'bah, he reported it from Abu Ishaq differing with them in it."
"6087","5183 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Amr ibn Maymun - and he did not mention 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate with this supplication, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6088","5184 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, who said: Umm Khalid daughter of Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As narrated to me: that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."
"6089","5185 - And 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman al-Shahham narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah narrated to me: that he passed by his father while he was supplicating, saying: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from disbelief, poverty, and punishment of the grave. So I took them from him, and used to supplicate with them at the end of prayer. So he passed by me while I was supplicating with them, and said to me: O my son, whence did you get these words? I said: O my father, I heard you supplicating with them at the end of prayer, so I took them from you. He said: Keep to them, O my son, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate with them at the end of prayer."
"6090","5186 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik said: Abu al-Zinad informed me, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate, saying: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of Hell, and seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, and seek refuge in You from the evil of the False Messiah, the Dajjal, and seek refuge in You from the trial of life and death."
"6091","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: Budayl narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave, the punishment of Hell, and the trial of the Dajjal."
"6092","And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Abd Allah ibn Khabbab, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said about the Dajjal: His eye is green like glass, so seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from the punishment of the grave."
"6093","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain, and we ask Allah for success."
"6094","Chapter explaining the problematic reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what people are punished with in their graves. Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb informed us, he said: Hannad ibn al-Sari informed us, from Waki', from al-A'mash, he said: I heard Mujahid narrating from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by two graves and said: They are both being punished, and they are not being punished for something major. As for this one, he did not keep himself clean of his urine, and as for this one, he used to go about with talebearing. Then he called for a moist palm stalk, split it in two, planted one on this grave and one on this grave, then said: Perhaps it will be lightened for them both so long as they do not dry out. Then an objector said: Why did he single out urine here to the exclusion of other impurities? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: urine does not show on bodies or clothes the way other impurities like excrement, blood, pus and the like show, for people avoid those things out of disgust for them, whereas urine is not like that, since it has no color to be avoided on its account. So it is possible he singled it out because people take it lightly and are careless about cleaning themselves of it, unlike their care about cleaning themselves of other things whose bearers people find dirty so that they avoid their gatherings and their closeness. So he singled out urine for that reason to the exclusion of the rest. As for his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): As for one of them, he did not shield himself from his urine, its explanation in our view, and Allah knows best, is: shielding is guarding, as in people's supplication for one another: May Allah shield you from the Fire, i.e., may Allah guard you from the Fire, and as in the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Guard yourselves from the Fire even with half a date, i.e., shield yourselves from the Fire even with half a date. Likewise: he did not shield himself from his urine, i.e., he did not guard himself from his urine."
"6095","And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, he said: Bahr ibn Marrar narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakrah narrated, he said: I was walking with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I and another man, when he passed by two graves, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The occupants of these two graves are being punished, and who will bring me a palm frond from these date palms? I raced with the man and outran him, broke off a frond and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He split it in two halves from the top and placed half on each of the two graves, and said: It will be eased for them both so long as anything of their moisture remains in them. They are being punished for backbiting and urine. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, knows best what His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by that, and Him we ask for success."
"6096","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his saying: Most of the punishment of the grave is from urine. Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, he said: I think, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Most of the punishment of the grave is from urine."
"6097","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Harun narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, without any doubt in any part of its chain. Then an advocate argued that what is meant is: people are punished in their graves with urine as they are punished with it in this world, since that is among the severest punishments of this world. And it may also mean the meaning we mentioned in the preceding discussion of these meanings: that the punishment which is most of the punishment of the grave is on account of urine, consisting of whatever kinds of His punishment Allah wills to punish with, and Allah knows best."
"6098","And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Yahya, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, raising the hadith, he said: Most of the punishment of the grave is from urine, so keep yourselves clean of urine. This hadith states that that punishment in the grave is on account of urine. The correct understanding of this hadith and the first hadith is that that punishment is on account of urine, consisting of whatever Allah wills that punishment to be with, not with urine itself, and Allah knows best what His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by that, and Him we ask for success."
"6099","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his denial that people are punished in their graves when he was asked about that after the Jewish woman said to A'ishah: May Allah protect you from the punishment of the grave. Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him. And al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, then both agreed together, saying: from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, from A'ishah: that a Jewish woman came asking her, and said: May Allah protect you from the punishment of the grave. So A'ishah asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Are people punished in their graves? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, seeking refuge with Allah from that. Then the Messenger of Allah rode a mount one morning, the sun eclipsed, he returned in the forenoon, passed among the dwellings, stood praying. She mentioned the eclipse prayer and how he prayed it. She said: Then he finished, said whatever Allah willed him to say, then commanded them to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave. This hadith first shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) denying that people are punished in their graves, and then his commanding people afterwards to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave. The Messenger's initial denial, in our view, and Allah knows best, was before it was revealed to him that people are punished in their graves; then it was revealed to him afterwards that they are punished in their graves, so he commanded them to seek refuge with Allah from that. Then an objector said: How can you accept this, when you have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned."
"6100","What Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, he said: Ibn Shihab said: And Ibn Abi Namlah narrated to me: that Abu Namlah al-Ansari informed him: that while he was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a Jewish man came to him and said: O Muhammad, does this bier speak? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah knows best. The Jew said: I bear witness that it speaks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever the People of the Book tell you, neither believe them nor disbelieve them, but say: We believe in Allah, His Books and His Messengers. If it is true, you will not have disbelieved them, and if it is false, you will not have believed them."
"6101","And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ibn Abi Namlah informed me: that Abu Namlah al-Ansari informed him, then he mentioned the same. He said: This hadith shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding not to disbelieve nor believe the People of the Book in what they narrate, since they had read from Allah's Books what those narrated-to among his community had not read. Yet in the first hadith he rejected what A'ishah narrated to him from the Jewish woman, and this is a severe contradiction. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: it is possible that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in the first hadith was before he was commanded to pay heed to what the People of the Book narrate to him; then afterwards he commanded pausing at it, leaving both belief in it and disbelief in it. What they narrate to him may be rejected by him, just as a man may reject what he does not know even if it is in reality true. Do you not see that if a man claims wealth against another man, and the one claimed against knows nothing of it, he is free to deny it and to swear to him about it, even though it may in fact be owed by him, whether his knowledge of it left him, or it came about by his rolling in his sleep onto wealth of his and destroying it for him, so its value became due from him, which is the wealth claimed against him. Likewise, when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about what he did not know, he was free to deny it even though it might in reality be other than as it stood with him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that the like, when said to him, be met with the like of what is in the second hadith, even though what is in the first hadith was permissible for him. Besides, we examined the hadith of Amrah which we began this chapter with, from A'ishah, and found more than one of the narrators from A'ishah differing from her version, among them Masruq ibn al-Ajda'."
"6102","As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, that she said: An old Jewish woman came to me and said: The people of the graves are punished. So I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: She spoke the truth; the people of the graves are punished with a punishment that the beasts hear."
"6103","And as al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, from A'ishah, she said: Two old women from the old Jewish women of Medina came to me and said to me: The people of the graves are punished in their graves. I disbelieved them both and did not believe them. They left, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, two old women came to me and claimed that the people of the graves are punished. He said: They spoke the truth; they are punished with a punishment that all the beasts hear. A'ishah said: After that I never saw him in a prayer without seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave. And among them is Dhakwan."
"6104","Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas and Asad narrated to us, they said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Dhakwan, from A'ishah, she said: A Jewish woman asked for food, saying: Feed me; may Allah protect you from the trial of the Dajjal and from the trial of the punishment of the grave. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is this Jewish woman saying? He said: And what did she say? I said: She said: May Allah protect you from the trial of the Dajjal and the trial of the punishment of the grave. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, raised his hands, and began seeking refuge with Allah from the trial of the Dajjal and the punishment of the grave. And Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from A'ishah about that."
"6105","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah: that a Jewish woman came to her while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with her, and said: Do you know that you will be tried in the graves? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was startled and said: Only the Jews are tried. A'ishah said: We stayed some nights, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you not know that it has been revealed to me that you will be tried in the graves? She said: Then I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith shows that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at first rejected that, before it was revealed to him that they are tried in their graves; then it was revealed to him that they are tried in their graves, so he returned to affirming it and seeking refuge from it. This also shows Urwah agreeing with Amrah on what she narrated of that from A'ishah. In our view, and Allah knows best, this is more deserving than what Masruq and Dhakwan narrated from A'ishah, because here the Messenger's initial rejection then later affirmation is present. What Masruq and Dhakwan had was the second matter, while Urwah and Amrah had both the first matter and the second, so they are more deserving, and by preserving that they preserved what Masruq and Dhakwan fell short of preserving, and we ask Allah for success."
"6106","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the punishment of the grave, whether anyone hears it or not. Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri informed us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered a garden of Banu al-Najjar riding his mule, and the mule shied at five or six graves, and swerved with him. He said: Which of you knows the occupants of these graves? A man said: I do, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Who are they? He said: They died in polytheism. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This nation is tried in their graves; were it not that you would stop burying one another, I would have asked Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to let you hear the punishment of the grave that is in it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from the punishment of the grave, so we said: We seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave. Then he said: Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the Fire, or: seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from the Fire, al-Jurayri doubted, so we said: We seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from the punishment of the Fire. He said: Seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from trials, the apparent and the hidden. We said: We seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from trials, the apparent and the hidden. Then he said: Seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from the trial of the Dajjal."
"6107","Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit al-Bunani and Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a grey mule and passed by a garden of Banu al-Najjar, and there was a grave whose occupant was being punished, so it shied. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that you would stop burying one another, I would have asked Allah to let you hear the punishment of the grave. Then an objector said: In this hadith of Anas is proof that the children of Adam do not hear the punishment of the grave, and that others besides them among the beasts mentioned in these two hadiths do hear it. Yet you have narrated what contradicts that, and he mentioned."
"6108","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father, from al-Bara ibn Azib, from Abu Ayyub: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out when the sun had set and said: These are the voices of Jews being punished in their graves. He said: This hadith shows that the children of Adam could hear the voices of the Jews being punished in their graves, contrary to what you narrated before it in this chapter. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: it is possible those voices they heard came after a supplication by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to let them hear them, after he said to them what he said in the other hadith. And it is possible that what they were made to hear was the voices of the Jews, while they did not hear the voices of punished Muslims in their graves as in the first hadith, and we seek refuge with Allah from that, and we ask Allah for success."
"6109","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the one who forbade those he forbade among the Children of Israel from cutting with scissors part of his body soiled by urine, and was punished for that in his grave. Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mihran, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasanah, he said: I set out, I and Amr ibn al-As, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with a shield or something like a shield, sat down, screened himself and urinated sitting. My companion and I said: Look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinating as a woman urinates, sitting. He came to us and said: Do you not know what befell the man of the Children of Israel? When any of them got urine on himself he would cut it off with scissors, so he forbade them from that, and was punished in his grave."
"6110","Isma'il ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash informed us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasanah, then he mentioned the same. Then an objector said: What is the basis for this man being punished, who forbade those among the Children of Israel he forbade from cutting their skin with scissors, such that he was punished for it in his grave, when cutting the skins of the children of Adam with scissors is disobedience? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: it is possible that the law of the Children of Israel regarding urine soiling their bodies was to cut it off with scissors, so that man forbade them from that, and by forbidding them he commanded them to abandon their law, which was among the gravest disobediences, so he was punished for it in his grave, and we ask Allah for success."
"6111","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the best of people: that he is the one whose life is long and whose deeds are good. Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which people are best, or he said: good? He said: The one whose life is long and whose deeds are good. It was said: Then which people are worst? He said: The one whose life is long and whose deeds are evil."
"6112","Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father."
"6113","And Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah."
"6114","And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in all these reports the same. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith and found that one with the described quality is not thereby better than the prophets, nor better than the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whom Allah favored over others besides them by His saying: Those of you who spent before the conquest and fought are not equal; they are greater in rank than those who spent afterwards and fought. We thus understood that what this hadith generalizes about people in its apparent wording was not intended in its apparent sense; rather what is meant is: among the best of people? So the general wording intends only part of it, and the Arabs do this often. The Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has a like example: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said about the companion of Sulayman: And she was given of everything, though she was not given anything of what Sulayman (peace be upon him) was given; and His saying, Mighty and Majestic, about the wind: Destroying everything by its Lord's command, though that was only of some things, not all things. Likewise the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith is about some of those mentioned, not all of them: his saying the best of people, or the most excellent of people, means: among the best of people, or among the most excellent of people. Also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter."
"6115","What Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak, from Abd Allah ibn Umayrah, from Durrah, she said: I was with A'ishah when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in and said: Bring me water for ablution. I hastened to the jug first, and he performed ablution. Then he raised the corner of his eye, or his eye, to me and said: You are from me and I am from you. Then a man came and said: It was not I who did it, but I was told. She said: He had asked him on the pulpit: Who are the best of people? He said: The most understanding of them in Allah's religion, Mighty and Majestic. Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of this in our view, and Allah knows best, is like the meaning of the first hadith we mentioned. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that Durrah daughter of Abu Lahab held with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the mentioned lofty station, the most exalted station. And there is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he did in her affair when those Ansari women harmed her on account of her father when she arrived in Medina as an emigrant."
"6116","What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Nafi', the freedman of Ibn Umar, and Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, he said: When Durrah daughter of Abu Lahab arrived in Medina as an emigrant, she stayed in the house of Rafi' ibn al-Mu'alla al-Zuraqi. Some women of Banu Zurayq who sat with her said to her: You are the daughter of Abu Lahab about whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: Perish the hands of Abu Lahab and perish, his wealth and what he earned availed him not. So what does your emigration avail you! She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), knelt before him and mentioned what they had said to her. He calmed her and said: Sit. Then he led the people in the noon prayer, then sat on the pulpit for a while, then said: O people, why am I harmed regarding my family? By Allah, my intercession will reach through my kinship, even that Hakam and Ha and Suda' and Salhab will attain it on the Day of Resurrection through my kinship. And Salhab in the genealogy of Yemen is from Daws. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) referring Durrah daughter of Abu Lahab's affair to herself, not to her father, because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has barred that any bearer bear another's burden, and because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the father of Abu Rimthah about his son Abu Rimthah: He does not wrong on your behalf nor do you wrong on his, so what came from Abu Lahab does not extend to a child or anyone else, and what his daughter Durrah earned and did of good does not extend to anyone besides her, father or otherwise, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6117","Chapter explaining the problematic report from Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abd Allah ibn Abbas, some raising it from Ali to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what is meant by Allah's saying, Mighty and Majestic: And you make your thanks in place of what we recite: Your provision, that you deny. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us. And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, then both agreed, saying: from Abd al-A'la, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, peace be upon him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And you make your provision that you deny, he said: Your thanks; you say: We were given rain by such-and-such star and such-and-such star."
"6118","And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-A'la, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, peace be upon him, the same, but he did not raise it."
"6119","And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, then both agreed, each saying: from Abu Bishr Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: that he used to recite: And you make your thanks that you deny. He said: It was revealed about the stars. When they were given rain at night, in the morning they would say: We were given rain by such-and-such star. Their saying that was disbelief, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: And you make your thanks for the provision and rain I sent down upon you that you deny; you say: We were given rain by such-and-such star. And it is also narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas that he recited in place of: And you make your thanks: And you make your provision."
"6120","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And you make your provision that you deny, he said: It is seeking rain by the stars. We examined these reports and found in some of them: And you make your thanks in place of what is recited: And you make your provision, which was open in meaning, like the Arabs' saying: I visited you to honor me, but you made my visit that you disdained me, meaning: you made the reward of my visit disdain of me. Likewise: you made thanks for My favor to you denial. So said al-Farra. And we found in some the recitation: And you make your provision that you deny, whose meaning returns to the other meaning, for some Arabs call thanks provision, among them Azd Shanu'ah, as Qutrub and al-Farra both mentioned. So its meaning returns to the meaning we mentioned for: And you make your thanks. And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what they used to say at rain, by which they were tested, and thanks was due from them at it."
"6121","What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Attab ibn Hunayn, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If Allah withheld rain from people for nine years, then sent it, a people would wake up disbelieving, saying: We were given rain by the star of al-Mijdah."
"6122","And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah gives a people blessing in the morning or evening, then a people wake up in the morning disbelieving in it, saying: By such-and-such star. Muhammad said: I mentioned this hadith to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: We too heard this from Abu Hurayrah. Abu Ja'far said: This saying of theirs by which they wake up disbelieving means disbelieving in Allah's favor upon them, not disbelief in anything else. This is like what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we mentioned earlier in this book, of his saying: I looked into the Fire and saw most of its people were women. It was said: Why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: For their ingratitude. It was said: Do they disbelieve in Allah? He said: No, they are ungrateful for kindness and ungrateful to the companion; if you do good to one of them for a lifetime, then she sees something from you, she says: I never saw any good from you. And we ask Allah for success."
"6123","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the laughter of rain and its speech. Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Riyahi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father, he said: I was sitting with my uncle Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman in the mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when an eminent old man with some weakness in his sight, of Banu Ghifar, appeared in a corner of the mosque. Humayd sent for him and called him. When he came, he said: Nephew, this man accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on some of his journeys; make room for him between me and you. So I made room for him. Humayd said to him: The hadith you mentioned that you heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say about the cloud? He said: Yes, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah creates the cloud, and it speaks the finest speech and laughs the finest laughter. We examined this hadith and found what is in it present in Arab speech. Among it is what al-Farra mentioned: the Arabs say: a laughing day, clear; a speaking cloud, pouring; by its speech they mean its returning and raining, by stars they recognize. Al-Farra said: I heard Abu Tharwan say: We wintered in an easy land to cross, abundant in joy, its cloud speaking, its gardens laughing. He described these things with human actions to affirm knowledge of what was intended: describing the cloud with speech meaning abundance of its water, and the gardens with laughter for the emergence of flowers and great pasture. He said: And in their proverbs: grey hair spoke on his head, and grey hair laughed likewise when it appears; likewise: the wall leaned, the garment burned, all this is intelligible in meaning. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whose people were the peak and hump of Arabic, addressed those he addressed, Arabs, with what they understand from him and grasp of his intent, for Allah sent him to them in their tongue to explain to them, as He said, Mighty and Majestic: We sent no messenger except in the tongue of his people to explain to them. So he addressed them in their tongue knowing their understanding of what he addressed them with, and we ask Allah for success."
"6124","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to Sa'd when he visited him in the illness in which he visited him, when Sa'd said to him: Am I dying of this illness of mine in the land I emigrated from? He said to him: I hope Allah will raise you so that a people benefit through you and others are harmed through you. Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I fell ill in the year of the Conquest with an illness from which I expected death. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have much wealth; shall I give all my wealth in charity? He said: No. I said: Then half? He said: No. I said: Then a third? He said: A third, and a third is much. That you leave your heirs rich is better than leaving them dependent begging people. You will not spend any spending but you will be rewarded for it, even the morsel you raise to your wife's mouth. I said: O Messenger of Allah, will I be left behind from my emigration? He said: You will not be left behind after me and do a deed seeking Allah's face but you will increase in rank and degree through it. Perhaps you will be left behind after me so that peoples benefit through you and others are harmed through you. O Allah, complete for my Companions their emigration and do not turn them back on their heels. But the wretched Sa'd ibn Khawlah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lamented him that he died in Mecca."
"6125","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit me in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage for a severe pain, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, pain has reached me as you see, and I am a man of wealth, and none inherits from me except a daughter of mine. Then he mentioned the hadith, except that he said: A third, and a third is much or large. Abu Ja'far said: Sufyan and Malik differed in this hadith about the journey in which Sa'd fell ill: Sufyan said it was the year of the Conquest, and Malik said it was the Farewell Pilgrimage. We wanted to see which of the two journeys it truly was."
"6126","We found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from Amr al-Qari, from his father, from his grandfather Amr al-Qari: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived in Mecca and left Sa'd behind ill when he went out to Hunayn. When he returned from al-Ji'ranah in umrah he entered upon him while he was sick and overcome, and Sa'd said: O Messenger of Allah, I have wealth, and I am inherited as kalalah; shall I bequeath all my wealth, or give it in charity? He said: No. He said: Shall I bequeath two thirds? He said: No. He said: Shall I bequeath a third? He said: Yes, and that is large. He said: O Messenger of Allah, will I die in the land I left as an emigrant? He said: I hope Allah will raise you so that peoples are harmed through you and others benefit through you. O Amr ibn al-Qari: if Sa'd dies after me, bury him here, meaning toward the road to Medina, and he gestured with his hand thus. This hadith decides for Ibn Uyaynah against Malik in their disagreement about the journey in which Sa'd's illness occurred in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him what he said in this hadith: that it was the year of the Conquest, not the Farewell Pilgrimage. Then we sought the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Perhaps you will be left behind so that peoples benefit through you and others are harmed through you, what it is."
"6127","We found Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Harmalah ibn Imran al-Tujibi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, he said: I asked Amir ibn Sa'd about the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him: Perhaps you will remain so that peoples benefit through you and others are harmed through you. Amir said: Sa'd was appointed over Iraq, and killed peoples for apostasy, so harmed them, and sought repentance from a people who used to chant the chant of Musaylimah the liar, and they repented and benefited through him. The like of this could not have been said by Amir as opinion or inference, for the like is not said by opinion or inference; rather he said it as transmitted knowledge, for the like is only said by transmission. Perhaps he heard it from his father, or from another from whom the like may rightly be taken. It is not permissible that the one he took it from took it except from the avenue from which the like is taken: either hearing from the Messenger of Allah, or hearing from one who heard it from him. Thus the meaning of what we mentioned in the first hadith, whose meaning was unclear, became clear, and we ask Allah for success."
"6128","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is meant by kalalah, who it is. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: I wish the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not died until he clarified for people chapters of usury, kalalah and the grandfather. And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Idris, he said: I heard Abu Hayyan, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Umar on the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Three things, O people, I wish the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had covenanted to us about them a covenant to hold to: the grandfather, kalalah, and chapters of usury."
"6129","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Murrah ibn Sharahil, from Umar, he said: Three things, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had clarified them for us before he died, are dearer to me than what is on earth: the caliphate, usury and kalalah. I said: Kalalah, there is no doubt, is what is besides child and father. He said: About the father they doubt."
"6130","And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain."
"6131","And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Murrah narrated to us, from Murrah, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Three things, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had clarified them for us, are dearer to me than the world and what is in it: the caliphate, kalalah and usury. In Shu'bah's hadith from Amr: that kalalah is what is besides the child, and they used to doubt about the father, whether he is like the child in that or not."
"6132","And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd al-Ghatafani, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah al-Ya'mari, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab stood as preacher, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: By Allah, I leave nothing more important to me than the matter of kalalah. I asked the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, and he was never sterner with me about anything than he was about it, until he poked with his finger in my chest or my side, and said: O Umar, does the verse revealed at the end of Surat al-Nisa not suffice you? And if I live I will decide in it with a decision about which no one who reads the Quran or does not read the Quran will differ."
"6133","And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Hasan, from Masruq, from his father, he said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab about a relative of mine who inherited as kalalah. He said: Kalalah, kalalah, kalalah, three times. Then he took his beard and said: By Allah, that I know it is dearer to me than anything on earth. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, and he said: Have you not heard the verse revealed in the summer? Twice."
"6134","And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about kalalah, and he said: The summer verse suffices you. All these reports show the one asked refraining from answering what it is, out of scrupulousness about speaking on Allah's Book, Mighty and Majestic, with what its truth was not established from Allah, until Umar died upon that."
"6135","As Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Tawus, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: I was the last of people to see Umar, and I heard him say: The saying is what I said. I said: And what did you say? He said: Kalalah is one who has no child. What Umar held in that, meaning the child, was that kalalah is absence of child, pausing about the father whether he is kalalah or not. And contrary to this hadith is narrated from him, may Allah be pleased with him, in that."
"6136","As Isa ibn Ibrahim also narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar had written a document about kalalah, and when death approached him he called for the document, erased it, and said: You may hold your own view in it."
"6137","And as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abd Allah al-Awdi, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to us in Basra, he said: Umar said when he was stabbed: As for me, I did not decide any decision about kalalah. Then we looked at what else is narrated about kalalah."
"6138","We found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hibban ibn Ali narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah ordered Sa'sa'ah ibn Sawhan to address the people. He spoke, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent Muhammad when traces had faded and landmarks had crumbled, and he conveyed what he was sent with. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, and Abu Bakr was made caliph; he established the codex, gave inheritance to kalalah, and was strong in Allah's affair, Mighty and Majestic. Then Abu Bakr died, and Umar was made caliph; he founded the garrison cities, fixed the stipends, and was strong in Allah's affair, Mighty and Majestic. Then Umar died, and the people agreed upon Uthman; his caliphate was decree and his killing was decree. Al-Mughirah said: See what he says when he reached Uthman. He said: You ordered me to address, so I addressed, then you ordered me to sit, so I sat. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith shows that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had given inheritance to kalalah, but we find no mention in it of what kalalah was in his view, so we looked into that."
"6139","We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Sha'bi: that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, and Umar said: Kalalah is one who has neither child nor parent. This hadith, despite its break, shows that Abu Bakr and Umar said: Kalalah is one who has neither child nor parent. Then we looked at what is narrated in that from other than these avenues we mentioned."
"6140","We found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Amr ibn Sa'id, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Three men of Banu Sa'd narrated to me: that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, fell ill in Mecca, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have much wealth, and I have no heir except kalalah; shall I bequeath all my wealth? He said: No. He said: Shall I bequeath half of it? He said: No. He said: Shall I bequeath a third of it? He said: A third, and a third is much."
"6141","And we found Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed us, from Amr ibn Sa'id, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari narrated to me, he said: Three men of Sa'd's children narrated to me, one of them, meaning Amir ibn Sa'd: that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas fell ill in Mecca, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit him. Sa'd said to him: O Messenger of Allah, I leave wealth, and I have no heir except kalalah; shall I bequeath all my wealth? He said: No. He said: Then half of it? He said: No. He said: Then a third of it? He said: A third, and a third is much. That you leave your family in comfort, or he said: in good, is better for you than leaving them begging people. This hadith contains Sa'd's saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have no heir except kalalah, though he had a daughter whom al-Zuhri mentioned from Amir ibn Sa'd in what we narrated in the chapter before this chapter. We thus understood, by authenticating the hadiths, that his saying: I have no heir except kalalah, means: I have no heir besides my daughter except kalalah, for the daughter is not kalalah according to all people of knowledge. Then we looked whether anything else is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about kalalah or not."
"6142","We found Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan say: Ibn al-Munkadir heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: I fell ill and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit me, finding me unconscious, with Abu Bakr walking with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution and poured his ablution water over me, and I came to. I said: O Messenger of Allah, how shall I dispose of my wealth, what shall I do with my wealth? He did not answer me until the verse of inheritance was revealed. He said: He had seven sisters, and had neither parent nor child. They said: Which verse is this? He said: They ask you for a ruling; say: Allah gives you a ruling about kalalah, to the end of the verse. And Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir said: Jabir said: This verse was revealed about me."
"6143","And we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit me while I was ill and senseless. He performed ablution and poured the ablution water over me, and I came to. I said: How is inheritance, for only kalalah will inherit from me? So the verse of shares was revealed. This hadith shows Jabir saying to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Only kalalah will inherit from me, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not reject, proving that kalalah is the heir, not the deceased."
"6144","And we found Yazid narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: I fell ill and had seven sisters. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me, blew water in my face, and I came to. I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I bequeath two thirds to my sisters? He said: Do good. I said: Half? He said: Do good. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left me, then returned and said: O Jabir, Allah has sent down and clarified what is for your sisters, making two thirds for them. Jabir used to say: These verses were revealed about me: They ask you for a ruling; say: Allah gives you a ruling about kalalah. This hadith shows the sisters whom Jabir mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as kalalah, which the Messenger did not reject. The child, which may bar the sisters if male and does not bar them if female, is not kalalah; so the parent, who never bars them in any case, is even more rightly not kalalah. What we mentioned proves kalalah are the heirs, not the deceased, supporting the reading of one who reads: If a man is inherited as kalalah, and Allah knows best. And Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Masadiri narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Kalalah is everyone who inherits other than father, son or brother; with the Arabs it is kalalah: He is inherited as kalalah, a verbal noun from takallalahu al-nasab. Kalalah is what encircles one's lineage of paternal uncles, cousins and agnates. He said: And some said: brothers are of kalalah. Abu Ja'far said: Our view is what we narrated in the two hadiths of Jabir and Sa'd: that kalalah are the heirs, not the deceased. And it is narrated that the kalalah verse is the last verse revealed."
"6145","As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Bara say: The last verse revealed was: They ask you for a ruling; say: Allah gives you a ruling about kalalah, and the last surah revealed was Bara'ah. And also narrated from Ibn Abbas about kalalah."
"6146","As Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad informed us, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about kalalah. He said: It is one who has neither child nor parent. I said: But Allah says: If a man perishes having no child. So he became angry with me and rebuked me. It is possible that the mention of the child in this verse, and leaving mention of the parent, is because those addressed know that the parent in this meaning is weightier than the child, so mention of the child suffices for mention of the parent, as He said, Glorious and Mighty: And your mothers who nursed you and your sisters through nursing, remaining silent about others besides them whom nursing makes unlawful, of paternal aunts, maternal aunts and the like, since those addressed know by what He addressed them His intent, Mighty and Majestic, in what He left unsaid. So is Arab speech: they address with a thing until, when they know the addressed understand what is wanted of them, they withhold the rest, since they have learned from him. The Quran has this: Allah said: If only a Quran by which the mountains were moved, or the earth cut, or the dead spoken to, then said: Nay, to Allah belongs the affair entirely. He did not inform of other than that about which language scholars differed over His intent, Mighty and Majestic: some said it is: this Quran would have been, and some said it is: they would have disbelieved in it, and Allah knows best His intent. And He said, Mighty and Majestic: Were it not for Allah's bounty upon you and His mercy, without mentioning what would have been, joining it to His saying: And that Allah is Relenting, Wise. This is abundant in Arab speech. And it was intelligible that kalalah is what encircles the deceased and the inheritance he left, of those entitled to it by the tie encircling him, and the child is not encircling him since he is of him; likewise the parent is not encircling him since he is of him. Thus established: kalalah is what is besides parent and child together, and we ask Allah for success."
"6147","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about praying seated cross-legged, whether it is disliked or not. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from a freedman of al-Sa'ib, from al-Sa'ib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of one seated is half the prayer of one standing, not cross-legged. This hadith indicates deficiency of the prayer of one seated cross-legged compared to the prayer of another seated not cross-legged. In our view this hadith is from one whose like is not adduced as proof, because the freedman of al-Sa'ib mentioned in its chain is unknown, and because Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir is not strong in his narration. Then an objector said: It is narrated from Ibn Mas'ud disliking sitting cross-legged in prayer."
"6148","He mentioned what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali informed us, from Husayn, from al-Haytham ibn Shihab, he said: Abd Allah said: That I sit on two embers is dearer to me than sitting cross-legged in prayer. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: he has no proof in this, for it may refer to the cross-legged sitting not permitted to the praying person in his prayer, namely sitting cross-legged for the tashahhud. Then we looked whether anything else is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about praying cross-legged besides the hadith we began this chapter with."
"6149","We found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Abd Allah narrated to us. And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Abd Allah informed me, then both agreed, saying: He said: Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated to us, from Hafs, Ishaq said: meaning Ibn Ghiyath, from Humayd, Ishaq said: meaning al-Tawil, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, from A'ishah, she said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray cross-legged. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith is sound in chain, with no aspersion on any of its narrators, so it is more deserving than the hadith of the freedman of al-Sa'ib who is unknown."
"6150","And we found from Umm Salamah and Umm al-Darda about that, of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi narrated to us, from Asim and Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, from his mother: that she saw Umm Salamah pray cross-legged due to inflammation in her eyes."
"6151","And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn al-Walid al-Qa'qa'i narrated to us, he said: Hani ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah narrated to me, he said: I saw Umm al-Darda pray cross-legged. This position in this chapter was more deserving by analogy, for we have seen gesturing in prayer differentiated between gesturing for bowing and gesturing for prostration, making one lower than the other, since each is a substitute for something other than what the other substitutes for. Likewise sitting as substitute for standing in prayer should differ from the sitting that is part of prayer unlike that, namely sitting for the tashahhud. By what we mentioned is confirmed what Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad used to say: commanding one unable to stand in prayer, whose inability permits him to pray seated, to pray cross-legged as substitute for the standing he would do if able, contrary to what Zufar says: that his sitting in it as substitute for his standing he is unable to do is like his sitting in it for his tashahhud, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6152","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the ruling for one invited to a wedding feast he is commanded to answer, when he knows there is amusement there whose attending is not proper elsewhere: is the duty to answer upon him as if it were not there, or has it lapsed from him? Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Gabriel stayed away from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then came to him and said: What kept you? He said: A puppy in your house. They looked and found a puppy under the bed, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered it removed. We mentioned in another chapter of these reports, earlier in this book, other than this hadith, of Gabriel's prior promise to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come at an hour, his delaying past it, then the mentioned speech in this hadith. Gabriel's promise to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was an absolute promise with no exception in it, yet the Law's bar against entering a house containing what was in the Prophet's house (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted fulfilling it. Likewise in jurisprudence is what scholars differ about: a man invited to a wedding feast he is commanded to attend and sit for, comes to it and finds amusement there which, found elsewhere, and some said his sitting in it does not harm him since it is sitting for what he is commanded, and even if he knew when invited that it is there, he should not refrain from attending, since it is what he is commanded with a command in which no exception occurred. Among those who said that: Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf."
"6153","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Ya'qub, from Abu Hanifah to that effect. He did not report disagreement between Muhammad and the two. And contrary to that is narrated from Muhammad: that the one invited to that may neither answer it nor sit at it. This second saying was the worthier of the two in our view, for what is commanded therein is only to follow the Sunnah, and the Sunnah forbids the like of this, so the prohibition in it is excepted from the command ordered therein, even if not excepted by tongue. Others argued against us with an added proof in this chapter, namely."
"6154","What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi and Amr ibn Uthman al-Raqqi narrated to us similarly, he said: Abu al-Malih narrated to us, so said Abd Allah in his hadith, and in Amr's hadith: from Abu al-Malih, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Nafi', he said: I was with Ibn Umar when he heard the sound of a shepherd's flute. He did thus, placing his two fingers in his ears, and turned off the road. Then he said: Do you hear anything? I said: I hear nothing. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do this. This advocate said: This hadith enters this meaning, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refrained from letting any of that disliked sound enter his ears, though on a road they may pass and travel. Likewise sitting for what the man we mentioned was invited to, a permissible sitting upon which a disliked matter supervened, he may not maintain the permissible sitting upon hearing what is forbidden to hear elsewhere. In our view this is not part of this chapter nor of its kind, for what is in this chapter is passing on a road whose passing is not obligatory; one passes on it by choice, not otherwise. So what he does by choice must not be mixed with what is forbidden. In the other meaning his attending was for duties upon him, so what supervened may have lifted his duty, or may not be permitted to lift his duty. What proved the lifting of his duty is what is in the first hadith we mentioned, not what is in this hadith, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6155","Chapter showing narrations indicating the possibility of what some historians said: that among those who pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the day people pledged in Mecca was a small son of Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, or of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq. Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Yazid ibn Babanus, he said: I came to A'ishah and asked her about things, and I heard her say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the illness in which Allah took his soul, a son of Abd Allah, or of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, passed by him with a green tooth-stick. He glanced at it, so I called him, took it from him and handed it to him, and he placed it on his mouth. His head was between my bosom and chest. While we were thus he raised his head; I thought it was something he wanted from his family. There was a cold wind, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, took his soul without my realizing. We learned by this hadith that Abd Allah or Abd al-Rahman then had a son, and it is impossible he was then in the state of one who walks unless his age preceded the conquest of Mecca. The people in Mecca had brought their minor sons who had not reached puberty to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so they pledged him along with their fathers, just as before that those minors pledged him: Ali and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both. The son of Abd Allah or Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr was likewise in that meaning, and Allah knows best. The people that day used to bring their sons to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would wipe their heads and supplicate for them."
"6156","As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, he said: Fayyad ibn Muhammad al-Raqqi narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, from Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj al-Kinani, from Abd Allah al-Hamdani, from al-Walid ibn Uqbah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Mecca, people would bring their boys and he would wipe their heads and supplicate for them. He said: I was brought to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perfumed with khaluq, and he neither supplicated for me nor wiped my head. Nothing kept him from that except that my mother had perfumed me with khaluq, or words like it. So the son of Abd Allah or Abd al-Rahman was among those boys. And it is possible he had understood the pledge then, how it is, and pledged him, so that is as said about him. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, would thus be unique in pledging the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that day: by himself, by his father, by his son, and by his son's son, upon what they pledged him that day. We know of none else among people for whom that combined, may Allah's pleasure be upon him, and we ask Allah for success."
"6157","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Nafi' from Ibn Umar from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the two transacting parties, that they have the option until they part, except a sale with option. Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab, meaning al-Thaqafi, narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Sa'id say: I heard Nafi' narrate from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The two transacting parties have the option in their sale so long as they have not parted, unless the sale is with option. Nafi' said: Abd Allah, when he bought something pleasing to him, would part from his companion."
"6158","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: Nafi' narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two transacting parties have no sale between them until they separate, except a sale with option. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith and found in al-Thaqafi's hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the two parties the option so long as they have not parted. That parting may be what he mentioned in this hadith of his from Ibn Umar: that when he bought something pleasing to him he parted from his companion, making that mentioned parting bodily parting. And it may be that the parting Ibn Umar practiced was not the parting by which the mentioned option ends in this hadith; rather he did it because the hadith bears it, though what he held therein was other than that. He did what he did of what Nafi' mentioned from him as precaution against others' saying, so that no saying of others contrary to what he wants in that sale attach to him. Like what befell him in the sale he sold with acquittal from its defects, holding the ruling therein was what he held, but it was litigated to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who ruled against him contrary to what he held, of what Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated as due therein: he saw upon him the oath: By Allah, Mighty and Majestic, I neither sold it with this nor knew it nor concealed it. He refused to swear that and took back the slave. So what Nafi' mentioned of his practice in the hadith we cited may be for the like meaning. And we found from him what showed his position in that meaning: that sale is completed in the sold item before its two parties bodily part after contracting the sale."
"6159","As Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamzah ibn Abd Allah: that Abd Allah ibn Umar said: Whatever the striking of hands overtakes alive is of the buyer's wealth."
"6160","And as Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Hamzah ibn Abd Allah: that Abd Allah ibn Umar said, then he mentioned the same. This hadith showed that Ibn Umar's position was: what the striking of hands overtakes alive is of its buyer's wealth. That is so only once its ownership has fallen upon him by the striking, though he has not parted his seller bodily. Hushaym's hadith from Yahya which we mentioned, of the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The two transacting parties have no sale between them until they part, except a sale with option, is in our view not contrary to his other hadith we mentioned. Its meaning: There is no sale between them without option until they part; once they part, that parting cuts their option in it, except a sale with option, meaning: the option remains for its holder afterwards until the stipulated option period. That mentioned parting scholars disputed in interpretation: what is it? One group said: it is between the seller's saying to the buyer: I have sold to you, and the buyer's saying: I have accepted that from you. The seller may retract what he said before the buyer says: I have accepted that from you, and the buyer may accept that saying so long as he has not parted the seller bodily; if he parted him bodily he may no longer accept the saying said to him. The holders of this saying said: were it not so, he could accept that saying after a long period and after parting its sayer bodily. Among those who held this saying and took this hadith's meaning to that interpretation was Abu Yusuf. As Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Bishr ibn al-Walid, from Abu Yusuf, and we mentioned it afterwards to Ahmad ibn Abi Imran, who agreed with it in his narration from Bishr ibn al-Walid. Isa ibn Aban also agreed with Abu Yusuf on this interpretation. Others among scholars said: the seller's saying to the buyer: I have sold to you, and the buyer's saying: I have accepted from you, makes them parted, like Allah's saying, Mighty and Majestic, about divorce: But if they separate, Allah will enrich each from His bounty. As if a husband saying to his wife: I have divorced you for such, and she saying: I have accepted that from you, makes them separated with the separation Allah, Mighty and Majestic, spoke of, though they have not parted bodily. Likewise the owner's saying to the bargainer: I have sold you my goods for such, and the other saying: I have accepted that from you, makes them parted with the parting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke of, though they have not parted bodily. Among those who said this and explained it thus was Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. Others said: the parting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in this hadith is bodily parting after contracting the sale, for the bargainer and the bargained-with before contracting are bargainers, not transactors; they become transactors only after contracting the sale, and there the option becomes due to them, not before. Among those who held that was al-Shafi'i. What he adduced we found language permits contrary to, for we found in it naming one near a thing with the meaning of one who has reached it and is of its people, though not so in reality. Among it is Allah's saying, Mighty and Majestic: When you divorce women and they reach their term, retain them in kindness, not meaning when they have completed their terms they are retained in kindness; rather it is about their nearing their terms. Proved by Allah's saying, Mighty and Majestic, in the other verse: When you divorce women and they reach their term, do not prevent them from marrying their husbands. Of that is what all Muslims apply to Ibrahim's son commanded to be sacrificed, whether Isma'il or Ishaq, peace be upon them both, calling him the sacrificed for his nearness to sacrifice though he was not sacrificed. Of that is what they apply of what al-Muzani related to us from al-Shafi'i in interpreting the verse we mentioned: that the Arabs say: So-and-so entered such city, for his nearness to it and intending to enter it, though in reality he had not entered it. Since that is so in what we mentioned, the hadith we narrated the like of bears the same, and Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by it. Then we looked at this hadith from narrators other than Yahya, from Nafi', how it is."
"6161","We found Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid al-Sakuni narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, or the sale is with option."
"6162","And we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bundar narrated to us, he said: Yahya, meaning Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every two sellers, there is no sale between them until they part, or there is option."
"6163","And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, he said: Nafi' narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same. What we narrated of Ubayd Allah's hadith returns in meaning to what we narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari from Nafi' before it in this meaning. Then we looked at how others besides those we mentioned narrated it from Nafi'."
"6164","We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Harb informed us, he said: Muhriz ibn al-Waddah narrated to us, from Isma'il, meaning Ibn Umayyah al-Umawi, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two transacting parties have the option so long as they have not parted, unless the sale was with option; if the sale was with option, the sale is binding. This hadith is like the hadith of Yahya and Ubayd Allah we mentioned before it. Then we looked at how others besides those we mentioned narrated it from Nafi'."
"6165","We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim Abu al-Nu'man, meaning Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Sadusi, narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, or one of them says to his companion: Choose; and perhaps he said: or the sale is with option."
"6166","And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option until they part or the sale is with option; and perhaps he said: he transacted, or one of them says to the other: Choose."
"6167","And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: Abd al-A'la, meaning Ibn Abd al-A'la al-Sami, narrated to us, he said: Sa'id, meaning Ibn Abi Arubah, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, or he says: Choose. What Ayyub narrated from Nafi' is like what those we mentioned before him narrated from Nafi', except that in it is: Or one of them says to his companion: Choose. It may mean a saying said after the sale, giving option to one who had none before; or it may mean an option upon which they contract the sale, stipulated by one for his companion in the sale. The latter interpretation is worthier, for it returns to granting what the addressed lacked before. Then we looked whether others besides those we mentioned narrated this hadith from Nafi'."
"6168","We found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Nafi' dictated to us: that Abd Allah ibn Umar informed him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When two transacting parties transact a sale, each has the option regarding his sale so long as they have not parted, or their sale is with option; if it is with option, it is binding."
"6169","And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Maymun informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain, except that he said: The sale is binding. Abu Ja'far said: This meaning entered the meaning we mentioned before it. Then we looked whether others besides those we mentioned narrated it from Nafi'."
"6170","We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him. And we found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i informed us, from Malik, then both agreed, saying: from Nafi', from Abd Allah ibn Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two transacting parties, each has the option over his companion so long as they have not parted, except a sale with option. This hadith's meaning is like those agreeing with it in wording among what we mentioned before it. Then we looked: did others besides those we mentioned narrate it from Nafi'."
"6171","We found al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth informed us, from Nafi', from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: When two men transact, each has the option so long as they have not parted and are together, or one gives the other option. If one gives the other option and they transact upon that, the sale is binding. If they part after transacting and neither abandons the sale, the sale is binding. Abu Ja'far said: Abu Abd al-Rahman, meaning al-Nasa'i, wrote this hadith from me. This hadith shows that: Or one gives the other option, in what we mentioned before it, is upon an option upon which the two parties contract the sale, as in this hadith, not upon anything else some people took it to mean. How could one with option by the sale contract be given option! This is far from hearts' acceptance. Option-giving is to grant what was not due before, which requires it be as al-Layth narrated from Nafi': the option one party gives his companion is upon the option they negotiate until they contract the sale upon it, not an option they newly initiate after the sale. This proves sale becomes binding by contracting, with no option for either party after contracting, unless the sale occurred with one having option for a period, giving him option until that period ends. We found one who holds option means bodily parting after contracting the sale saying: when one gives his companion option after the sale, the option due to him by that giving is the option due to him before it. What the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of that was to convey a meaning to his community; on the interpretation of those we mentioned, it would carry no benefit, and far be it from Allah to be so. Rather in our view, and Allah knows best, it is as al-Layth clarified in his hadith, of what the sale contract occurs upon. When option upon which the sale occurred does not prevent its holder from owning what he bought before his option ends after he and his companion part from the sale place, they are likewise so before parting from the sale place. The mentioned binding option in the hadith is unlike that: the option between offer and acceptance, as we mentioned from its holders in this chapter. Then we returned to what reflection requires, and found ownerships may be in properties, in benefits, namely leases, and in private parts, namely what marriages grant and what khul' grants. Ownerships in private parts are completed before their contractors part, likewise leases are completed before their contractors part. By analogy likewise ownerships of properties, namely sales, are completed before their contractors bodily part after contracting, and we ask Allah for success."
"6172","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Abd Allah ibn Dinar from Ibn Umar from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every two sellers, there is no sale between them until they part, or the sale is with option."
"6173","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same."
"6174","And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same."
"6175","And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi and Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz al-Qasmali narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Thus those we mentioned narrated this hadith from Abd Allah ibn Dinar from Ibn Umar. And Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated it from Abd Allah ibn Dinar from Abd Allah ibn Umar, differing from that."
"6176","As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar: that he heard Abd Allah ibn Umar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The two sellers, each has the option over his companion so long as they have not parted, or their sale is with option; if the sale is with option, it is binding. The meaning of what is narrated from Abd Allah ibn Dinar from Ibn Umar in this meaning, despite its variant transmission, returns to agreeing with what we narrated before it. Our discussion of it is like our discussion of its like of what preceded of what is narrated from Nafi' from Ibn Umar, and we ask Allah for success."
"6177","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. Abu al-Awwam Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to me, from Ibn Ajlan, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two transacting parties have the option so long as they have not parted, unless it is a sale with option; it is not lawful for him to part from his companion fearing he will ask him to rescind."
"6178","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. We examined this hadith: in it the two parties have the option so long as they have not parted, like the hadith of Nafi' from Ibn Umar agreeing with it, whose meaning returns to it among what we mentioned earlier in this book. His saying: Or it is a sale with option, is as in Ibn Umar's hadith: Except a sale with option. What is in it of his saying: It is not lawful for him to part from him fearing he will ask him to rescind, we found no meaning worthier for it than: it is not lawful for the one against whom option lies, of the two parties in a sale with option, to part from his companion against whom he holds the option, fearing he will ask him to rescind, meaning: ask him in his sale to return it to him and dissolve it between them. That parting differs from the first parting disputed in interpretation as we mentioned, and is not cut off from him if he seeks him until he returns it to him and clears himself to him of guaranteeing it. For language applies that until a man says: I have not parted so-and-so for such years, not meaning he never left his sight or his body near his body, but that he never parted him in the understood constancy of the like. This supports what Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan held: that one with option of the two parties may not annul the sale by his option except in his companion's presence. And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by that, and Him we ask for success."
"6179","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Hakim ibn Hizam from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, from Hakim ibn Hizam, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The two sellers have the option until they part, or so long as they have not parted. If they are truthful and clarify, their sale is blessed for them; if they lie and conceal, the blessing of their sale is effaced."
"6180","And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salih Abu al-Khalil, from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, from Hakim ibn Hizam: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option until they part, or so long as they have not separated. If they are truthful and clarify, their sale is blessed for them; if they lie and conceal, perhaps some surplus will circulate between them, and the blessing of their sale is effaced. Hammam said: I heard Abu al-Tayyah say: I heard this hadith from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith from Hakim ibn Hizam from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. We examined this hadith: the two parties may transact goods of animals or other for prices that are in liabilities, of dinars, dirhams and other. The buyer thereby bears no clarification, since it is in his liability; clarification lies upon the seller of the goods, of a defect in it, or of a price he bought it for if he sold it at profit or at cost. And they may transact goods for goods, making upon each in what he sells his companion like what is upon his companion in what he sells him. The Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If they are truthful and clarify, their sale is blessed for them; if they lie and conceal, means some sellers, not all sellers, by what distinguishes some of them from others among what we mentioned, and we ask Allah for success."
"6181","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Abu Barzah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Wadi', he said: We stopped at a camp, and a companion of ours sold a horse to a man. We stayed in our camp our day and night. Next day the man stood to saddle his horse, and his companion said to him: You have sold to me. They disputed to Abu Barzah, who said: If you wish I will judge between you by the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, and I do not see you two have parted. Abu Ja'far said: Some who hold the option due to the two parties after contracting the sale used to argue with this hadith and with Abu Barzah's conduct in it and his saying: I do not see you two have parted. What is in this hadith is no proof for him, for the two parties stayed in their camp where they transacted a day and a night. We know each of them in his day and night did what the like does: rising for need of stool and urine, thereby parting his companion, and rising for prayer, thereby leaving what he was in and busying with other. The like, had it been in an exchange they contracted between them, then one did the like of what we know they did of rising for stool and urine without taking possession of what they exchanged, would void their exchange and exit them from it. Likewise the option, had it been due after the sale, these things would cut it. And Abu Barzah said to them: I do not see you two have parted, proving that parting in his view was other than bodily parting."
"6182","And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Abu al-Wadi', from Abu Barzah: that they disputed to him about a man who sold a slave girl, and the seller slept with her; in the morning he said: I do not approve her. Abu Barzah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, and they were in a hair tent. What is in this hadith differs from the hadith mentioned before it, for in the first hadith the sold item was a horse, and in the second the sold item was a slave girl. The hadith returns to Abu Barzah with the difference in these two narrations. When such difference occurs in it as we mentioned, neither narration is worthier than the other, and none may argue with one without his opponent arguing with the other. Neither contains what requires that the due option in the hadith we narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is bodily parting, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6183","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Utbah narrated to us, from Abu Kathir al-Ghubari, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, or the sale is with option. This hadith's meaning is like those agreeing with it in wording among the hadiths of Nafi' from Ibn Umar we mentioned, and the discussion of it is like our discussion there, and we ask Allah for success."
"6184","Chapter explaining the problematic report of Samurah ibn Jundub from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers have the option so long as they have not parted, and each takes what he approves of the sale. We examined this hadith and found in it: And each takes what he approves of the sale. The holders in this chapter unanimously hold that the mentioned parting in this hadith, if after the sale by bodies, the buyer may not take what he approves of the sale and leave the rest; with them he takes it all or leaves it all. He takes some and leaves some only before contracting the sale, so the sale is contracted between him and his companion in what he approves of it, not in what he disapproves. This proves the option of the two parties is before the sale's conclusion between them: between one saying to his companion: I have sold to you, and the other saying: I have accepted from you, and we ask Allah for success."
"6185","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying about the people of Badr, may Allah's pleasure be upon them: They are the best of people, and of his saying: The best of my community is my generation among whom I was sent, and that neither contradicts the other. Isma'il ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, meaning Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: Gabriel, peace be upon him, or a great angel, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: How are the people of Badr among you? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With us they are the best of people. The angel said: Likewise those among the angels with us who witnessed Badr. An objector said: This hadith gives the angels of Badr superiority over all people. Yet you narrated earlier in this book from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The best of my community is the generation among whom I was sent. Of that generation are those who witnessed Badr and those who did not, so this includes them all, witnesses and non-witnesses, while the first hadith gives the people of Badr superiority over the rest of that generation, a severe contradiction. Our answer, by Allah's aid, is: there is no contradiction, for the generation among whom he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent is the best of all generations, while they differ among themselves by causes distinguishing the excellent from the less excellent, as the prophets, Allah's blessings upon them, are the best of people yet differ among themselves by causes favoring some over others, as Allah, Exalted His praise, said in His Book: And We favored some of the prophets over others. Likewise the generation among whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent are the best and most excellent generations, yet differ among themselves by meanings distinguishing and favoring some over others. Likewise the people of Badr are distinguished from that generation's people by superiority over them, and they and the rest of that generation are distinguished from the rest of this community's generations by goodness and excellence. So the people of Badr are the best of the excellent, the generation, the best of generations, by their qualities not in others of that generation. Thus by Allah's praise and favor there is no contradiction in any of these reports; each aspect has a meaning other than the other, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6186","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: Do not pray after Asr unless the sun is high. Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Yazid ibn Sinan both narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us; each of them said in his hadith: He said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Mansur informed me, he said: I heard Hilal ibn Yasaf narrate from Wahb ibn al-Ajda', he said: I heard Ali, peace be upon him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not pray after Asr unless the sun is high."
"6187","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us, he said: Abidah, meaning Ibn Humayd, narrated to us, from Mansur. And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman, meaning Ibn Mahdi, narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah and Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Wahb ibn al-Ajda', from Ali, peace be upon him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6188","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Yahya, meaning Ibn Adam, from Sharik, from Mansur, from Salim, meaning Ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Wahb ibn al-Ajda', from Ali, peace be upon him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer after Asr unless you pray while the sun is high. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith and found in it the Messenger of Allah's permission for people to pray after Asr while the sun is high, the state when it is white untinged with yellowness, and his forbidding prayer after Asr in other than that state, namely the sun's declining to set, the state with its yellowing and reddening. Then an objector said: You narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, of what he addressed Ali, peace be upon him, with, proving the matter with Ali, peace be upon him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was contrary to what you narrated from Ali from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned in that."
"6189","What Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, that he informed him, he said: Hizam ibn Darraj informed me: that Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, prayed two rak'ahs after Asr on the road to Mecca. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, called him and rebuked him angrily, then said: By Allah, you know well that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid them."
"6190","And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Ubayd Allah al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Salih, meaning Ibn Abi al-Akhdar, narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Rabi'ah ibn Darraj: that Ali, peace be upon him, prayed two rak'ahs after Asr, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him, rebuked him angrily and said: You know well that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them. Abu Ja'far said: So is this hadith in what Ibn Uzayz and Abu Umayyah both narrated to us with the two chains in their two hadiths from Uqayl and Salih. Yazid ibn Abi Habib differed from them, inserting between Ibn Shihab and Rabi'ah ibn Darraj Ibn Muhayriz."
"6191","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me. And as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me: that Ibn Shihab wrote mentioning that Ibn Muhayriz informed him, from Rabi'ah ibn Darraj, informing him: that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, traveled and prayed Asr two rak'ahs on the road to Mecca, then turned and saw Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, praying voluntary prayers after it. He rebuked him angrily, then said: By Allah, you know well that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid them. Both agreed with them in what they narrated of this hadith: Yunus ibn Yazid, differing from Yazid ibn Abi Habib."
"6192","As Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ibn Darraj narrated to me: that Ali, peace be upon him, prayed two rak'ahs after Asr on the road to Mecca, so Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, called him and rebuked him angrily, then said: By Allah, you know well that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid them. He said: This hadith contains Umar's address to Ali of what he addressed him with, and in it: that Ali knew the Messenger of Allah's prohibition of prayer after Asr. Umar said it to him only knowing he knew the Messenger's prohibition of that, especially as Ali did not reject what he said to him. Is there in that what contradicts Wahb ibn al-Ajda's hadith from him or not? Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, is: neither Wahb's hadith nor Ibn Darraj's hadith contradicts the other in our view, and Allah knows best. Ali, peace be upon him, may have prayed while in his view the sun was high with the elevation permitting prayer by the Messenger's permission, while in Umar's view, may Allah be pleased with him, it was otherwise. They differed about the sun's elevation permitting prayer, not about what Ali knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, since elevation may be meant in a sense understood differently by different hearers. What was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Wahb ibn al-Ajda's hadith was prohibition of prayer after the sun's declining, not before that after Asr prayer. Then afterwards the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after Asr until the sun sets, encompassing what is in Wahb's hadith from Ali and adding to Wahb's hadith prohibition of prayer after Asr even while the sun is high until it sets. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, learned that, adopted it and imposed it on people. Ali, peace be upon him, did not learn it and did not know it, remaining upon what is in Wahb's hadith he narrated from him, and the hadith narrated from Umar in that."
"6193","The hadith narrated from Umar in that is what Abd al-Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah al-Attabi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us. And what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, Fahd ibn Sulayman and Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, then all said: He said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: More than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me, among them Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar was the dearest of them to me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after Fajr until the sun rises, and prayer after Asr until the sun sets. Except that Abd al-Aziz said in his hadith: Trustworthy men testified to me, among them Umar, and the most pleasing of them to me was Umar."
"6194","And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Mas'ud and Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la informed us, he said: Khalid, meaning Ibn al-Harith, narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu al-Aliyah narrate from Ibn Abbas, he said: A group most pleasing of them to me was Umar, then he mentioned the same."
"6195","And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sa'id, meaning Ibn Abi Arubah, narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Trustworthy men narrated to me, the most pleasing of them to me was Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same."
"6196","And what Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, he said: Trustworthy men testified to me, among them Umar, and the most pleasing of them to me was Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same."
"6197","And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban, meaning Ibn Yazid, narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Trustworthy men testified to me, among them Umar, and the most pleasing of them to me was: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same. This hadith requires forbidding prayer after Asr until the sun sets. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, imposed that on people. What Ali, peace be upon him, knew of the Messenger of Allah's prohibition of what he forbade among what we mentioned from him in Wahb ibn al-Ajda's hadith entered into it, and we ask Allah for success."
"6198","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from A'ishah in her interpretation of Umar ibn al-Khattab's prohibition of prayer after Asr, may Allah be pleased with him. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, he said: I said to A'ishah: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do, as if meaning after his noon prayer and after his Asr prayer? She said: He used to pray the midday prayer, then pray two rak'ahs after it, then pray Asr, then pray two rak'ahs after it. He said: I said: But I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, beating a man he saw praying two rak'ahs after Asr. She said: Umar prayed them, and knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed them. But your people, the people of Yemen, are uncouth folk; when they prayed noon they prayed after it until Asr, and when they prayed Asr they prayed after it until Maghrib. So he did well. This hadith may indicate that what A'ishah held about forbidding prayer after Asr was like what Ali, peace be upon him, held among what we mentioned from Wahb ibn al-Ajda' from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the chapter before this chapter. She did not have what Umar had from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding prayer after Asr until the sun sets. What Umar had in that was worthier than what Ali and she had, for what Umar had encompassed what they had of it and added what they lacked. A'ishah's hadith we mentioned shows that the Messenger of Allah's praying the two rak'ahs after Asr which he prayed was before his forbidding prayer after Asr until the sun sets, and that his forbidding prayer after Asr until the sun sets abrogated that, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6199","Chapter explaining the problematic disagreement over the ages of blood-money camels due for accidental killing, what they are, by what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, meaning Ibn Artah, from Zayd ibn Jubayr, from Khishf ibn Malik, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the blood-money for mistake in fifths."
"6200","And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil Fudayl ibn al-Husayn al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Jubayr, from Khishf ibn Malik al-Ta'i, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the blood-money for mistake: twenty jadha'ah, twenty hiqqah, twenty ibnat labun, twenty ibnat makhad, and twenty male ibn makhad."
"6201","And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Jubayr al-Jushami narrated to me, from Khishf ibn Malik al-Ta'i, he said: I heard Ibn Mas'ud say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed in the blood-money for mistake: twenty hiqqah, twenty jadha'ah, twenty ibnat labun, twenty ibnat makhad, and twenty male ibn labun. He said: What is in Ibn Abi Dawud's hadith of male ibn labun in place of male ibn makhad in Yazid's hadith, the correct in our view, and Allah knows best, is what is in Yazid's hadith, for scholars unanimously hold Ibn Mas'ud's saying about blood-money for mistake was so. This is a jurisprudential chapter scholars differ in: one group holds the saying upon which we authenticated Ibn Mas'ud's hadith, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions. Another group holds they are also fifths, putting ibn labun in place of ibn makhad as in Ibn Abi Dawud's hadith we mentioned. Among them holding that: Malik ibn Anas. They narrated it from Sulayman ibn Yasar, not going beyond him to anyone above him among the Prophet's Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, from Sulayman ibn Yasar as we mentioned from him. What Abu Hanifah and his companions held was worthier in our view, for ibn makhad are below ibn labun. We should not impose in that anything except what we certainly know is due; we certainly know the lower age is due, not the higher age. We mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book about the aggravated blood-money due in quasi-intentional killing: that it is one hundred camels, forty khalifah pregnant with their young. The remaining ages of it, in the saying of all holders of this saying, are: thirty hiqqah and thirty jadha'ah. Since what is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein is what we mentioned as established from him, the Messenger is the proof, and none may contradict what is established from him in anything. Quasi-intentional is weightier than mistake, for in it is intent toward killing, while mistake has none of that. So what is due of blood-money in mistake must be lighter than what is due in quasi-intentional. Since what is due in quasi-intentional of camels is hiqqah, jadha'ah and khalifah, what is due in mistake is lighter than that: what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein. And ibn makhad are below ibn labun, so what is due in that of the disputed ibn makhad and ibn labun in blood-money for mistake is ibn makhad, not ibn labun, for we know ibn makhad are below ibn labun. So what is due of that is the unanimously agreed amount, not the disputed amount, for properties are inviolable until dues in them are known. By what we mentioned is proved that the blood-money due in mistake is twenty hiqqah, twenty jadha'ah, twenty ibnat makhad, twenty ibn labun, and twenty ibn makhad; and that the blood-money due in quasi-intentional is thirty hiqqah, thirty jadha'ah, and forty between thaniyyah and bazil, all pregnant. So Muhammad ibn al-Hasan held about these two blood-moneys together, differing from Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf in what they held about blood-money in quasi-intentional: that it is quarters: twenty-five hiqqah, twenty-five jadha'ah, twenty-five ibnat makhad, and twenty-five ibnat labun, and we ask Allah for success."
"6202","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to the accusing husband after he and his wife finished li'an: You have no way to her. Yunus and Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conducted li'an between two brothers of Banu al-Ajlan, then said: Allah knows that one of you two is lying; you have no way to her. He said: My dowry which I paid her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you were truthful about her, it is for what you made lawful of her private part; if you lied about her, it is further from you than it. Al-Shafi'i said, in what al-Muzani related to us from him: in the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the accuser: You have no way to her, is proof he may never marry her. This question scholars differed about the duty in: one group held he may never marry her. Among them holding that: Malik and Abu Yusuf. Another group held he may not marry her so long as he stands upon his saying to her, but whenever he retracts it and declares himself liar and is flogged for that, he may marry her. Among those holding that: Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. We examined what al-Shafi'i said and found no proof for him in it, since the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the accuser: You have no way to her, was an answer to his seeking from her the dowry he paid her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it to him on account of that saying he said to him. This is worthier for the hadith, since it turns upon Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and Sa'id's position about the two mutual accusers was."
"6203","What Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shuja' narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that he used to say: When a man accuses his wife and they are separated, then he declares himself liar, his wife is returned to him while she is in waiting. That proves his position about the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned was contrary to the position al-Shafi'i took in it. His position was that one's narrating a hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and interpreting it in a meaning proves the intent is that meaning. Of that: what he said about Ibn Umar's hadith on parting after sale, that it is by bodies, adducing Ibn Umar's practice therein as proof of the Prophet's intent in it, as we mentioned in the chapter we cited it in earlier in this book. Of that: he made Amr ibn Dinar's saying in the hadith he narrated: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged by oath with witness, that it is in properties, making that his proof that judging by oath with witness is in properties specifically to the exclusion of others. And an advocate holding they never reunite, meaning the two accusers, said: It is narrated from Sahl ibn Sa'd his witnessing the Messenger of Allah's li'an between the spouses he conducted li'an between. Al-Zuhri said after that: The Sunnah has passed that they never reunite, and he mentioned in that."
"6204","What Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, of the story of the Messenger of Allah's li'an between the spouses he conducted li'an between. Ibn Shihab said: The Sunnah has passed that when they accuse each other they are separated, then never reunite. He said: This Sunnah in his view was the duty for the two accusers. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: it may be that was for the accuser while standing upon the accusation by which he accused his wife. And we found from al-Zuhri of his position in that also as we mentioned of this possibility."
"6205","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Zuhri, about the two accusers: They never return to each other, unless he declares himself liar and is flogged the hadd, and declares her innocence, then there is no blame on them to return to each other. Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib preceded al-Zuhri in this saying."
"6206","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from a man, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib: that the accuser, when he declares himself liar, his wife is returned to him. Sufyan said: We met Ibn Abi Hind and he narrated it to us from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib. Abu Ja'far said: His saying: His wife is returned to him, may mean by a new marriage. Like Sa'id's saying is also narrated from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i."
"6207","As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he said: If he is struck afterwards, meaning the accuser, he is a suitor among suitors; he may marry her if he wills and she wills. This advocate said: It is narrated from more than one of the Prophet's Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they never reunite."
"6208","He mentioned what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he said: The two mutual accusers never reunite."
"6209","And what Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, from Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr, from Ali the same."
"6210","And what Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, from Qays, I think he informed us from Asim, from Abu Wa'il, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud the same. Doubt about Asim in particular, I think it dropped from my book. He said: Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is: it may mean they intend by their saying: they do not reunite, upon the state in which they were separated, as al-Zuhri took the meaning of ever upon the like. This saying was worthier by analogy in our view, for we found them at first: the wife may seek the husband until li'an is conducted between them, the li'an requiring separation between them. And we found that if the husband declared himself liar and was flogged for that, then the wife sought his separation by his saying to her, she has no such right. So the cause for which li'an requiring separation is conducted between them is the husband's standing upon what he said to his wife, and that ceases with that cause's ceasing and with the hadd's execution upon him where due. They stand afterwards as spouses as before that saying. Likewise by analogy, when separated after li'an, the ruling barring their reunion stands between them so long as he stands upon the saying requiring li'an at first producing separation. When that saying ceases, they may stand upon what they were upon before that first saying; after separation likewise. The bar to reunion in the new state is what required the li'an producing the opposite of reunion. When that meaning ceases, what bars their reunion ceases, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"6211","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what he recited when he woke at night, of what Ibn Abbas narrated from him, of Surah Al Imran. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abi Namir, meaning Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir, narrated to us: that Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, informed him that he heard Ibn Abbas say: I spent a night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he finished the last evening prayer I left with him. When he entered the house he bowed two light rak'ahs, their bowing like their prostration, their prostration like their standing, in winter. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the chamber and I in the house. He said: I said: By Allah, I will watch the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tonight and see how he prays. He said: He lay down in his prayer place until I heard his snoring. He said: Then he woke, stood, looked to the sky and reflected, then recited the five verses of Surah Al Imran, then took a tooth-stick and cleaned his teeth, then went out and relieved himself, then returned to a hanging waterskin, poured on his hand, performed ablution, waking no one. Then he stood and prayed two rak'ahs, their bowing like their prostration, their prostration like their standing. He said: I think he prayed like what he slept. He said: Then he lay down in his place and slept until I heard his snoring. Then he did that five times, praying ten rak'ahs, then prayed witr with one. Bilal came and announced dawn to him, so he prayed the two dawn rak'ahs, then went out to dawn prayer. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith shows what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited of Surah Al Imran was five verses of it, from its end, though not mentioned in this hadith; it is mentioned in Malik's hadith we cited in the third chapter of this book from Makhramah ibn Sulayman from Kurayb, that Ibn Abbas informed him: that he spent the night with Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood until when midnight or a little after or a little before, awoke, then recited the ten concluding verses of Surah Al Imran. We mentioned in that chapter also in Ali ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Abbas's hadith that he recited: Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth, until he sealed the surah. We thus understood that what he recited of Surah Al Imran in the hadith cited in this chapter is: Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth, to the completion of five verses of it, His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Indeed You do not fail the promise. Then an objector said: Whence this difference? Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: that difference came only from the narrators of these hadiths besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). What is in the first hadith we narrated in this chapter is what hearts find was what the Messenger recited, for he recited what he recited seeking the supplication and reflection mentioned in those verses. What is after the five verses mentioned therein is not of that meaning at all; rather it is mention of Allah's answering those mentioned in those verses, then other than that of other meanings to the surah's end. Allah knows best the truth of what he, peace be upon him, did therein, and Him we ask for success."
"6212","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his giving option to the Bedouin after buying from him what he bought from him. Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: One whom I approve narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Jurayj, that Abu al-Zubayr narrated to him, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought from a Bedouin, I think Abu al-Zubayr said: of Banu Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah, a load of qart or a load of khabat. When it became due to him, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Choose. The Bedouin said: I never saw like today a buyer giving his seller option. Of whom are you? He said: Of Quraysh. Abu Ja'far said: Some people claim the man al-Layth left unnamed in this hadith is Abd Allah ibn Wahb. Some who hold the two parties have option in what they transact after contracting the sale until they part after the sale argue for it with this hadith, for in some narrations in that: Or one says to his companion: Choose, choose. We mentioned that, what is narrated therein, and what scholars said therein earlier in this book. In what we mentioned we established that option-giving is of what the sale is contracted upon, ending with the contract's completion. We argued for that with al-Layth's hadith he narrated in that from Nafi' from Ibn Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: Or one gives the other option; when one gives the other option and they transact upon that, the sale is binding. We established that option-giving is of what the sale is contracted upon, not upon option-giving by one party to his companion after the sale. What is in this hadith we narrated in this chapter: when it became binding, meaning the sold item, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him, meaning the Bedouin: Choose, shows the sale's binding between them before that option-giving. Then an objector said: What was the meaning of the Prophet's option-giving mentioned in this hadith to the Bedouin he gave option to? Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: that was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for what Abu Hurayrah narrated from him."
"6213","Of what Muhammad ibn Harb al-Madani Abu Abd Allah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever rescinds the sale of a regretful one, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will rescind his slip on the Day of Resurrection. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave that Bedouin option in what he bought from him, to have the reward of one rescinding a regretful seller, mentioned in this hadith, if he was regretful in what he sold him. And it is narrated that that buying by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from its seller was in a sale they transacted before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent and before he was made prophet."
"6214","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought before prophethood from a Bedouin a camel or other than that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him after the sale: Choose. The Bedouin looked and said to him: By your life of Allah, who are you? When Islam came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made option after sale. That shows what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before rulings and laws. This advocate said: In this hadith: When Islam came the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made option after sale. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: that option may be upon choice, not obligation. What is sought therein is what we mentioned in interpreting the hadith cited at this chapter's beginning, and we ask Allah for success. And also narrated from Ibn Abbas from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning."
"6215","What Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mu'adh, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) transacted with a man. When they transacted he said to him: Choose. He said: I have chosen. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Such is sale. Those holding option is due to the two parties after sale, or one gives his companion option, said: This hadith proves our position, confirmed by the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Such is sale. In our view this hadith adds nothing to the first hadith's meaning we mentioned. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Such is sale, may mean: such is the sale people conduct, that they conduct their sales upon giving one another option, to attain thereby the meaning in Abu Hurayrah's hadith we cited in this chapter about rescinding the regretful one's sale, and with Allah is success."
"6216","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his commanding Umar or Umayr, freedman of the family of Abu al-Lahm, when he asked him what he asked of Khaybar spoils, to wear the sword before ordering him anything of them. Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Hakam al-Judhami informed me, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Muhajir, that he narrated to him, he said: Umayr, freedman of the family of Abu al-Lahm, narrated to me, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he conquered Khaybar. I stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, my share. He said: Take this sword and wear it. He said: I wore it, and its shoe trailed. He said: He ordered for me from the khurthi. Uthman said: I said to him: And was he then a slave? He said: I do not know; true is what is said true. This hadith shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding this mentioned man to wear the sword, and when he wore it its shoe trailed on the ground, so he ordered for him from the khurthi what he ordered him of it. We examined this hadith to learn the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to wear the sword, leaving commanding others among people with him then, to learn its intent, Allah willing. We looked: was there in that man a meaning distinguishing him from others present at that conquest."
"6217","We found Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuh Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Muhajir, from Umayr, freedman of Abu al-Lahm, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Khaybar with the spoils present, and I was an owned slave. I said: O Messenger of Allah, give me. He said: Wear the sword. I wore it, and it fell to the ground. So he gave me from the khurthi of goods. By this hadith we learned that man was a slave. His Sunnah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about slaves attending fighting was not to allot them a share, but to reward them from the spoils of that fighting."
"6218","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Qays, meaning Qays ibn Sa'd, narrate from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, he said: Najdah ibn Amir wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the woman and slave attending battle, are shares allotted them? Ibn Abbas wrote to him, and I am witness: They were not allotted shares when attending battle, except to be rewarded from the people's spoils. Since his Sunnah about slaves attending fighting was what we mentioned, we understood that what he rewarded them with of spoils was according to their avail in the fighting producing those spoils. They were not in his Sunnah like others of freemen therein, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, undertook the measures of freemen's shares of spoils, equalizing their strong and weak therein. Slaves in what we mentioned were unlike that of what we described. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that mentioned man to wear the sword to learn his avail's measure in that fighting, to give him of the spoils therefrom accordingly. Then an objector said: How may he give him of the spoils what he deserves by his fighting of them, when what he deserves belongs to his owner, and in what you narrated is nothing proving his owner permitted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to give him that and hand it to him. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: it is narrated that those owning him asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that and permitted him it."
"6219","As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhajir, from Umayr, freedman of Abu al-Lahm, he said: I witnessed Khaybar with my masters. They spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about me and told him I am owned. He commanded me, and I wore the sword, and I dragged it. He ordered for me something from the khurthi of goods. We thus understood: the Messenger of Allah's giving to that owned one what he gave of what belongs to his owner was by his owner's asking him that. Thus by Allah's praise and favor when these reports are combined, all narrated therein is within the Messenger of Allah's Sunnah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his rulings, and we ask Allah for success."
"6220","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the dearest people to him. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah informed us, he said: Umar ibn Abi Salamah informed us, from his father, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, he said: I passed and Ali and al-Abbas, peace be upon them both, were sitting. They said: O Usamah, seek permission for us. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Ali and al-Abbas are at the door seeking permission. He said: Do you know what brought them? I said: No. He said: But I know; admit them. They entered, and Ali said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are dearest to you? He said: Fatimah daughter of Muhammad. He said: I am not asking about women. He said: One upon whom Allah bestowed favor and I bestowed favor: Usamah ibn Zayd. Ali said: Then whom? He said: Then you."
"6221","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hammam Fahd ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: Ali and al-Abbas, peace be upon them both, came while I was in the mosque. They said: Seek permission for us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I entered and sought permission for them. He said: Do you know why they came? I said: No, by Allah. He said: But I know; admit them. They entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we came to ask you about the dearest of your household to you. He said: Fatimah. They said: We are not asking you about women; we ask you about men. He said: Usamah. Al-Abbas said, as if angry: Then whom, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Then Ali. He said: You made your uncle the last of the people! He said: O Abbas, Ali preceded you in emigration. Abu Ja'far said: In Ibrahim ibn Marzuq's hadith Ali's question to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was about the dearest people to him, and in Ibn Abi Dawud's hadith his question was about the dearest of his household to him. His answer, peace be upon him, to him in that was what he answered him in these two hadiths, and in them: that Usamah was the dearest of men to him. Then an objector said: You narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) elsewhere that Usamah's place in his love contradicts this, and he mentioned."
"6222","What Yazid ibn Sinan and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Umar said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Usamah ibn Zayd, and some people criticized his command. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: If you criticize his command, you used to criticize his father's command before. By Allah, he was fit for command, and he was among the dearest people to me, and this one is among the dearest people to me after him."
"6223","And what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us. And what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, then each said: He said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith. He said: This hadith has the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Usamah is among the dearest people to him, while the hadith narrated before it has that he is the dearest of men to him. These are two contradictory hadiths. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: they are not contradictory as thought, for the first hadith has Ali's asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the dearest people to him and about the dearest of his household to him, and his informing him in answer that it is Fatimah. In the second hadith his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Usamah: He is among the dearest people to him, and people include Fatimah. Since Fatimah, peace be upon her, was above Usamah in his love, peace be upon him, Usamah's place in his love is below that. So he is among the dearest people to him when people include women and men, and the dearest of men to him, since Fatimah is not of men but of women. This proves no contradiction of either hadith with the other. He said: You narrated of his answer to Amr ibn al-As when he asked him about the dearest people to him, and he mentioned."
"6224","What Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, he said: Amr ibn al-As narrated to me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with the army of Dhat al-Salasil. He said: I said: Which people are dearest to you? He said: A'ishah. I said: Then among men? He said: Then her father. I said: Then whom? He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab. Then he counted men. He said: By this hadith the Messenger of Allah's answer to Amr with what he answered him therein contradicts what he answered Ali with in Usamah's hadith cited in this chapter. And he mentioned in that also."
"6225","What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq informed us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Isma'il, meaning Ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays, meaning Ibn Abi Hazim, from Amr ibn al-As, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are dearest to you that I love him? He said: A'ishah. I said: I am not asking you about women; I ask you about men. He said: Abu Bakr, or he said: Her father, may Allah be pleased with him. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: Amr may have known that the Prophet's household have of his love what others lack, so his asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the dearest people to him meant people besides his household. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew his intent therein and answered him with the answer mentioned in his hadith. Usamah's hadith has Ali's asking him, peace be upon him, about what he asked, and Ali is of his household, so he answered him with what he answered him in that hadith. Then an objector said: Usamah is mentioned therein, and he is not of his household. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: it may be that was when Usamah was of his household, for his father used to be called his son, said Zayd ibn Muhammad."
"6226","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Ghamr narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: By Allah, we used to call Zayd ibn Harithah: Zayd ibn Muhammad, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Call them by their fathers. Abu Ja'far said: Usamah was then to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a son's son, thus of his household. By that meaning he preceded others besides him of the Prophet's household mentioned in his hadith in the Messenger's love. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated that with what He abrogated it, of what we recited, and by His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, returning Zayd, Usamah and their likes to His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Call them by their fathers; that is more just with Allah. If you know not their fathers, they are your brothers in religion and your freedmen. This proves that when Usamah left the sonship wherein he deserved precedence over others of the Messenger of Allah's household (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Messenger's love, the Messenger's love (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) afterwards returned to whom he mentioned of his love after him of his household. And another objector said: You narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning what you narrated of it among what you mentioned in this chapter, while you narrate from him what contradicts that, and he mentioned."
"6227","What Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, he said: I asked A'ishah: Which of the Messenger of Allah's Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was dearest to him? She said: Abu Bakr. I said: Then whom? She said: Then Umar. I said: Then whom? She said: Then Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. He said: I said: Then whom? So she fell silent. He said: What is in this hadith of this meaning contradicts what you narrated before it in Usamah ibn Zayd's hadith in this chapter. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: there is no contradiction in anything we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for what we narrated from him in Usamah's hadith is upon the truths of what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held therein, since he was asked about it and answered his questioner with what he answered him in Usamah's hadith. What is in A'ishah's hadith is her answer to what she was asked about what he was upon, upon what occurs in her heart of what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon, and it may be contrary to that. He said: You narrated from her an answer to the like question contradicting this answer, and he mentioned."
"6228","What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Adam informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from Jumay', meaning Ibn Umayr, he said: I entered with my father upon A'ishah while I was a boy, and he mentioned Ali to her. She said: I never saw a man dearer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than him, nor a woman dearer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than his wife."
"6229","And what al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Jumay' ibn Umayr, he said: I entered with my mother upon A'ishah, and my mother said to her: Which women were dearest to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: Fatimah. She said: Then among men? She said: Her husband. He said: What is from her in this hadith contradicts what is from her in the hadith you mentioned from her before it in this chapter. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help: there is no contradiction in that as thought; rather A'ishah was asked in her first hadith about the dearest people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and what she held was that none escapes him that none precedes his household in his love, just as none besides them preceded them in conveying from him at the season, Surah Bara'ah, and in his saying: None conveys from me except a man of my household. So she answered with the mentioned answer therein about the dearest people to him besides his household. And she was asked in her second hadith about Ali, who is of his household, and answered therein with the answer she answered therein. This confirms what we took Usamah's hadith and Amr's hadith's meaning upon, of each one's meaning we mentioned in this chapter. And what confirms what we mentioned in what we narrated from A'ishah about the rest of the Messenger of Allah's household (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and others besides them among people in his love."
"6230","What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth narrated to us, he said: al-Nu'man ibn Bashir said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and heard A'ishah's voice saying: By Allah, I know that Ali is dearer to you than my father, twice or thrice. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission and entered, made toward her and said: O daughter of so-and-so, shall I not hear you raising your voice over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon what A'ishah said of that without rejecting it. All meanings of all we narrated in this chapter thus emerge without contradiction. Our mentioning Ali's precedence, peace be upon him, over Abu Bakr in the Messenger of Allah's love (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not bar Abu Bakr preceding him in excellence with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); each has his place with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in love and excellence, may Allah's pleasure be upon them both and upon his rest of Companions besides them, and we ask Allah for success."
"6231","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will clothe you with a shirt; if they want you to take it off, do not take it off. Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Baghandi and Fahd ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, they said: al-Minhal ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt pain one day and sent for Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. I heard him say to him: O Uthman, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will clothe you with a shirt; if they want you to take it off, do not take it off. It was said to her: Where were you, that you did not mention this! She said: I forgot it."
"6232","And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us. And Fahd and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Amir, from Nu'man ibn Bashir al-Ansari, he said: A'ishah said to me: I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Uthman ibn Affan, perhaps Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will clothe you with a shirt; if they want you to take it off, do not take it off. O Uthman ibn Affan, perhaps Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will clothe you with a shirt; if they want you to take it off, do not take it off. He said: I said: O Mother of the Believers, where were you regarding this hadith? She said: I forgot it, by Allah, O son of my sister; I did not think I heard it. We examined this hadith and found Uthman's pledge, may Allah be pleased with him, was a pledge of guidance, rectitude, uprightness, and agreement of the Emigrants, Helpers and the Messenger of Allah's other Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon it. They did not dispute therein nor differ. The affair continued for him, may Allah's pleasure be upon him, upon that for whatever of his caliphate's term Allah willed. Then there occurred among people about his affair what occurred of disagreement. Some claimed against him alteration and change of what he was upon before, far be it from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that it was so, until that caused their grouping against him in his affair and their differing over him therein, until some intended removing him from that, claiming against him departure from it by innovations they claimed he innovated, with which his remaining upon it is not proper. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) foretold in his affair of what he addressed him with in his covenant to him in that affair, of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, informed him of it among what we narrated in this hadith, proves that his states, may Allah's pleasure be upon him, then are the states by which he deserved what he deserved of the caliphate at his affair's beginning and people's agreement upon that for him: he had not changed from that nor turned from it to other than it. Had he changed from that and turned to other than it of what was claimed against him, he would thereby have exited what his authority was due to him by, of the causes entitling him when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to hold to the caliphate he was upon. He would have commanded him to return it to another besides him deserving it, for Allah, Blessed and Exalted, had informed him what would befall him and what would be sought from him of abandoning the caliphate that was his before that, of what his deserving it was by, of the causes in him. In the Messenger of Allah's commanding him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to keep to it and hold to it is proof that his states at that time are states of deserving it, with no alteration with it therein, nor change from what he was upon before of what he deserved it by, and with Allah is success."
"6233","Chapter explaining the problematic report narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about cursing parents: that it is the greatest of sins, or the greatest of major sins. Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The greatest sin is that a man curse his parents. It was said to him: O Messenger of Allah, how does a man curse his parents? He said: He curses a man, so he curses his father; and he curses his mother, so he curses his mother."
"6234","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrate from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The greatest of major sins is that a man curse his parents. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how does a man curse his parents? He said: He curses a man, so he curses his father, so he curses his father; and he curses his mother, so he curses his mother."
"6235","And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to me. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. We examined this hadith and found in it: that a man's cursing his parents is the greatest of major sins, or the greatest of sins. That seemed remote to hearts to be so, for among major sins and sins is what is above a man's cursing his parents: associating with Allah, Mighty and Majestic. We looked whether this hadith is narrated contrary to what we mentioned it with in this chapter."
"6236","5317 - We found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban - meaning al-Nahwi - narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the major sins? He said: Associating partners with Allah, mighty and majestic is He. He said: Then what? He said: Then disobedience to parents. He said: Then what? He said: Then the engulfing oath (false oath that immerses one in sin). Abu Umayyah said to us: In my book this hadith is in two places, one from Shayban and the other from Sufyan. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith goes back to Abdullah ibn Amr, who also narrated the first hadith, and both of his hadiths are raised to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and both chains are unassailable with no doubt about any narrator. Thus we understood that al-Sha'bi preserved from Abdullah ibn Amr what Humayd fell short of, and one who preserves is weightier than one who falls short. So the greatest of major sins returns to associating partners with Allah as in al-Sha'bi's hadith, then disobedience to parents as also in his hadith, then the engulfing oath as also in his hadith. Both he and Humayd agreed from Abdullah ibn Amr that disobedience to parents is among sins or major sins, but al-Sha'bi preserved that he placed it after shirk with Allah, so it is established in the second rank of major sins or sins, while Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman preserved that it is in the first rank. The weightier of both narrations from Abdullah ibn Amr is what al-Sha'bi narrated for what we mentioned, and we ask Allah for success."
"6237","Chapter 850 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what came from him concerning Barwa' bint Washiq, authenticating its chains from him, and clarifying the rulings in it. 5318 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Khilas ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn Utbah, and from Abu Hassan, from Abdullah ibn Utbah: that Ibn Mas'ud was repeatedly visited about a man who married a woman then died without fixing a dower for her and without consummating with her, and they differed coming to him for a month, then he judged that she has the dower of her womenfolk, and she has inheritance, and upon her is the waiting period, then he said: If it is correct it is from Allah, mighty and majestic, and if it is wrong it is from me. Then al-Jarrah and Abu Sinan stood and testified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged thus concerning Barwa' bint Washiq al-Ashja'iyyah, whose husband was Hilal ibn Marwan. Abu Ja'far said: We know of no one who opposed Hammam in the chain of this hadith."
"6238","5319 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: Abdullah was approached about a woman whose husband died leaving her without fixing a dower and without consummating with her, and they kept coming to him and he did not give them a fatwa, until they persisted with him, then he said: I shall speak by my opinion; I see for her the dower of her womenfolk, neither deficient nor excessive, upon her is the waiting period, and for her is inheritance. Then Ma'qil ibn Sinan stood and testified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning Barwa' bint Washiq al-Ashja'iyyah as you judged, so Abdullah rejoiced."
"6239","5320 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, then he mentioned the same, except he did not say the dower of her womenfolk but said the dower of her equals. Sufyan said: And by this we take. Abu Ja'far said: We know of no narrator of this hadith from Mansur who opposed al-Thawri in what he narrated from him nor in the chain, and Za'idah ibn Qudamah also narrated it from Mansur and agreed with al-Thawri in its text and chain, except he added al-Aswad along with Alqamah."
"6240","5321 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri informed us, he said: Abu Sa'id Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah - Abu Ja'far said: meaning the freedman of Banu Hashim - narrated to us, from Za'idah ibn Qudamah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: Abdullah was approached about a man who married a woman without fixing for her, then died before consummating with her, so Abdullah said: Ask, do you find any precedent in it? They said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, we find no precedent. He said: I speak by my opinion, and if it is correct it is from Allah, mighty and majestic: She has the dower of her womenfolk, neither deficient nor excessive, for her is inheritance and upon her is the waiting period. Then a man of Ashja' stood and said: In such a case the Messenger judged among us concerning a woman called Barwa' bint Washiq who married a man and he died before consummating with her, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged for her the like of the dower of her womenfolk, for her inheritance and upon her the waiting period. So Abdullah raised his hands and said Allah is Most Great. As for al-Sha'bi, there was disagreement from him about from whom he took this hadith; Abdullah ibn Awn narrated from him that he took it from the Ashja'i without naming him in his hadith."
"6241","5322 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Awn informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from the Ashja'i, he said: I saw Ibn Mas'ud rejoice with a joy I never saw the like of. A man came to him and asked about a man who married a woman without fixing a dower and without consummating, then died. He said: I have heard nothing about it. The man said: Even if I kept coming for a month I would ask none but you, and I find none to ask but you. He said: I shall speak about it by my opinion; if I am right Allah will grant me success: I see for her the dower of her womenfolk, neither deficient nor excessive, and upon her is the waiting period. Then the Ashja'i said: I testify that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged as you judged. Abu Ja'far said: The mentioned Ashja'i from whom al-Sha'bi took this hadith is Ma'qil ibn Sinan, whose death was late among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he died on the day of al-Harrah, one of the Companions killed in it. As for Dawud ibn Abi Hind, he mentioned from al-Sha'bi that he took it from Alqamah."
"6242","5323 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud: that they asked him about a man who married a woman and died without fixing a dower for her. He kept them waiting a month, then said: I speak about it by my opinion; if it is correct it is from Allah, and if wrong from myself: She has the dower of her womenfolk, neither deficient nor excessive, for her inheritance and upon her the waiting period. Then Ma'qil ibn Sinan stood and said: I testify that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged thus concerning a woman of ours called Barwa' bint Washiq. As for Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, he also mentioned from him that he took it from Alqamah."
"6243","5324 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to me, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, and he mentioned it from Alqamah, then he mentioned it with the meaning of what Dawud mentioned from him. As for Firas ibn Yahya, he mentioned that he - meaning al-Sha'bi - took it from Masruq."
"6244","5325 - As Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Suri narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Firas, from Amir, from Masruq, from Ibn Mas'ud, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Rabi', from Asad, from Hammad, from Dawud."
"6245","5326 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur - meaning al-Kawsaj - informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman - meaning Ibn Mahdi - narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, then he mentioned the same. Abu Ja'far said: It is possible that al-Sha'bi took it from all three of them, narrating it once from one of them, another time from another, and another time from the third. As for Abd Khayr, he narrated it from Ma'qil without disagreement from him in its chain."
"6246","5326m - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to me, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Abd Khayr, he said: A man came to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and asked about a man who married a woman without fixing for her, then died without consummating. Abdullah kept putting them off, then said: I speak about it by my opinion; if correct from Allah, mighty and majestic, if wrong from me and Satan: I see for her the dower of her womenfolk, upon her the waiting period, and for her inheritance. Then Ma'qil ibn Sinan al-Ashja'i, who was present, said: I testify the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged thus concerning a woman of ours called Barwa' bint Washiq. He said: Abdullah was never seen more joyful than that day, for agreeing with the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then we returned to the rulings in this hadith: in it is the permissibility of marriage without a named dower, as Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri, the companions of Abu Hanifah and al-Shafi'i say, contrary to Malik who annuls it during the spouses' lifetime before consummation and leaves annulment after consummation or death of either spouse. The Book of Allah testifies for the former in His saying: There is no blame on you if you divorce women you have not touched nor fixed a dower for, and provide for them. Divorce does not occur except in a valid marriage, so what we mentioned is established by the Book of Allah, then the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then by the consensus of Muslims; for they do not differ that inheritance is due to the survivor from the estate, and inheritance is due only by a valid marriage before the death. Then by their consensus that consummation does not annul such marriage, and consummation does not validate what is invalid. Thus we understood marriage stands by itself, not by the dower it entails. Then we found the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed on the validity of the contract when it occurs thus, and on inheritance therein, differing only on the dower for the wife after death. Some said she has the dower, among them Abdullah ibn Mas'ud in what we narrated in this chapter. Among those who said she has no dower: Ali ibn Abi Talib, Abdullah ibn Abbas, Abdullah ibn Umar, and Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them."
"6247","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: Abd Khayr narrated to me, from Ali - peace be upon him - about a man who marries a woman then dies without fixing a dower and without consummating: She has inheritance, upon her is the waiting period, and she has no dower."
"6248","And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abd Khayr, from Ali likewise."
"6249","And as Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Ma'n narrated to us, he said: I asked Ibn Jurayj about a woman whose husband died before consummation without naming a dower, so he narrated to me from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Inheritance suffices her."
"6250","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik informed him from Nafi': that the daughter of Ubaydullah ibn Umar, whose mother was the daughter of Zayd ibn al-Khattab, was under a son of Abdullah ibn Umar, and he died without consummating with her, so her mother sought her dower, and Abdullah ibn Umar said: She has no dower; had she a dower we would not withhold it nor wrong her. She refused to accept that, so they appointed Zayd ibn Thabit between them, and he judged she has no dower but has inheritance."
"6251","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Sulayman ibn Yasar: that Ibn Umar married a son of his to the daughter of his brother Ubaydullah ibn Umar, his son then being small, without fixing a dower, and the boy remained a while then died, so the girl's maternal uncle disputed with Ibn Umar before Zayd ibn Thabit. Ibn Umar said to Zayd: I married my son intending to do good for him, but he died before that without fixing a dower. Zayd said: She has inheritance if the boy had property, upon her is the waiting period, and she has no dower. Then we returned to what analogy requires: we found the agreed principle that a divorcee before consummation with a named dower has half of it and no waiting period, and if no dower was named she has provision and no waiting period; and if he consummated then divorced, she has the dower of her equals if none was named, and all what was named if named, with waiting period upon her. So the case with waiting period has dower, and the case without has half or provision. And when he dies leaving her without named dower and without consummation, upon her is the waiting period by all their sayings, indicating death being among cases requiring waiting period is among cases requiring dower. And in the hadith of Barwa' bint Washiq is also the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for her with the dower of her equals among her women, neither deficient nor excessive. Her women reasonably are the women of her clan, as in Arabic speech until the Book of Allah brought it in: Come, let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women; those women were their equals among the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the women of those he called to imprecation, not others. Likewise the women of a wife whose dower of equals is sought are her womenfolk; this is the meaning of Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Shafi'i. As for Ibn Abi Layla, he said: Her women are those from her father's side: her paternal aunts, her sisters, and her maternal aunts. As for Malik, he said: They are her equals in rank and beauty, without regard to lineage. What the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicates is weightiest. As for Ibn Abi Layla's including maternal aunts, it has no meaning to us, for a woman may be of Quraysh while her maternal aunts are slavewomen. When his saying was invalid, we considered the other two, and what Malik said is what hearts accept, not contradicting what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Yet we considered Malik's saying and found it regards the woman's desirable qualities like beauty and intellect, and we found she is desired also for lineage, nobility and qualities distinguishing her from her equals in beauty and intellect; as beauty and intellect are considered, so her kind, house and fathers for whose place she is desired are also considered. Malik himself said about a woman confused about menstruation that she considers the days of her womenfolk in such; and if that is considered in menstruation in which a woman may differ from her mother and sister, then considering it for dower is more deserving, and we ask Allah for success."
"6252","Chapter 851 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the intent of what he used in his sermons and speech in his saying: Amma ba'd (As to what follows). 5327 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: Amma ba'd, the sons of Hisham ibn al-Mughirah sought permission to marry their daughter to Ali ibn Abi Talib, and I do not permit, for Fatimah is a part of me. We already mentioned this hadith of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah earlier in this book with other chains."
"6253","5328 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said in his sermon: Amma ba'd. Also included in this chapter is what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the sermon of need mentioning therein Amma ba'd, as we mentioned earlier in this book. An objector said: What is intended by Amma ba'd in these reports and in speech begun with nothing preceding it to be after? Our answer by Allah's success: The Arabs use in speech brevity and indications of meanings they intend, knowing the knowledge of those they address. Their saying Amma ba'd with which they begin speech intends a deleted meaning on which that speech is built; among that is that they begin what they intend with praise of Allah and His name, and books proceeded after them upon that. So the meaning of Amma ba'd is: As to after what was from them of naming and praise, then such and such, then they mention what they intend while omitting mention of what they intended. Proof is their raising ba'du, since the annexed and annexed-to are as one thing; had they brought it complete they would say: Amma ba'da kitābinā hādhā, with ba'da accusative as an adjective, then say: such occurred. When they omitted that, they raised ba'du, which linguists call ghayah (limit). Likewise Allah's saying: To Allah belongs the command before and after, meaning before everything and after everything to which ba'du is annexed; when He omitted mention, He raised qablu and ba'du as ghayah. Likewise they say: I give you a dirham, la ghayru, raising ghayru; had they completed they would make it accusative: I gave you a dirham, la ghayrahu. And success is from Allah."
"6254","Chapter 852 - Clarification of the obligation in what scholars differed about a man mutilating his slave, whether emancipation is due thereby or otherwise without emancipation. 5329 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Umar ibn Isa al-Qurashi then al-Asadi, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A slavegirl came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: My master accused me and seated me on fire until my private part burned. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to her: Did he see that upon you? She said: No. He said: Did you confess anything to him? She said: No. Umar said: Bring him to me. When Umar saw the man he said to him: You punish with Allah's punishment! He said: O Commander of the Faithful, I accused her regarding herself. He said: Did you see that upon her? The man said: No. He said: Did she confess to you? He said: No. He said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, had I not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No slave is retaliated from his owner, nor a child from his parent, I would have retaliated her from you. Then he stripped him and beat him one hundred lashes, and said: Go, you are free for the sake of Allah, mighty and majestic, and you are a freed ally of Allah and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I testify I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever burns his slave with fire or mutilates him with a mutilation, he is free, and he is the ally of Allah and His Messenger. Al-Layth said: This is an acted-upon matter."
"6255","5330 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Laqit narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Sandar, from his father: that he was a slave of Zinba' ibn Salamah, who blamed him then castrated and mutilated him, so he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who spoke harshly to Zinba' and freed him from him. Abu Ja'far said: These two hadiths are what those who hold the slave is freed upon his master by mutilation argue with among what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); they are those who hold the saying of Malik and of al-Layth, except Malik made his alliance to his master. What they also argue with is what is narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, therein."
"6256","As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that Abu Yazid al-Qaddah informed him, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab when a black slavegirl came to him burned with fire, so Umar said Inna lillahi when he saw her, and said: Who is your master? She said: So-and-so, so he was brought, and he said: You punished her with Allah's punishment. By Allah, were it not, I would have retaliated her from you. So he freed her and ordered him flogged. We examined what they argued with: we found the first hadith we mentioned returns to Umar ibn Isa, who is unknown, and such a matter cannot stand on his like. We found the second hadith, though above the first, also not decisive in this chapter, nor establishing proof against an opponent when argued with its like. We found the third hadith, though its chain is fair with acceptable men, also contains no proof for those arguing, for Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, may have done it as punishment of the doer, since his school was punishments in the wealth of offenders, as he did with Hatib regarding his slaves whom he starved until it drove them to steal a she-camel of a Muzani man worth four hundred dirhams, so Hatib paid eight hundred dirhams."
"6257","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib: that slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel of a Muzani man and slaughtered it, so it was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who ordered Kathir ibn al-Salt to cut their hands, then Umar said: I see you starve them, then Umar said: By Allah I shall fine you a fine that will burden you, then said to the Muzani: What was your camel's price? He said four hundred dirhams, so Umar said: Give him eight hundred dirhams. What Umar held of this, the one arguing with his hadith we narrated in this chapter does not say. Since Umar's act admits what we mentioned, the emancipation of the fire-burned slavegirl may also be of that kind. When Umar's view therein is widened by consensus against what he did, and since his school therein was among early-Islam punishments in wealth: among that what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on zakah: Whoever gives it seeking reward, we accept it from him, otherwise we shall take it and half his wealth, a resolve of our Lord's resolves. And among that what was narrated about one who falls upon his wife's slavegirl, coerced or not, which we shall mention later in this book if Allah wills. When that was abrogated and its opposite used, likewise punishments in wealth by mutilations and others are likewise, and punishments return to their likes, and taking others by them is left. Then we returned to what is narrated from the Messenger entering this chapter."
"6258","5331 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik informed him from Hilal ibn Usamah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Umar ibn al-Hakam: that he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a slavegirl who grazed sheep of mine, and I came to her and a sheep was missing, so I asked her and she said: The wolf ate it, so I grieved over it, and I was of the sons of Adam, so I slapped her face, and I owe a neck (emancipation), shall I free her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Where is Allah, mighty and majestic? She said: In the heaven. He said: Who am I? She said: You are the Messenger of Allah. He said: Free her. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Malik says in this hadith's chain: Hilal ibn Usamah, while others narrating from Hilal say Hilal ibn Ali, and some say Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah. It is possible Hilal is Ibn Ali ibn Usamah, so Malik attributed him to his grandfather, or his father from Ali was surnamed Abu Maymunah. And in it is from Umar ibn al-Hakam, while all people say concerning it: from Mu'awiyah ibn al-Hakam, opposing Malik therein."
"6259","5332 - We found Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Mu'awiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami, he said: I released small sheep of mine grazed by a slavegirl of mine toward Uhud and al-Jawwaniyyah, and found the wolf had taken a sheep, so I struck her a blow, and I told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that, saying: O Messenger of Allah, had I known she is a believer I would have freed her, so he said: Bring her to me. I brought her, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Where is Allah, mighty and majestic? She said: In the heaven. He said to her: Who am I? She said: You are the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: She is a believer, so free her."
"6260","5333 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr informed us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah narrated to me, he said: Ata ibn Yasar narrated to me, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami narrated to me, then he mentioned it. He said: What is in this hadith of mentioning the strike does not oppose the first hadith's mentioning the slap, for a slap may be called a strike, among it Allah's saying: Then his wife came in a cry and struck her face. A slap may cause a blemish in the face which is mutilation of the slapped. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left uncovering that before the slavegirl's presence to know he had caused in her face what is mutilation, then freed her or judged her emancipation upon her master who did that, we understood thereby that his mutilating her does not entail her emancipation upon him, as those we mentioned in this chapter say."
"6261","5334 - We found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us. 5335 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, then each of Yazid and Ibrahim said in his hadith, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: He asked me my name, I said: Shu'bah, so he said: Abu Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: A man slapped the face of a servant of his before Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, so Suwayd said: Do you not know the image is inviolable? I saw myself as seventh of seven brothers with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we had but one servant, and one of us slapped his face, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to free him. He said: In the Messenger ordering him to free him is proof emancipation was not due before that by his slapping, which may cause mutilation in his face. We found from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering this chapter what is more indicative of negating emancipation by the act we mentioned, namely."
"6262","5336 - What Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Firas, from Abu Salih - Abu Ja'far said: his name is Maysarah, one of the imams of Kufah - from Zadhadh, he said: I was with Ibn Umar, and he called a slave of his and freed him, then lifted something from the ground and said: I have in it no reward weighing or equaling this. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever beats his slave for a prescribed punishment he did not commit, its expiation is his emancipation."
"6263","5337 - We found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Firas, from Abu Salih, from Zadhadh, he said: I was with Abdullah ibn Umar, and he had freed a slave of his, and took a stick from the ground and said: I have in it no reward equaling this. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever slaps his slave or beats him for a prescribed punishment he did not commit, its expiation is to free him. Abu Ja'far said: Beating for a prescribed punishment is among the most exemplary mutilations and chastisements Allah made among punishments of offenders entailing its like, yet He did not make one who does that to his slave have his slave freed upon him, by His saying: Its expiation is to free him, while before freeing he is a slave. In what we mentioned is established proof for one negating emancipation by the mutilation we described against one affirming it, and we ask Allah for success."
"6264","Chapter 853 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the greatest of sins. 5338 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash and Mansur narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which sins are greatest? He said: That you set a rival to your Creator while He created you, that you kill your child fearing he eats with you, and that you commit adultery with your neighbor's wife. Then the Qur'an came confirming the Messenger's saying: And those who do not call upon another god with Allah, nor kill the soul Allah forbade except by right, nor commit adultery. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Bakkar narrated this hadith without ordering some of these sins over others."
"6265","5339 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, they said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which sin is greatest? He said: That you set a rival to your Creator, mighty and majestic, though He created you. He said: I said: Then which? He said: That you kill your child fearing he eats with you. He said: I said: Then which? He said: Then that you commit adultery with your neighbor's wife. He said: Then the Qur'an came confirming the Prophet's saying: And those who do not call upon another god with Allah, nor kill the soul Allah forbade except by right."
"6266","5340 - Yazid also narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur and Wasil al-Ahdab and al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, and he mentioned the like."
"6267","5341 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, which sin is greatest before Allah, mighty and majestic? Then he mentioned like Sufyan's hadith from al-A'mash. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the greatest sins are shirk with Allah, then a man killing his child fearing he eats with him, then his adultery with his neighbor's wife. We had mentioned earlier in this book the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The greatest major sins are shirk with Allah, then disobedience to parents, then false testimony. An objector said: These two hadiths contradict. Our answer by Allah's success: There is no contradiction from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); rather in them is the Messenger's answer to what he was asked among mentioned things, and both Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and Abdullah ibn Amr preserved that the greatest sin is one setting a rival to Allah while He created him, reasonably no sin greater. Then he was asked about the next sin: Ibn Mas'ud preserved his answer: That you kill your child fearing he eats with you, while Ibn Amr preserved: Then disobedience to parents. We understood killing a soul Allah forbade without right is greater than disobedience to parents, so what Ibn Mas'ud preserved is what was his answer, especially as the killing mentioned is a man killing his child whose provision and clothing Allah placed upon him for his growth, unlike others without paternity, so it is among the greatest killing, and other killing is below it. Then he was asked about the next sin: what Ibn Mas'ud preserved is a man's adultery with his neighbor's wife, while what Abdullah ibn Amr preserved is false testimony. We understood adultery is greater than false testimony, especially with the neighbor's wife, for upon him is greater guarding his neighbor and not transgressing to his dislike than for others. Thus what Ibn Mas'ud narrated as his answer to what he was asked is weightier of the two answers in the two hadiths. What we established authenticating both: the greatest mentioned sins are shirk with Allah, then killing the soul Allah forbade except by right though victims differ, then adultery though adulterers differ. Then after these three, disobedience to parents and false testimony are each in the place mentioned from him from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So these two hadiths have no contradiction from the Messenger, and what the objector thought contradiction is not from the Messenger but from one preserving something his companion fell short of, and we ask Allah for success."
"6268","Chapter 854 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating what is the Sur (Trumpet) Allah mentioned in His Book. 5342 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us. 5343 - Ibn Abi Imran also narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Warkani narrated to us, they said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: How can I enjoy while the Companion of the Horn has seized the Horn, inclined his ear, bent his forehead, waiting when he will be ordered to blow, then blow? They said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall we say? He said: Say: Allah suffices us and excellent is the Trustee, upon Allah we rely. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith Abu Salih took it from Abu Sa'id."
"6269","5344 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Shu'ayb al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. He said: In this hadith Abu Salih's taking it is from Abu Hurayrah, not Abu Sa'id."
"6270","5345 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Imran - who is al-Bariqi - from Atiyyah al-Awfi, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"6271","5346 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. In this hadith Atiyyah's taking it is from Abu Sa'id."
"6272","5347 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Atiyyah, from Ibn Abbas on His saying: When the Trumpet is blown: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How can I enjoy while the Companion of the Horn has seized the Horn, and he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"6273","5348 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan - Malik ibn Isma'il - narrated to us, he said: Dhawwad ibn Ulbah narrated to us, from Atiyyah, from Ibn Abbas - Abu Ghassan said, and others said: from Abu Sa'id - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How can I enjoy, then he mentioned the like. In what we narrated, the Sur is a horn blown into."
"6274","5349 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, he said: Aslam - Abu Ja'far said: he is al-Ijli - narrated to us, from Bishr ibn Shaghaf, narrated to him from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A Bedouin asked him: What is the Sur? He said: A horn blown into. Abu Ja'far said: What is in this hadith agrees with the hadiths we narrated before it. We considered what is in Allah's Book of His mentioning the Sur: we found therein His saying in Surah Ya-Sin: And the Trumpet is blown, and behold they hasten from graves to their Lord. In this verse is proof blowing in the Sur returned their souls until they hastened after being dead without souls; it may be the blowing was cause of their souls' return, as people of reports say. As for linguists, among them Abu Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna said, as Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us from al-Masadiri from Abu Ubaydah on: The Day the Trumpet is blown, he said: a group: an image, like their saying surah and suwar. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned in al-Athram's narration in this book: And the Trumpet is blown, plural of image, on the pattern of busrah and busr. What al-Farra mentioned in his book on meanings of the Qur'an: it may be said the Sur is a horn, and said it is plural of images blown into for the dead, and Allah knows best. In the verse of Ya-Sin is proof they were in graves without souls in bodies until Allah returned their souls as He willed. In Surah al-Naml: And the Day the Trumpet is blown, whoever is in heavens and earth shall be terrified except whom Allah wills. In this verse that blowing was while alive, so they died thereby. Likewise in Surah al-Zumar: And the Trumpet is blown, and whoever is in heavens and earth shall fall down except whom Allah wills, then He said: Then it is blown another time. That proves the blown-into is one thing, not different things, proving correct what people of reports said in this chapter. Of the Qur'anic verses we recited, some prove people were dead then souls returned, namely Ya-Sin, and some prove they were alive then died, namely al-Naml and al-Zumar. There is narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the meaning we inferred from these two surahs."
"6275","5350 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prefer me over Moses, for people shall fall down on Resurrection Day, and I shall be first to recover, and behold Moses (peace be upon him) holding the side of the Throne. I do not know: did he fall among those who fell and recover before me, or was he among those Allah excepted."
"6276","5351 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Trumpet is blown, and whoever is in heavens and earth falls down except whom Allah wills, then it is blown another time, and I shall be first to raise my head, and behold Moses (peace be upon him) holding a leg of the Throne's legs. I do not know: was he among those Allah excepted or raised before me. In these two hadiths the blowing in the Sur was while alive, so they died thereby, then Allah revived them with the second blow. What we narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proves the Sur is the mentioned Horn in these reports, not what others meant by images. What we hold, and Allah knows best, is that what we mentioned of the Sur upon the verse of Ya-Sin is because those blown into were dead, so spirit was blown into them; and what is in the other two is blowing while people alive, so they died. That is impossible to mean images. Allah knows best what He intended in His Book and on His Messenger's tongue, and we ask Allah for success."
"6277","Chapter 855 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his writing concerning the sea of Aylah to its king. 5352 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Abdullah al-Hammal narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Abbas ibn Sahl, from Abu Humayd, he said: We went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Tabuk until we came to Wadi al-Qura, and the king of Aylah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), gifted him a white mule, so the Messenger clothed him with a cloak and wrote for him concerning their sea. An objector said: What is the meaning of the Prophet's writing concerning the sea of Aylah to its king? Our answer by Allah's success: The sea intended may be expanse including sea-water and else, as linguists say of bahr; water-seas are called bihar for their vastness. Until they say when a place widens with water entering: the place has become sea-like. And they say: So-and-so expanded in knowledge. And baharta a thing: you split it; baharta a camel: you slit its ear lengthwise; hence bahirah Allah mentioned for its slit ear. And hence the Prophet's saying on the horse he rode for Abu Talhah: It is a sea, we found it a sea. And hence Jabir ibn Zayd's saying: But the sea refused - meaning Ibn Abbas - for his vastness in the meaning he said it. Then we sought the Messenger's writing therein, how it was, to know intended meanings if Allah wills."
"6278","5353 - We found Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to us narrating from Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam, from Uthman ibn Salih, from Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Aylah: In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Merciful, this is a security from Allah and Muhammad the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Yuhannah ibn Ru'bah and the people of Aylah for their ships and caravans, their sea and land: the protection of Allah and of Muhammad the Prophet, and for whoever is with them of every passer among people of Sham, Yemen and sea-folk. Whoever commits an offense, his wealth shall not shield his person, and he is lawful prize for whoever seizes him among people. It is not lawful to bar water they come to nor a road they use by land or sea. This is the writing of Juhaym ibn al-Salt. We found Muhammad ibn Uzayz ibn Abdullah al-Ayli mentioned that the writing the Prophet wrote to Yuhannah and Aylah, taken elder from elder, we took from Muhammad ibn Uzayz: In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Merciful, this is a security from Allah and Muhammad the Prophet, Messenger of Allah, to Yuhannah ibn Ru'bah and the people of Aylah, their ships and caravans in sea and land: for them is Allah's protection and Muhammad the Prophet's, and for whoever is with them of every passer of Yemen and sea. Whoever of them offends, his wealth shall not shield his person, and he is lawful prize for whoever seizes him, and it is not lawful to bar water they come to nor a road they use by sea or land. This is the writing of Juhaym ibn al-Salt and Shurahbil. By these two narrations we knew how the Messenger's writing in that meaning was. Then we considered why he wrote it: we found comers from Yemen and Sham upon other than Islam, of shirk, Christianity and Judaism, and Muslims meeting them in those places could take them as spoils, as we spoil enemy-folk found in our lands entering without security. So the Messenger by what he wrote made them contrary to that rule, making them safe in persons and wealth when entering these places. That was greatest benefit to those written for, for they bring them food they live on and other useful things, especially as Aylah has no crops. If one says: Were they tithed as enemy traders entering Islam's abode with security with wealth for trade? Our answer by Allah's success: They may have been tithed like other war-abode traders entering with security, or the Messenger may have lifted it to encourage carrying to that place, as Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, lightened on those coming to Madinah from Sham with trade, reducing them from a tenth to half a tenth to cause carrying to Madinah. We shall mention what was narrated from the Messenger obliging tithing war-folk for trade entering Islam's abode, and from his Companions, later in this book if Allah wills, and we ask Allah for success."
"6279","Chapter 856 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a mouse dying in ghee, whether benefiting thereby is lawful. 5354 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He was asked about a mouse falling in ghee. He said: If solid, take it and what is around it and cast it; if melted or liquid, use it for lamps or benefit thereby. In this hadith is the Messenger permitting lamp-use or benefit from impure ghee. We know none whose narration is proof who narrated in this meaning a hadith clarifying this as Ma'mar did. An objector said: Muhammad ibn Dinar al-Tahi narrated this hadith from Ma'mar with other wording, and he mentioned."
"6280","5355 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Dinar al-Tahi narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a mouse falling in ghee. He said: If liquid it is poured out, and if solid it and what is around are taken and the rest eaten. Our answer by Allah's success: Each of Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad and Muhammad ibn Dinar if alone in a hadith would be accepted, and if such a one is alone in an addition it is accepted. He said: This hadith was narrated from al-Zuhri by other than Ma'mar, namely Ibn Uyaynah and Malik, who opposed Ma'mar in chain and text, and he mentioned."
"6281","5356 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymunah wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a mouse falling in ghee and dying. He said: Cast it and what is around it, and eat it."
"6282","5357 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Maymunah, without mentioning Ibn Abbas in his hadith."
"6283","5358 - And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri: that Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed him: that Ibn Abbas informed him: that Maymunah asked the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, a mouse fell in ghee and died. He said: Take it and what is around it of ghee and cast it."
"6284","5359 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Malik and Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymunah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. Our answer by Allah's success: al-Zuhri may have had in this chapter from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib what Ma'mar narrated from him, and from Ubaydullah what Ibn Uyaynah and Malik narrated, so we do not make one narration repel the other, but authenticate both and act by both. This objector said: You narrate from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibition of what this hadith from Ma'mar permits of lamp-use with what it permits."
"6285","5360 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. 5361 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, they said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah: that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood in the year of the Conquest and said: Allah has forbidden sale of wine, idols, carrion and swine. Some Muslims said to him: What do you see about carrion fats used to grease ships and skins, and people use for lamps? He said: It is unlawful. May Allah fight the Jews; when fats were forbidden to them they melted them, sold them and ate their price. He said: In this hadith is the Messenger forbidding lamp-use with carrion fats, and no difference between carrion fats and ghee mixed with carrion. Our answer by Allah's success: What is in the first section from Ma'mar's hadith and in this second section from Jabir's hadith differ; for Jabir's hadith is on carrion fats themselves unlawful, and their fats likewise, and benefiting from the unlawful is not lawful. What is in Ma'mar's hadith in the first section is benefiting from impure ghee; for impure things may be benefited from, like impure clothes whose impurity does not bar wearing or sleeping in when dry touching no bodies. Likewise impure ghee may be benefited from, since it is not carrion itself, though what made it impure is carrion, so both hadiths we narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand without contradiction. This meaning on impure ghee is narrated from more than one Companion."
"6286","As Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Ata - meaning Ibn al-Sa'ib - from Maysarah and Zadhadh, from Ali - peace be upon him - he said: When a mouse falls in ghee while solid, cast it and what is around it of ghee, then eat it; if the ghee is melted, take it and cast it, and benefit thereby for lamps, and do not eat it."
"6287","And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Masruq, from Abdullah on a mouse falling in ghee, he said: If solid, it and what is around are cast; if melted, it is used for lamps."
"6288","And as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar on a mouse dying in fat: he permitted it for lamps."
"6289","And as Yahya narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Ali ibn Thabit, from Nafi' from Ibn Umar on a mouse dying in oil, and he ordered them to use it for lamps and give it to tanners."
"6290","And as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah informed us, from Nafi', from Safiyyah. And as Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi', from Safiyyah: that a mouse fell in broths of Abdullah's family, so Abdullah said: Use it for lamps and grease hides with it."
"6291","And as Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi': that Ibn Umar ordered them to use it for lamps and grease skins with it - meaning concerning a mouse falling in ghee."
"6292","And as Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: that they brought barley-drink and found a dead gecko in it, so Abu Musa said: Do not eat, but sell, and do not sell it to Muslims, but disclose to whom you sell. In Abu Musa's hadith is unrestricted sale. An objector said: Do you permit its sale? Our answer by Allah's success: Since benefiting thereby despite impurity is allowed, like benefiting from clothes despite impurity, and sale of such clothes is allowed, sale of such ghee is allowed. If he says: Clothes may be washed and become pure, while ghee never becomes pure. It is said: Clothes before returning to what you described are like the ghee we mentioned in impurity. We found houses whose outlets' places are impure by what came for their building which cannot be purified, yet that does not bar their sale. So the mentioned ghee is like them. The permissibility of its sale was held by imams al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar."
"6293","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Talhah ibn Abi Sa'id, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from al-Qasim and Salim: that he asked them about oil in which a mouse dies, may it be eaten? They said: No. We said: Shall we sell it? They said: Yes. Then eat its price, but disclose to whom you sell what fell in it. By this saying Abu Hanifah, may Allah be pleased with him, and his companions held in this meaning, and by it we take, and we ask Allah for success."
"6294","Chapter 857 - Clarification of the difficulty in what a ring-wearer should do in ablution for prayer, moving it or otherwise. Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi said. 5362 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Isma'il ibn Kathir, from Asim ibn Laqit ibn Sabirah, Asim informing from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ablution fully, and pass fingers between toes."
"6295","5363 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Kathir, from Asim ibn Laqit ibn Sabirah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. Abu Ja'far said: When passing between toes in prayer ablution despite width between them is recommended for the ablution-performer, then a ring-wearer with narrowness between it and the finger wearing it is more deserving of moving his ring in ablution. It is narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, this school also."
"6296","As Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Tinnisi informed us, Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Khayr, from Abu Tamim al-Jayshani, he said: I and my brothers entered upon Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and on one of them was a ring, so Umar said to him: How is your ablution complete with this upon you, so he removed and cast it. Some scholars did not hold that, among them Malik ibn Anas as Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim related from him. What the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Laqit ibn Sabirah of what we mentioned, and what Umar said after that of what we described, with no known opponent among the Prophet's Companions, prove it, and success is from Allah."
"6297","Chapter 858 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on intercession before Allah on Resurrection Day by Paradise-folk for Fire-folk. 5364 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrating from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Resurrection Day comes Allah gathers Paradise-folk in rows and Fire-folk in rows, and a man from Fire rows looks to a man from Paradise rows and says: O so-and-so, do you not remember the day you did me a favor in the world? Then it is said: Take his hand, admit him to Paradise by Allah's mercy. Anas said: I testify I heard the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith intercession on Resurrection Day may be from those of high ranks before Allah though not prophets, for others of sinners deserving Fire. Reasonably that is only for sinning monotheists, not others besides monotheists. That is not strange of Allah's bounty upon His righteous servants, granting them intercession in what they intercede for; for since they are with Allah in the rank He placed them, though not as prophets' ranks, being saints' ranks, and prophets with high ranks are granted intercession, these by their ranks are also granted intercession, and success is from Allah."
"6298","Chapter 859 - Clarification of the difficulty in what Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar narrated from his father from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a slave shared between partners, one freeing him while solvent for the value of partners' shares, and else of its valuation. 5365 - al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim, from his father: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a slave is between two, and one frees his share, if solvent he is assessed the highest value, and he is freed. Sufyan said: Sometimes Amr said: a just value, neither deficient nor excessive. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the ruling of the freer when solvent of what is taken from him by his freeing, with nothing on the freed slave's ruling when the freer is insolvent."
"6299","5366 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, what remains is assessed from his wealth, if he has wealth reaching the slave's price. Abd al-Razzaq said: I do not know whether from al-Zuhri's saying or in the hadith - meaning his saying: If he has wealth to its end."
"6300","5367 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim - meaning Ibn Rahawayh - informed us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, what remains is assessed from his wealth. al-Zuhri said: If he has wealth reaching its price. Abu Ja'far said: In Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb's hadith is clarity that: if he has wealth reaching its price, is from al-Zuhri's speech, not what Salim narrated from his father from the Prophet. So this hadith returns to the solvent freeing partner's ruling without mention of his ruling when insolvent. This is undisputed among scholars of guarantee upon the solvent freeing partner of the jointly-owned slave. As for insolvent, they differ, and in this hadith is nothing deciding for some against others, and success is from Allah."
"6301","Chapter 860 - Clarification of the difficulty in what Nafi', freedman of Abdullah ibn Umar, narrated from Abdullah ibn Umar from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. 5368 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, Yahya - who is Ibn Sa'id al-Qattan - narrated to us, from Ubaydullah - who is Ibn Umar - Nafi' narrated to me, from Ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, all of him is freed; if the one who freed his share has wealth reaching its price, upon him is all its freeing."
"6302","5369 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Mas'ud informed us, Khalid - who is Ibn al-Harith - narrated to us, Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever had a share in a slave and freed him, all of him is freed; if he has wealth, a just value is assessed upon him in his wealth, and if he has no wealth, then freed of him is what is freed. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the Messenger informing that the slave is wholly freed by the freeing of the one who freed him, though owning only part not all. What is therein besides of what is due upon him by solvency is additional, separate from that. Therein is no mention of the slave's ruling when his freer owning part not the rest is insolvent, how it is. Some people held it is upon him therein though insolvent, as when solvent. Such holders hold insolvency does not bar offenders from what is due upon them by offenses in insolvency: what they damaged is assessed for owners, and their rulings therein in insolvency are as in solvency, except taking in insolvency is lifted for inability, not else of what they are taken with in solvency. Among what they argued for that is what is narrated from Ibn Umar besides Nafi's hadith."
"6303","5370 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas - meaning Sallam ibn Sulaym al-Hanafi - narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a portion of his in a slave, he guarantees his partners their share."
"6304","5371 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Hannad ibn al-Sari informed us, from Abu al-Ahwas, then he mentioned with his chain the like, except he said: He guarantees his companions their shares."
"6305","5372 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Hilal ibn al-Ala al-Raqqi informed me, al-Husayn ibn Ayyash - meaning al-Bajudda'i - narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay' informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar and Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Umar, I said: From the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. He said: Whoever frees a freeing in which is a partner, its completion is upon the one who freed it. Abu Ja'far said: Abu al-Ahwas and Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah differed over Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay' in this hadith's chain, each narrating from him as we mentioned, and Allah knows the truth therein. Among proof against holders of this saying in their opposition: what is in this hadith of what its narrator preserved from Ibn Umar from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may be preserved on his ruling when solvent, not when insolvent. In Ubaydullah ibn Umar's hadith from Nafi' we mentioned is mention of that ruling in freeing also, and mention of the due after in the freer's solvency. Weightier is to authenticate both hadiths and make them on what is due upon the freer in solvency, not else. Then we considered Ubaydullah's hadith from Nafi' besides Yahya and Khalid's from him, how it is."
"6306","5373 - We found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah and Ibn Numayr narrated to us, they said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, upon him is all its freeing if he has wealth reaching its price; if he has no wealth, a just value is assessed upon the freer, and freed of him is what is freed. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is Ibn Umar informing from the Messenger that what is due upon the freer of what he mentioned is when he has wealth reaching its price. That may be what Nafi' preserved from Abdullah ibn Umar, falling short of what those narrating from Ibn Umar without that preserved. Then we considered this hadith: how did others besides those we mentioned narrate it from Nafi'?"
"6307","5374 - We found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, or a share of his in a servant, and had wealth reaching its value at just value, then he is freed. Nafi' said: Otherwise freed of him is what is freed. Ayyub said: I do not know, is it something Nafi' said or in the hadith?"
"6308","5375 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi informed us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever frees a portion of his in a slave, and had wealth reaching its price at just value, then he is freed, and sometimes he said: If he has no wealth, then freed of him is what is freed, and sometimes he did not say it. My strongest thought is it is something Nafi' says from himself."
"6309","5376 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Zurarah informed us, Isma'il - meaning Ibn Ullayah - informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever frees a share of his - or said: a portion or share - in a servant, and had wealth reaching its price at just value, then he is freed, otherwise freed of him is what is freed. Ayyub said: Sometimes Nafi' said this hadith, and sometimes he did not say it, so I do not know: is it in the hadith or Nafi' said it from himself? - meaning his saying: Freed of him is what is freed."
"6310","5377 - We found Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, and had wealth reaching its price at just value, then he is freed from his wealth. Abu Ja'far said: What Ayyub narrated from Nafi' in this hadith is that the guarantee due upon the mentioned freer is when he has wealth reaching its price, not others among freers in such who own not what reaches its price. Then we considered this hadith, how others besides those we mentioned narrated it from Nafi'?"
"6311","5378 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Nafi', from Abdullah ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, and had wealth reaching the slave's price, a just value is assessed upon him, his partners are given their shares, and the slave is freed upon him; otherwise freed upon him is what is freed. What is in this hadith also proves the mentioned guarantee upon the freer is when solvent, with nothing on his ruling when insolvent. If one says: In it is: otherwise freed upon him is what is freed, proving when insolvent only what he owned is freed. Our answer: That is not in the hadith as stated; therein is only: otherwise freed upon him is what is freed, which may be the whole slave or else, as in Yahya al-Qattan's hadith from Ubaydullah from Nafi': He is wholly freed, then followed by: If the freer has wealth reaching its price, upon him is all its freeing. This proves the slave is wholly freed by one owner's freeing, and the ruling after is upon what was freed, possibly all or part. It confirms the intended guarantee is upon the owner of wealth reaching the price, not others, as in Salim's hadith from Ibn Umar from the Prophet in the prior chapter: When a slave is between two and one frees his share, if solvent he is assessed the highest value and freed. That shows no mentioned ruling for the freed slave when his freer is otherwise than solvent. Then an objector said: There is narrated from Ibn Umar what proves when insolvent the un-freed part remains enslaved, and he mentioned."
"6312","5379 - What Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Marzuq al-Ka'bi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah and Ubaydullah ibn Umar and Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said on a slave between two partners when one frees: The Messenger said: A just value is assessed upon him in his wealth, and he is freed upon him; if his wealth has not what frees him free, he said: Freed of him is what is freed, and enslaved of him is what remains enslaved. Our answer: This addition is found only herein. We mentioned it from Yahya al-Qattan, Khalid ibn al-Harith and others from Ubaydullah from Nafi' without that; they are the proof in such over Yahya ibn Ayyub, and this hadith from Yahya ibn Ayyub is only via Isma'il ibn Marzuq, whose narration is not decisive herein. The objector said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated it from Yahya ibn Sa'id from Nafi' as from Ubaydullah. Our answer: We found it from Yahya ibn Sa'id via one in memory and precision unlike Yahya ibn Ayyub, namely Hushaym ibn Bashir al-Wasiti."
"6313","5380 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur informed us, Hushaym narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man having a share in a slave and freeing his share, upon him is completing its freeing at just value. This hadith from Yahya ibn Sa'id as Hushaym narrated, with his ضبط and precision unlike Yahya ibn Ayyub, has no mention of the slave's ruling when his freer owns not wealth to assess the rest. We mentioned this hadith from Nafi' from Ibn Umar via the three proof-men in Nafi': Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Umari, Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani and Malik ibn Anas, leaving others since theirs returns to like theirs. Then we sought the due in such freed slave, how it is."
"6314","We found Isma'il ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb al-Mula'i narrated to us, from Abu Khalid - who is Yazid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dalani - from Ibrahim al-Sa'igh - who is Ibn Maymun - from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: Two men shared a slave, and one freed his share. He said: If he had wealth he freed half the slave, and wala' was his; if he had no wealth, the slave labored for the rest of the value, and they shared in wala'. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith is undisputed in chain soundness, for it turns on Abu Khalid al-Dalani, a proof in narration, an imam in his land, and on Ibrahim al-Sa'igh, an imam of Khurasan. For what we authenticated of reports from Ibn Umar, the acted-upon is: the whole slave is freed by one owner's freeing whether solvent or insolvent, with guarantee of partners' shares' value after if solvent, and the slave's laboring for partners' shares' values if insolvent. What others besides Abdullah ibn Umar narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) strengthens that the whole slave must be freed by one owner's freeing."
"6315","5381 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih - meaning Ibn Usamah al-Hudhali - from his father: A man freed a portion of his in a slave, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed all of it upon him, and said: Allah has no partner."
"6316","5382 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is proof that when part of the slave becomes Allah's by one's freeing, the shares of other owners before that cease from him and complete for Allah, mighty and majestic. That proves what we authenticated of what we narrated before in this chapter. An objector said: This hadith was raised from Abu al-Malih from his father only by Hammam ibn Yahya; Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah and Hisham al-Dastuwa'i narrated it halted upon Abu al-Malih, not reaching his father, and he mentioned."
"6317","5383 - What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'ammal ibn Hisham narrated to us, Isma'il - meaning Ibn Ullayah - narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Abu Malih: A man freed a portion of his in a slave, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made its release in his wealth, and said: Allah has no partner, mighty and majestic."
"6318","5384 - And what Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, Abu Amir narrated to me, Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih: A man freed a portion in a slave, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is freed from his wealth if he has wealth, and said: Allah has no partner. Our answer: Sa'id and Hisham narrated this hadith from Qatadah as stated, and Hammam added over them from Qatadah what he added; Hammam if alone in a hadith is trusted, his narration accepted, and so in an addition. The objector said: In this hadith: He is freed from his wealth if he has wealth, Allah has no partner, proving such freeing and devotion to Allah is only with wealth, otherwise contrary. Our answer: The hadith has no such proof; therein is the slave's freeing from his freer's wealth if he has wealth, with nothing barring his freeing from other than his freer's wealth if the freer has no wealth. This is weightiest to avoid contradicting others we mentioned in this chapter. Then we returned to the regional muftis on such slave when his freer is insolvent: some said the slave is wholly free by his owner's freeing, and upon the slave to labor for non-freers and pay them; among them Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan among many Kufans. Some said freed of the slave is what was freed, and its holder remains upon his share: if he frees, it is free, and the slave becomes free by the later freeing after the first; and if he wishes he makes the slave labor for his share's value until paid; among them Abu Hanifah, arguing with what was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, therein as."
"6319","Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: We had a boy who witnessed al-Qadisiyyah and proved himself, shared between me, my mother and my brother al-Aswad; they wanted to free him while I was small, so al-Aswad mentioned it to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Free you; when Abd al-Rahman reaches, if he desires as you desired he frees, otherwise he guarantees you. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith is sound in chain, clear in meaning, except what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposing it of what we mentioned is weightier. Some said freed is the freer's share, the rest remains owned as before; among them Malik and al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on them, among many Hijazis. What we authenticated of Ibn Umar's hadith is weightier. As for what we mentioned in Ibrahim al-Sa'igh's hadith that the slave's wala' when his freer is insolvent is for his freer and those he labors for, all we mentioned reject it and make wala' for his freer alone except Abu Hanifah, who made wala' so as in this Ibrahim hadith. Our saying therein is his opponents' saying, for the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala' is for the freer. This slave was freed wholly, or freed what was freed, by his owner's freeing, not by labor performed. So reasonably its wala' is for the one through whom freeing entered, not others. Especially as we mentioned in Yahya al-Qattan's hadith from Ubaydullah from Nafi' from Ibn Umar from the Messenger: the slave is wholly freed by his owner's freeing. When so, reasonably bondage ceased by that freeing, with no freeing after by an owner of part nor by labor for non-freers. The saying that part is freed and the rest owned, with earnings one day for himself by freeing and another day for the rest-owner, is not required by reason; for the slave working for himself earns wholly of partly-owned and partly-otherwise, so reasonably all he earns returns to the ruling of his whole state, partly unowned by non-freers and partly unfree by remaining ownership. Both shares' owned is upon both rulings, not one share alone. Do you not see if one injures such a one entailing compensation, it is not confined to the day's ruling but is for himself by freeing and for the rest-owner by bondage. Do you not see if in the slave's place were a slavegirl married for a dower with her consent and the rest-owner's leave: the dower in their saying returns to her freeing and bondage, not the day she uses herself in freedom or otherwise. When injury compensations and marriage dowers are as we stated, referred to the entitled's rulings not the days' rulings of freeing and bondage, likewise earnings return to his freeing and bondage rulings, not the days earned in, upon the holders' way. In that is proof negating what they said, and in negating it is its opposite established. Ibn Abi Layla and Ibn Shubrumah had said similar, and Allah knows best."
"6320","Chapter 861 - Clarification of the difficulty in what Abu Hurayrah narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. 5385 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nuhayk, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever frees a share or portion of his in an owned slave, upon him is all its release in his wealth; if he has no wealth, the slave is made to labor without hardship upon him."
"6321","5386 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali al-Jahdami informed us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nuhayk, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"6322","5387 - Ahmad narrated to us, al-Mu'ammal ibn Hisham informed us, Isma'il - meaning Ibn Ullayah - narrated to us, from Sa'id, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"6323","5388 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah and Yahya ibn Subayh, from Qatadah, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"6324","5389 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Qatadah, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"6325","5390 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid al-Attar narrated to us, from Qatadah, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"6326","5391 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Qatadah, then he mentioned with his chain the like. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the obligation of what we authenticated of Ibn Umar's hadith we narrated in the prior chapter. An objector said: Shu'bah and Hisham narrated in this chapter from Qatadah without mentioning laboring, and he mentioned."
"6327","5392 - What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, they said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far Ghundar narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nuhayk, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a slave between two men when one frees his share, he said: He guarantees."
"6328","5393 - And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed me, Abu Amir narrated to us, from Hisham, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr, from Bashir ibn Nuhayk, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, he is freed from his wealth if he has wealth. This objector said: This is the origin of this hadith with no mention of laboring. Our answer: What is in these two hadiths does not oppose the first narrations from Qatadah, but is shortcoming of Shu'bah and Hisham from what Sa'id and those we mentioned with him preserved from Qatadah. One who preserves is weightier than one who falls short. Sa'id is most attached to Qatadah and most preserving of his hadith, unsurpassed before his confusion; his hadith taken before confusion is what Yazid ibn Zuray' and likes narrate from him, and they are the proof. An objector said: Hammam narrated this hadith from Qatadah opposing those narrators, and he mentioned 5394 - what."
"6329","5394 - More than one of our companions narrated to me, among them Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ash'ath al-Kufi, they said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nuhayk, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: A man freed a portion of his in a slave, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fined him the rest of its price. He said: Qatadah used to say: If no wealth, the slave labors. He said: In this hadith the laboring is from Qatadah's saying, not the hadith itself. Our answer: That does not oppose the prior hadiths; for this hadith mentions a judgment of the Messenger upon a share-freer with the guarantee he judged, while the first hadiths mention the Messenger's statement of the due upon the freer if solvent and upon the slave if insolvent - two distinct meanings. Weightiest is to carry what a trustworthy narrator narrates upon agreement by authentication, not opposition. Qatadah had by this chain two hadiths: one with the Messenger's statement as Sa'id and agreers narrated, the other with a judgment as Hammam narrated, each in other than its fellow's meaning. What Hammam related from Qatadah of laboring is Qatadah's saying, taken from the other hadith Sa'id narrated, so all reports agree without repelling. How may one leave what Sa'id, Yahya ibn Subayh, Jarir ibn Hazim, al-Hajjaj ibn Artah and Aban ibn Yazid narrated from Qatadah, with Ma'mar ibn Rashid agreeing though shortening its chain dropping a man, and with others' agreement in large number, for what fewer narrated, though not opposing but shortening. One who does not shorten is worthier of acceptance than one who shortens, and success is from Allah."
"6330","Chapter 862 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his intent by his saying: No child shall repay his parent except he finds him enslaved, buys and frees him. 5395 - Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No child repays his parent except he finds him enslaved, buys and frees him."
"6331","5396 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, Yahya ibn Isa informed us, and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, they said: Sufyan - meaning al-Thawri - narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"6332","5397 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah informed us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. An objector said: This hadith proves a man may be his son's slave; for therein: except he finds him enslaved, buys and frees him, proving after owning him he is his slave until he frees him. This is a saying we know none among regional muftis, nor Companions nor Followers, said. In our view the Messenger's saying: except he finds him enslaved, buys and frees him, is other than the objector fancied: and he frees him, meaning he frees him by buying him, for he is cause of his freeing - sound used speech. We found in Allah's Book what negates a father's owning his son: His saying: They said the Most Merciful took a son, to His saying: There is none in heavens and earth but comes to the Most Merciful as a servant. That is: had Allah a son, he would not be His servant; for a son is not his father's servant, nor does his ownership fall upon him. When a son is not his father's servant, it is negated Allah has a son, since all in heavens and earth are His servants. When a father's owning his son is negated by sonship, a son's owning his father is likelier negated by fatherhood. This is strengthened by what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): whoever owns a prohibited kin is free."
"6333","5398 - As Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Makhlad al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu Umayr Ibn al-Nahhas narrated to us, Damrah narrated to us. 5399 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Isa ibn Muhammad - meaning Abu Umayr - and Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Damrah, from Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns a prohibited kin is freed."
"6334","5400 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us. 5401 - And as Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns a prohibited kin of his is free. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that whoever owns a prohibited kin is free."
"6335","5402 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns a prohibited kin is free. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith: Whoever owns a prohibited kin is free. It may mean kin among prohibited, and the prior hadith meant kin among prohibited, so both stand without contradiction, returning to: whoever owns a prohibited kin is free. Then we considered: was this hadith narrated in any way thus or not?"
"6336","5403 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Sa'id informed us, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal and Qatadah, then he mentioned a word - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb the speaker - meaning: from al-Hasan, from Samurah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns a kin among prohibited is free. By that is established what we authenticated of the two hadiths we mentioned from Samurah hereupon. That strengthened Damrah's hadith from al-Thawri we mentioned in this chapter. Then we considered: was anything narrated therein from any Companion of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"6337","We found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar, he said: Whoever owns a prohibited kin is free. An objector impugned this hadith's chain, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated this hadith from Abu Awanah halted."
"6338","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, without mentioning after him any of Ibrahim or al-Aswad, he said: Umar said: Whoever owns a kin is free. Our answer: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi so narrated from Abu Awanah, while Abu Asim narrated from Abu Awanah as we mentioned; he is precise-preserving, whose addition over a precise-preserver is accepted. Among confirming Abu Asim's narration from Abu Awanah."
"6339","What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: I heard Abu al-Walid - meaning al-Tayalisi - say: I saw in Abu Awanah's book - meaning this hadith - Haddathana al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar, then he mentioned the like - meaning like Abu Asim's hadith. We understood thereby Abu Asim preserved of this hadith's chain from Abu Awanah what Abd al-Rahman did not preserve. One who preserves is weightier than one who falls short."
"6340","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Mustawrid: A man married his brother's son to his slavegirl, and she bore children, then he wanted to enslave her children. His brother's son came to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and said: My uncle married me his slavegirl, and she bore me children, and he wants to enslave my children. Abdullah said: He lied; that is not his. In this hadith is proof Abdullah ibn Mas'ud's school in this meaning was as Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him. We know of no Companion opposing them therein. What comes thus leaves none opposing nor saying otherwise. So held Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri and most Iraqis. Malik ibn Anas held parents must be freed upon their child, brother upon brother, child however low upon its begetter, but not a nephew upon an uncle. Others among them: al-Shafi'i, freed only in a parent however high, a child however low, and mothers however high, but none else. When freeing is established in prohibited kin upon their like kin, it proves prohibited wombs likewise. In that is support for what we carried the Messenger's hadith we began with hereupon, and we ask Allah for success."
"6341","Chapter 863 - Clarification of the difficulty in what A'ishah, Umm Salamah and others narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on reading the Opening of the Book: Master of Judgment Day, or Owner of Judgment Day. 5404 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli narrated to us, Khalid ibn Nizar al-Ayli narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Mabrur, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: People complained to the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of rain drought, so he ordered a pulpit placed, then prayed, and promised people to go out one day. A'ishah said: The Messenger went out when the sun's edge appeared, sat upon the pulpit, praised Allah then said: You complained to me of your lands' barrenness and rain's delay from its season, and Allah ordered you to call Him promising to answer you, then he said: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of worlds, Master of Judgment Day; there is no god but Allah doing what He wills. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the Messenger read in the Opening: Master of Judgment Day, not Owner of Judgment Day."
"6342","5405 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakha'i narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Umm Salamah that the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in her house, reading: In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Merciful, Praise belongs to Allah Lord of worlds, the Most Merciful the Merciful, Owner of Judgment Day, You we worship and You we seek help, Guide us the straight path, the path of those You favored, not of those angered upon nor astray, Amen."
"6343","5406 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us what he mentioned Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated to him, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Umm Salamah wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut his reading: In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Merciful, Praise belongs to Allah Lord of worlds, the Most Merciful the Merciful, Master of Judgment Day."
"6344","5407 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad informed us, Umar ibn Harun narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) read: In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Merciful to its end, counting it with fingers of one hand seven verses: In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Merciful, and he read: Master of Judgment Day, not Owner of Judgment Day. We considered this Umm Salamah hadith's chain and found al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated it from Ibn Abi Mulaykah with an added man between him and Umm Salamah."
"6345","5408 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ya'la - meaning Ibn Mamlak - that he asked Umm Salamah about the Messenger's reading, and she described a distinct letter-by-letter reading. It may be Umm Salamah's describing the Prophet's reading how he read other than this of the Qur'an. So by authenticating what we narrated from her is established no proof in what we narrated from her on whether that letter is Malik or Maliki. This is argued by one narrating this hadith as Hafs and Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated, not as Umar ibn Harun narrated."
"6346","5409 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Harun ibn Musa al-Nahwi narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ibn Abi Husayn or Umm Husayn, from his grandmother Umm Husayn that she prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he read: Owner of Judgment Day, until reaching: nor astray, he said: Amen. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith is among the best narrated in this chapter, for though turning on Isma'il ibn Muslim - who is al-Abdi - he is accepted, firm in narration."
"6347","5410 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Khadr ibn Muhammad ibn Shuja' al-Harrani narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Humaysi, from Malik ibn Dinar, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) read: Owner of Judgment Day. Abu Ja'far said: These reports balanced on Malik and Maliki, neither weightier, so we sought the way from what others besides those we mentioned narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6348","5411 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: that Malik narrated to him, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman: that he heard Abu al-Sa'ib - freedman of Hisham ibn Zuhrah - say: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah, mighty and majestic, said: I divided - meaning prayer - between Me and My servant in halves, half for Me and half for My servant, and for My servant what he asks. The Messenger said: Recite: Praise belongs to Allah Lord of worlds, Allah says: My servant praised Me. The servant says: The Most Merciful the Merciful, Allah says: My servant lauded Me. He says: Master of Judgment Day, Allah says: My servant glorified Me, and this verse is between Me and My servant. The servant says: You we worship and You we seek help, Guide us the straight path, the path of those You favored, not of those angered upon nor astray. These are for My servant, and for My servant what he asks. In this hadith the Messenger read in the Opening: Master of Judgment Day."
"6349","Except we found Qutaybah ibn Sa'id opposed Ibn Wahb from Malik, mentioning Maliki in place of Maliki. 5412 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us, from Malik, then he mentioned like Ibn Wahb's hadith with its chain and text, except he said in place of Master of Judgment Day: Owner. Then we considered this letter in others' narration from al-Ala, how it is."
"6350","5413 - We found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Abu Ghassan - Muhammad ibn Mutarrif - narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like except he said therein: Owner of Judgment Day."
"6351","5414 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus ibn al-Qasim al-Yamami narrated to us, Jahdam ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like, except he said therein: Owner of Judgment Day in place of Master of Judgment Day."
"6352","5415 - We found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abi al-Ta'i, who is al-Himsi, praiseworthy in narration, narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Yahya ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Ibn Thawban narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to me, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub al-Huraqi, from Abu al-Sa'ib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like, except he said: Judgment Day in place of Owner of Judgment Day."
"6353","5416 - We found Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu al-Sa'ib, freedman of Hisham ibn Zuhrah, that Abu Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like, saying therein: Master of Judgment Day in place of Owner of Judgment Day."
"6354","5417 - We found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isma'il al-Wasiti narrated to us, Waki' informed us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he read: Owner of Judgment Day."
"6355","5418 - We found Muhammad ibn Ali also narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he read: Master of Judgment Day. Sufyan and Ibn Fudayl differed from al-A'mash in this letter, each narrating from him as we mentioned in this chapter. We know of nothing narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning of acceptable chains besides what we mentioned except something Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated therein, though hearts have what they have of Ayyub, namely."
"6356","5419 - What Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Anas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar read: Owner of Judgment Day. Abu Ja'far said: The correct in this hadith."
"6357","5420 - What Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Safwan ibn Isa al-Basri and Ibn al-Munkadir informed us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar the like, without mentioning Anas."
"6358","Then we considered what was narrated from Abu Hurayrah, how he read this letter after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to know thereby the correct of what was narrated from him from the Prophet. We found Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah: that he read it: Owner of Judgment Day."
"6359","We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from Sulayman, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah the like. So what was narrated from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was strengthened in hearts by what was narrated from him that he read after him: Owner, not Master. Then we considered what was narrated from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, of what they read after the Prophet; we found nothing from Abu Bakr therein."
"6360","We found from Umar therein what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad that they read: Owner of Judgment Day, and they reported Umar read it Owner of Judgment Day. So it was strengthened in hearts the correct from Umar therein is Owner, not Master. Then we considered the readers' reading of that letter after them, how it was."
"6361","We found Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Sa'id say: I saw none dare ask al-A'mash but two men: Hafs ibn Ghiyath and Abu Mu'awiyah, except I saw Abbad ibn Kathir ask him: O Abu Muhammad, how do you read this letter, Owner or Master? He said: Owner of Judgment Day. Al-A'mash's reading returns to Yahya ibn Waththab, Yahya's to Ubayd ibn Nadlah, Ubayd's to Alqamah, Alqamah's to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud's to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6362","We found Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: al-Ju'fi narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim that he read: Owner of Judgment Day."
"6363","We found Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash said to me: Asim said to me: None among people taught me but Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman had read upon Ali. Asim said: I used to return from Abu Abd al-Rahman and recite to Zirr ibn Hubaysh, and Zirr had read upon Abdullah. He said: I said to Asim: You have made firm. Khalaf said in this narration: Asim read it: Owner of Judgment Day. Khalaf said: And al-A'mash reads it likewise."
"6364","We found Rawh narrated to us, he said: al-Ju'fi narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like, adding therein: Abdullah read upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and before that what he mentioned of Ali's reading, he said: Ali read upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In this al-Ju'fi narration from Abu Bakr from Asim: that he read: Owner of Judgment Day."
"6365","I heard Ibn Abi Imran say: I heard Yahya ibn Aktham say: If reading is taken from soundness of articulation, we know none of soundness like Asim's reading. Then he mentioned this account of his reading upon Abu Abd al-Rahman, Abu Abd al-Rahman's upon Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, Ali's upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his reading upon Zirr ibn Hubaysh, Zirr's upon Abdullah, and Abdullah's upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ja'far said: Asim's reading has also an addition upon this meaning."
"6366","Namely what Ibrahim ibn Ahmad ibn Marwan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Hafs ibn Sulayman, from Asim, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, he said: I recited the reading upon Ali ibn Abi Talib, and Ali ibn Abi Talib recited upon the Prophet, peace be upon him. Then I performed Hajj and met Zayd ibn Thabit and recited the Qur'an to him as I recited to Ali, and he did not differ in a single letter. Then we returned to what others besides Asim read this letter upon: we found Hamzah ibn Habib read it: Master of Judgment Day."
"6367","As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Sulaym ibn Isa narrated to us, from Hamzah, then he mentioned that. In his narration he said: Hamzah said: I read the Qur'an only upon two men: Ibn Abi Layla and al-A'mash; what was of Ibn Abi Layla's letter is upon Ali's letter, and what was of al-A'mash's is upon Abdullah's letter. Ibn Abi Layla's reading he took from his brother Isa, his brother Isa from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, and his father from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. As for Nafi', he read it: Master of Judgment Day also. He read upon a group, among them Abu Ja'far Yazid ibn al-Qa'qa', Abu Ja'far read upon his freedman Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, and his freedman read upon Ubayy ibn Ka'b. Other readers besides those we mentioned of disagreement in this letter are upon like those we mentioned of disagreement. Then we returned to seeking the way by derivation: we found Abu Ubayd mentioned, as Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us from him, that he chose therein: Master of Judgment Day over Owner of Judgment Day, mentioning that Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to him from Harun ibn Musa al-Ataki, he said: Asim al-Jahdari read it without alif, meaning Master of Judgment Day. Harun said: I mentioned that to Abu Amr, and he argued therein against one reading Owner with alif, saying: He must read: Say I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind, Owner of mankind. Abu Amr said: Yes, for agreeing with Asim thereon - do they not read: Exalted is Allah the True Owner. Abu Ubayd said: We choose this reading also, mentioning speech therein: because Master has what Owner has not, for one may be an owner not a king, but none is a king except an owner. They did not differ in reading: Whose is sovereignty today, Allah's the One the Subduer, that it is so. Some arguing for Owner argue with Allah's saying: Say O Allah Owner of sovereignty. Weightiest in our view, and Allah knows best, is to return what Allah named Himself with to what Allah named Himself with: Allah named Himself in His Book with what we recited in: Say I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind, and with what He mentioned in Surah al-Hashr: He is Allah besides whom there is no god, the King the Holy, and with what He mentioned in Surah al-Jumu'ah: Whatever is in heavens and earth glorifies Allah, the King the Holy. So what He named Himself with of what we recited in these verses is weightiest to return the disputed letter of Owner and Master to Master, not Owner, and success is from Allah."
"6368","Chapter 864 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Do not precede me in bowing nor prostration, for what I precede you with when I bow, you catch me with when I rise. 5421 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Abdullah ibn Muhayriz, from Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Do not hasten me to bowing and prostration, for I have become heavy, and whatever I precede you with when I bow, you catch me with when I rise."
"6369","5421m - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Sa'id al-Azdi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Ibn Ajlan informed me, then he mentioned with his chain the like, adding: And whatever I precede you with when I prostrate, you catch me with when I rise."
"6370","5422 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, then he mentioned with his chain the like. In this hadith is proof that when the imam precedes the follower with something of bowing, he makes it up while standing behind the imam. Likewise what was narrated from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6371","5423 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Amir al-Duba'i narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam prostrates before you and rises before you. The Prophet of Allah said: So that for that."
"6372","5424 - And as Ibrahim narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, Hammam, Abu Awanah and Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Qatadah, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"6373","There is narrated the like also from Umar ibn al-Khattab and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud not raised beyond them to the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): what Yunus narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ya'qub ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad al-Zuraqi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: When one of you raises his head while the imam is prostrating, let him prostrate; when the imam raises his head, let him remain as long as he raised."
"6374","And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Ya'qub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad al-Zuraqi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, the like."
"6375","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from one who heard Ya'qub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrating, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad, from his father: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever raises his head before the imam in bowing or prostration, let him replace his head. Abu Ja'far said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b added in this hadith's chain from his father - meaning the father of al-Harith - which is likelier correct, and Allah knows best, for his son al-Harith's narration in people's hands is from Abu Hurayrah."
"6376","And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Hayyan, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: Do not hasten your imams in bowing and prostration; when one of you raises his head before the imam, let him place his head, then remain as long as he raised before him. An objector said: When the follower is ordered with what is in these two hadiths of Umar and Abdullah, he leaves something of standing, so he should be ordered to make it up, yet you do not order him so. Our answer: We found bowing differed from standing in prayer: one coming to the imam while bowing is ordered to say Allah is Most Great then bow with him, without making up what the imam preceded him of the standing before that bowing. When he misses bowing, the rest of that rak'ah of prostration and sitting is not counted, and he must make it up whole with standing, bowing, prostration and sitting. Since standing when wholly missed is not made up, and the bowing done after suffices, likewise what misses our mentioned prayer-performer of the imam's standing by occupying with making up what the imam preceded him of his bowing need not be made up; his bowing with the imam he bowed with and after suffices. That is unlike bowing, which one attains the rak'ah only by attaining it with the imam, and Allah is the Granter of success."
"6377","Chapter 865 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his ordering exaggeration in sniffing water in ablution for prayer unless the ablution-performer is fasting. 5425 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Kathir, from Asim ibn Laqit ibn Sabirah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said to him: Exaggerate in sniffing unless you are fasting."
"6378","5426 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Isma'il ibn Kathir, from Asim ibn Laqit, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"6379","5427 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Kathir, from Asim ibn Laqit, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the Messenger ordered exaggeration in sniffing in ablution for prayer when not fasting, and forbade it when fasting. That proves the exaggeration ordered when not fasting was optional, not obligatory; for had it been obligatory fasting would not lift it. In his forbidding it when fasting is proof it invalidates fasting by water entering thereby from the place reached, causing reaching the user's throat, invalidating his fast, and we ask Allah for success."
"6380","Chapter 867 - Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of displaying takbir in Eid, and in which state it is: on the way to it, or after sitting therein. 5428 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Ishaq ibn Buzruj narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to wear the best we find, to sacrifice the fattest we find; a cow for seven, a camel for ten, and to display takbir while upon us are calmness and dignity. In this hadith is the Messenger ordering display of takbir in Eid without mentioning the state of that takbir, whether on the way to Eid and what else. We considered: do we find therein anything proving the state of that takbir?"
"6381","We found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Aidh ibn Habib narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Said ibn Ashwa, from Hanash ibn al-Mutamir who said: I saw Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when his mule was brought to him on the day of Adha and he rode it, and he kept saying the takbir until he reached the graveyard prayer ground."
"6382","We found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that he used to go out on the day of Fitr and the day of Adha saying the takbir, raising his voice with it until he reached the prayer ground."
"6383","We found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah and Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar, when he left his house for the Eid, would say the takbir until he reached the prayer ground, and he would not go out until the sun had risen."
"6384","We found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim: that Abu Qatadah used to say the takbir on the day of Eid until he reached the prayer ground. Abu Jafar said: So what we narrated from Ali, Ibn Umar and Abu Qatadah about that takbir is that it is on the road to the prayer ground, not elsewhere."
"6385","We found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Awn al-Makhzumi, then al-Umari, narrated to us, al-Amash informed us, from Tamim ibn Salamah who said: Ibn al-Zubayr went out on the day of Eid and did not see them saying the takbir, so he said: What is wrong with them that they do not say the takbir?! By Allah, if they do that, I saw us in an army whose two ends could not be seen, and a man would say the takbir and the one next to him would say the takbir, until the army would shake, and between you and them is like between the lowest earth and the lowest heaven. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith from Ibn al-Zubayr about the takbir on the road to the prayer ground is like what is in the hadith of Ali, Ibn Umar and Abu Qatadah, and in his hadith is his report about that from those before him who were in the rank above the people of the time whom he saw not saying the takbir in it. Then someone said: But there is narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas what contradicts what is in these reports."
"6386","So he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Shubah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, who said: I used to lead Ibn Abbas to the prayer ground and he would hear the people saying the takbir, and he would say: What is wrong with the people, is the imam saying the takbir? I would say: No, and he would say: Are the people mad? Our answer to him about that was: It is possible that the takbir which Ibn Abbas objected to when he heard it was the takbir of those in the prayer ground, and that is not a place of takbir, so he said what he said because of that: that that place is one in which people only say the takbir after entering the Eid prayer, and for the imam's takbir which he says in it from what the people say with his takbir in it, and this is the most fitting interpretation of what was narrated from him, so that it is not outside what we narrated from others in this chapter. Then someone said: But there is narrated from Ibrahim what indicates his dislike of that, so he mentioned."
"6387","What Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Hayy, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai: that he was asked about the takbir on the day of Fitr, and he said: Only the weavers do it. Our answer to him about that was: What we narrated in this chapter from those we narrated it from is more worthy to be followed than what we narrated from Ibrahim contradicting it, even though it is not connected to him in its chain, because Ali ibn Hayy never met him nor heard from him, and there is narrated in the interpretation of the saying of Allah Almighty: And that you magnify Allah for having guided you, what indicates contrary to that from what we mentioned before it in this chapter."
"6388","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Qays who said: I heard Zayd ibn Aslam say about: And complete the number and magnify Allah for having guided you, he said: The takbir on the day of Fitr."
"6389","And it is narrated from Ata ibn Abi Rabah: that the takbir on the Eid is a sunnah. As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Qays al-Dabbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata about the takbir on the day of Eid, he said: It is a sunnah. And in what we mentioned in this chapter from what requires the takbir on the day of Eid on the road to the prayer ground is what must be held to, leaving its contrary, and success is from Allah."
"6390","Chapter 868: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about men wearing leather socks in ihram: is that permitted for them as it is permitted when not in ihram, or permitted for them only when lacking sandals, after cutting them below the ankles? 5429 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah informed us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah who said: Umar heard singing from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, so he came to him in part of the night, then in the morning he saw leather socks on him, and said: Leather socks along with singing?! He said: I wore them with one better than you, meaning the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is the report of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf to Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he wore the leather socks, meaning in ihram, with one better than him, meaning the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Then someone said: There is no proof in this, because he did not report that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had known of that from him and approved it for him, and he said: Like that is what Rifah ibn Rafi al-Ansari mentioned to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about water from water, that they used to have intercourse in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then not perform ghusl if they did not ejaculate, and the saying of Umar to him at that: Did you mention that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he approved it for you? He said: No, so Umar did not consider that anything. Our answer to him about that was: The matter in that is as he mentioned, but we found from Abd al-Rahman about that what indicates that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, knew of that from him and did not object to him in it."
"6391","5430 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, al-Husayn al-Ashqar narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah who said: I went out with Umar to Makkah with a man with us chanting rajaz poetry, and when dawn rose he said to him: Quiet, remember Allah, for dawn has risen, then he turned and saw leather socks on Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf while he was in ihram, and said: Leather socks too while you are in ihram?! He said: I did it with one better than you, with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he did not object to me. So this added meaning in this hadith over the hadith we mentioned before it indicates that the wearing was by Abd al-Rahman in ihram, and that ihram does not prevent people from the like of that in their ihram. And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about that another meaning."
"6392","5431 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Shatha who said: Ibn Abbas informed us that he heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, delivering a sermon at Arafah, saying: Whoever does not find a waist-wrapper should wear trousers, and whoever does not find sandals should wear leather socks. I said: And he did not say: cut them? He said: No."
"6393","5432 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb and Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us. 5433 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar who said: I heard Jabir ibn Zayd say: I heard Ibn Abbas say: I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Arafah say: Whoever does not find a waist-wrapper should wear trousers, and whoever does not find sandals should wear leather socks."
"6394","5434 - And as Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Sufyan, meaning al-Thawri, narrated to me, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it, except that he did not mention Arafah."
"6395","5435 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym informed us, Amr ibn Dinar informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6396","5436 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas who said: I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was delivering a sermon, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6397","5437 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, and he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention: while he was delivering a sermon."
"6398","5438 - And as al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Abu al-Zubayr informed us, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever does not find sandals should wear leather socks, and whoever does not find a waist-wrapper should wear trousers. So in these reports is the permission of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for men to wear leather socks in ihram when they do not find sandals, and there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about that another meaning."
"6399","5439 - As Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked what the pilgrim in ihram wears? He said: He does not wear the shirt nor the turban nor the hooded cloak, nor trousers nor leather socks except if he finds no sandals, and if he finds no sandals then let him cut them until below the ankles."
"6400","5440 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that a man asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what clothes we wear when we enter ihram? He said: Do not wear trousers, nor turbans, nor hooded cloaks, nor leather socks except if one has no sandals, then let him wear leather socks below the ankles."
"6401","5441 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"6402","5442 - And as Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"6403","5443 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"6404","5444 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: Whoever does not find sandals should wear leather socks and cut them from at the ankles. Abu Jafar said: So in these reports: whoever among the male pilgrims in ihram does not find sandals may wear leather socks after cutting them below the ankles. Then someone said: These are contradictory meanings, you have narrated each meaning with the reports you narrated it with, so do you find a way to interpret them so that this contradiction is removed? Our answer to him about that was: The way we found sound for them, and the most fitting way in our view, Allah knows best, is that the ruling of wearing leather socks in ihram for men was at first permitted whether sandals were available or not in ihram, as in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf with which we began in this chapter. Then that was abrogated, so they were forbidden to wear them when sandals were available, and permitted to wear them when sandals were unavailable, as in the two hadiths of Ibn Abbas and Jabir which we mentioned second in this chapter. Then that was abrogated, so wearing them in ihram when sandals are unavailable was permitted after cutting them below the ankles, as in the hadith of Ibn Umar which we mentioned third in this chapter. And this is a chapter of fiqh in which its people differed after agreeing on the abrogation of what is in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman with which we began in this chapter: a group among them held to what is in the two hadiths of Ibn Abbas and Jabir, among them al-Shafii, and some narrated it from al-Thawri; and a group held to what is in the hadith of Ibn Umar, among them Abu Hanifah, Malik ibn Anas and their companions. And the reasoning for that is that when they found wearing leather socks for those who have sandals in ihram forbidden, they considered its ruling when sandals are unavailable, and found the things forbidden in ihram outside cases of necessity, such as wearing the shirt and shaving the hair: whoever is compelled to that, shaving his hair due to ailment or wearing his shirt due to ailment, necessity does not lift from him the expiation that would be on him had those things been from him outside necessity. So they understood by that that necessities which permit forbidden things outside necessity only lift sins, not anything else. Likewise the necessity to wear leather socks when sandals are lacking, permitted in ihram, lifts sins but not the obligatory expiations for them outside necessity. This is the view required by reasoning in this chapter in our view, and we ask Allah for success. And in the hadith of Ibn Umar which we narrated in this chapter, the saying of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: nor leather socks except if one has no sandals, then let him wear leather socks below the ankles, was from him before entering hajj, because in it a man said: O Messenger of Allah, what clothes do we wear when we enter ihram?"
"6405","5445 - Likewise Yazid ibn Sinan narrated it to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Umar ibn Nafi, from his father, from Ibn Umar: that a man asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what clothes we wear when we enter ihram? Then he mentioned the hadith."
"6406","5446 - And likewise Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, Abdah and Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar with these same words."
"6407","5447 - And likewise Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Abu al-Ashath narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar with these same words. So his answer, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was what is in this hadith of his, and what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas which he mentioned from him, as we mentioned from him in this chapter, was from him at Arafah while delivering the sermon to the people there. So it is possible that was from him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, unrestricted, without describing the leather socks with what he described them with in the hadith of Ibn Umar, since he knew they had learned from what was in the hadith of Ibn Umar which leather socks permitted in ihram were; so he had no need to describe them in his sermon at Arafah. And that is like the saying of Allah Almighty in the verse of debt describing witnesses with acceptability, in His saying: From among those you approve of as witnesses, then He mentioned witnesses in other verses of His Book, including His saying: Why did they not bring four witnesses for it, without describing them as He described them in the verse of debt, because what He described them with in the verse of debt suffices, and they understand that the witnesses mentioned in this verse are those mentioned in the verse of debt. Likewise the leather socks in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, mentioned unrestrictedly, are those described in the hadith of Ibn Umar, sufficing without description in the hadith of Ibn Abbas. And the hadith of Jabir, if it is from the sermon of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Arafah, its discussion is like that of Ibn Abbas, and that is the most fitting interpretation so it agrees with the hadith of Ibn Umar and does not contradict it, and success is from Allah."
"6408","Chapter 869: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about water passing over lands, passing over some before others: what is the ruling? And about what their people withhold until it reaches what it reaches, then release it after? 5448 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus ibn Yazid and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam: that he disputed with a man of the Ansar who had been present at Badr with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, over water-channels of the Harrah with which both watered date-palms. He said to the Ansari: Let the water pass through, but he refused him. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Irrigate, O Zubayr, then send the water to your brother or your neighbor. The Ansari became angry and said: O Messenger of Allah, because he is your aunt's son? The face of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, changed color, then he said: O Zubayr, irrigate, then withhold the water until it returns to the embankment. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave Zubayr his full right, whereas before that he had advised Zubayr with a view seeking latitude for him and the Ansari. But when the Ansari angered the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he gave Zubayr his full right in plain judgment. He said: So al-Zubayr said: I do not think this verse was revealed except about that: But no, by your Lord, they will not believe until they make you judge in what disputes arise between them, then find no distress in themselves over what you decide, and submit fully. One of them adds over his companion in the story. Yunus said to us: Ibn Wahb said to us: Al-jadr is the base."
"6409","5449 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, and al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, then they agreed and Ibrahim said: I heard Ibn Shihab, and al-Rabi said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. So in this hadith, when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave Zubayr his full right in plain judgment, he ordered him to withhold the water until it returns to the embankment, then send it to his neighbor. Then someone said: But you narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in his judgment about the valley of Mahzur what contradicts that."
"6410","5450 - So he mentioned what Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi had narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us; and what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki had narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, then they agreed and said: From Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Malik ibn Thalabah, Muhammad ibn Ali said in his hadith: son of Abu Malik, then they agreed and said: From his father who said: Dispute was brought to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about Mahzur, the valley of Banu Qurayzah, and he judged that water is to the ankles, the upper one not withholding from the lower. So in this hadith is the judgment of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to withhold water to the embankment, and these differ. Our answer to him about that is that it is possible the amount of water reaching the ankles is the same as reaches the embankment, so that meaning may be expressed as water reaching the ankles or as reaching the embankment; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mentioned it once this way and once that way. And this is the most fitting interpretation of what is narrated from him here and elsewhere, not as contradiction and conflict, and Allah is the Grantor of success."
"6411","Chapter 870: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about ruqba. 5451 - Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: Do not give umra gifts nor ruqba gifts; whoever is given an umra gift or a ruqba gift, it belongs to the heir when he dies."
"6412","5452 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata who said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No umra and no ruqba; whoever is given an umra gift or a ruqba gift, it is his for his life and his death."
"6413","5453 - And Ahmad narrated to us, Abdah ibn Abd al-Rahim informed us, Waki informed us, from Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Jad, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit who said: I heard Ibn Umar say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade ruqba. He said: And whoever is given a ruqba gift, it is his. So in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the ruqba belongs to the one given it, and the condition stipulated on him in it is void with no effect. This is a question in which scholars differed, and in the nature of the ruqba to which this ruling applies. A group among them said: It is a man saying to a man: I have made this house of mine a ruqba for you; if I die before you it is mine, and if you die before me it is yours. So they made it like a loan for use and did not establish ownership for the recipient in what was given him thus. Among those who said that: Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, holding that view of its nature. And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim reported an answer to Asad when he asked him about Malik's view on it, that Malik did not know it, and he explained it to him like the explanation we mentioned from Abu Hanifah and Muhammad, and he said: There is no good in it. Abu Jafar said: What we mentioned from Abu Hanifah, Malik and Muhammad is not correct in our view, because in it, if the recipient dies, what was given him belongs to the giver; they should have treated that as a bequest to the one given it, since bequests are like that. Another group said about its nature contrary to this, saying: It is a man saying to a man: I have given you ownership of this house of mine on condition we watch in it; if I die before you it returns to me, and if you die before me it is yours. So the watching is about its return to its owner or to the giver, not about ownership itself. They deemed it valid for the recipient, never returning to the giver in any case. Among them: al-Thawri, Abu Yusuf and al-Shafii, and this is the worthier of the two views in our view, Allah knows best."
"6414","Chapter 871: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about umra: its nature and its ruling. 5454 - Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar: that a governor over Madinah called Tariq judged the umra for the heir, on the word of Jabir, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6415","5455 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr who said: I bear witness that I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is given an umra gift, it is his for his life and his death."
"6416","5456 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Aban al-Attar narrated to us, from Yahya, that is Ibn Abi Kathir, Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The umra belongs to the one given it."
"6417","5457 - And Yunus narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr informed us, al-Awzai informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is given an umra gift, it is his and his descendants'; whoever inherits from his descendants inherits it. So in these reports the umra belongs to the one given it in his life and after his death. And scholars differed about the nature of this umra. A group said: It is a man saying to a man: I have given you ownership of this house for the days of your life; so it is his in his life and his heirs' after his death. Among them: Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri and their companions, and al-Shafii. Others said: The umra with this ruling is when a man says: I have given you and your descendants this house as umra; it is his in his life, and if he does not mention and your descendants it returns to the giver after the recipient's death. Among them: Ibn Shihab, Malik and many Medinans. And those holding this view argued."
"6418","With what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: The umra which the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, permitted is to say: It is yours and your descendants'; but if he says: It is yours as long as you live, it returns to its owner. And al-Zuhri used to give fatwa by that. And this hadith in the view of their opponents only indicates the words of al-Zuhri, and Abd al-Razzaq erred in it, making it from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir with those words. The proof is that one more retentive than Abd al-Razzaq, namely Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated it from Mamar differently."
"6419","5458 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that Jabir ibn Abdullah informed him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged that whoever gives a man an umra gift, it belongs to the one given it and his heirs after him. And others besides Mamar narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri with what indicates this meaning too."
"6420","5459 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged for whoever is given an umra gift that it is his and his descendants'; it is his outright, no condition or consent of the giver being valid in it. Abu Salamah said: Because he gave a gift in which inheritances apply, so inheritances cut off his condition."
"6421","5460 - And as Yazid narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any man given an umra gift for himself and his descendants, it belongs to the one given it and does not return to the giver, because he gave a gift in which inheritances apply."
"6422","5461 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6423","5462 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abdullah that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever gives a man an umra gift for himself and his descendants, his word has cut off his right in it, and it belongs to the one given it and his descendants. Then someone said: In this hadith from the narration of Ibn Abi Dhib, Malik and al-Layth, from al-Zuhri: whoever is given an umra gift for himself and his descendants, in it is mention of the umra for descendants, so the hadith of Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar which you mentioned is confirmed. Our answer to him about that was: That does not go beyond our interpretation of the hadith of Mamar, and these words in it are only the words of other than the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. This is shown by what is in the hadith of Ibn Abi Dhib, attributing some of those words to Abu Salamah and excluding them from the words of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. It is also shown by what occurred between Qatadah and al-Zuhri of disagreement in the place where they met, and Qatadah's argument against him with what he argued."
"6424","5463 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hisham said to me: What do you say about umra? I said: Al-Nadr ibn Anas narrated to me, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Umra is valid. Al-Zuhri said: It is not umra until it is made for him and his descendants. He said: Then he said to Ata, meaning Ibn Abi Rabah: What do you say? He said: Jabir narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Umra is valid. Do you not see al-Zuhri's silence when Ata narrated from Jabir what he narrated from him, omitting mention of descendants, and al-Zuhri did not refute him by saying: Abu Salamah narrated to me from Jabir contrary to what you narrate from Jabir? His silence about that proves that mention of descendants is not in the hadith of Jabir from Abu Salamah, just as it is not in the hadith of Jabir from Ata. And Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki narrated this hadith from Jabir explained."
"6425","5464 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Hisham, meaning al-Dastuwai, narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is given an umra gift for his life, it is his for his life and after his death. So that proves the umra narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has no mention of the recipient's descendants, and that it proceeds contrary to the giver's condition, whose condition is as no condition. This is shown by the hadith of Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, which we mentioned in the chapter before this one: whoever gives it for his life, it is his for his life and after his death. Then this Abdullah ibn Umar gave fatwa by that after the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6426","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit who said: I heard Ibn Umar, when a man asked him about a man who gifted a she-camel to a man for his life and it gave birth, he said: It is his with its offspring. He said: Then I asked him after that and he said: It is his alive and dead. Abu Jafar said: And this meaning is understood from the hadith of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, because they agree that if he makes it for him and his descendants and the recipient dies leaving a wife, she inherits from it what a wife inherits from his other wealth, and it is sold to settle his debt if he has debt, and his bequests in it are executed if he bequeathed anything in it. In that is proof that the condition in it is void and not acted upon, for had it been acted upon it would not pass from him to another; and its passing to another in what we mentioned proves it passes from him in all cases. And others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, besides those we mentioned, narrated the validity of umra from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6427","5465 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Muawiyah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Umra is valid for its people."
"6428","5466 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Hujr ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Umra is inheritance."
"6429","5467 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Hujr al-Madari, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The way of umra is the way of inheritance."
"6430","5468 - And as Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Zayd ibn Thabit, without mentioning anyone between them, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is given something as umra for his life, it is his and his heir's."
"6431","5469 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, from Hujr, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged the umra for the heir."
"6432","5470 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Kathir, meaning Ibn Jafar, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No umra; whoever is given something as umra, it is his."
"6433","5471 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samurah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Umra is valid. And in what we mentioned is made clear the correctness of the view of Abu Hanifah, his companions and al-Shafii about umra, refuting their opponents. We mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr from Hisham in this chapter, but omitted to mention al-Thawri alongside him since he narrated it from Hisham, so we needed to mention it here."
"6434","5472 - Namely what Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Keep your wealth to yourselves, do not give umra gifts; whoever is given something as umra, it is his. And we omitted to mention the agreement of Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah with Hisham on that, namely that."
"6435","5473 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Keep your wealth to yourselves, do not squander it; whoever is given an umra gift, it is his alive and dead, and his descendants'. Abu Jafar said: And his descendants are all who succeed him in his wealth by inheritance from him or by his bequest of it to them, and we ask Allah for success."
"6436","Chapter 872: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbidding the pebble sale. 5474 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the pebble sale and the sale involving uncertainty."
"6437","5475 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Said ibn Amr al-Ashathi narrated to us, Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade two sales and two garments: as for the two garments, that a man wraps himself in his garment on one side, and that he squats in a garment with his private part toward the sky, as if meaning exposing his private part to the sky; as for the two sales, throw to me and I throw to you, and throw the stone."
"6438","5476 - And Abu Ayyub Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Imran al-Tabarani, known as Ibn Khalaf, narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"6439","5477 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Hisham, that is Ibn Hassan, narrated to us, from Muhammad, that is Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah who said: Two garments and two sales were forbidden, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Then a questioner asked about the forbidden pebble sale, what is it? Our answer to him was that it was a sale of the pre-Islamic people contracted among them: when one of them wanted to take his companion's garment and own it against compensation, he would throw a pebble or stone on it, becoming entitled to it, and the owner could not prevent him. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade that and returned sale to the choice of the two contracting parties at Allah's revelation to him: O you who believe, do not consume your wealth among yourselves unjustly, except it be trade by mutual consent among you. So Allah returned things to their owners' consent to remove them from ownership to whom they give them, or to keep them for themselves, and informed that whoever proceeds otherwise consumes wealth unjustly, and success is from Allah."
"6440","Chapter 873: Explanation of the problem in what is meant by the saying of Allah Almighty, Woe to those who pray, to the end of the surah in which that is mentioned, through what is narrated of what was said about it in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and what is narrated from his Companions about it. 5478 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us; and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Mualla ibn Mansur al-Razi narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us; and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, then each one of them said in his hadith: From Asim, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah who said: Every good deed is charity; we used to count al-maun in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, as the cooking pot, the bucket and the like. Abu Jafar said: This is among what transmitters include among hadiths attributed to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. And there is narrated from more than one Companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about what is meant in this verse, reports agreeing with this view and disagreeing; among what is narrated about that from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6441","What Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, about His saying: Those who show off and withhold al-maun, he said: They show off with their prayers and withhold the zakah of their wealth. And among what is narrated from Abdullah ibn Masud, in which what they used to say about it in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is not mentioned."
"6442","What Isa narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Ibn Masud who said: It is the loan of household goods: the cooking pot, the axe and the bucket."
"6443","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar who said: A man of Banu Numayr, nicknamed Abu al-Ubaydayn, blind, used to ask Abdullah, and Abdullah would be made known to him; he asked him about al-maun, and he said: Withholding the axe, the cooking pot and the bucket."
"6444","And among what is narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him: what Isa narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, meaning Ibn Abi Yazid, who heard Ibn Abbas say: It is the loan of household goods."
"6445","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that he said about al-maun: What people pass among themselves; and Ali said: It is zakah."
"6446","And what Ibrahim narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: Al-maun is withholding the axe, and what people help one another with."
"6447","And what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas who said: Al-maun is the loan for use. Abu Jafar said: So Abdullah ibn Masud and Abdullah ibn Abbas agreed on what is meant in their interpretation of this verse, and that it is what we mentioned from them in these hadiths of theirs."
"6448","And among what is narrated from Abdullah ibn Umar: what Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Said ibn Ubayd al-Tai, from Ibn Umar who said: It is zakah. So what is narrated about that from Ibn Umar agrees with what is narrated from Ali; and what is narrated from Umm Atiyyah indicates that what was meant by it in their view."
"6449","Namely what Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, Yahya, that is Ibn Said, narrated to us, Jabir ibn al-Subh narrated to us, Umm Sharahil narrated to me, she said: Umm Atiyyah said to me: Go to such-and-such woman, convey salam to her, and say: Umm Atiyyah enjoins you to fear Allah Almighty, so do not withhold al-maun. She said: My lady, what is al-maun? She said: Have you lost your senses! It is the craft people pass among themselves. Abu Jafar said: So this meaning from Umm Atiyyah agrees with what Ibn Masud and Ibn Abbas both held. Abu Jafar said: So we pondered this verse and found those mentioned in it threatened with woe, like those threatened in Surah al-Jathiyah by His saying: Woe to every sinful liar who hears the verses of Allah recited to him, to His saying: So give him tidings of a painful punishment; and like those threatened in Surah Ha-Mim al-Sajdah by His saying: And woe to the polytheists who do not give zakah and disbelieve in the Hereafter; and like those threatened in Surah al-Zukhruf by His saying: So woe to those who wronged from the punishment of a painful day; and like those threatened in Surah al-Tur by His saying: So woe that day to the deniers who play in vanity, the day they are thrust into the fire of Hell. In these verses those threatened with woe are the people of the Fire, so it strengthened in hearts that those threatened in Surah Have you seen are also them. And in Allah's describing them as heedless of their prayers is proof of their hypocrisy and of abandoning them when alone, like those neglectful; whoever is like that is a hypocrite. And where Allah mentioned where hypocrites are, in His saying: The hypocrites are in the lowest depth of the Fire; whoever is like that, zakah of wealth is not sought from him, for Allah made it purification for those taken from, in His saying: Take from their wealth charity to purify and cleanse them, and had it been taken from hypocrites it would not purify nor cleanse them. Then He said: And pray over them, for your prayer is comfort for them; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when believers came with their zakahs would pray over them, as we mentioned from him earlier in this book from Ibn Abi Awfa who said: My father sent me to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with his charity, and he said: O Allah, bless the family of Abu Awfa. And he never prayed over hypocrites. And in what we mentioned is that the interpretation of this verse as Abdullah ibn Masud and Abdullah ibn Abbas interpreted it is worthier than others we mentioned in this chapter, and success is from Allah. And the people of language used to interpret it thus."
"6450","As Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us, al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah about: And they withhold al-maun: in pre-Islamic times every benefit and gift, and in Islam obedience and zakah. Himyan ibn Quhafah said: He does not deprive al-maun from him. Abu Ubaydah said: And I heard a man say: If only we had camped, you would have done with your she-camel a deed giving you al-maun, meaning: she obeys you. And as al-Farra mentioned in his book on the meanings of the Quran, he said: I heard some Arabs say: Al-maun is water, and he recited to me about it. And what we mentioned before this from the people of fiqh and reports in this chapter is worthier, and success is from Allah."
"6451","Chapter 874: Explanation of the problem of what is preferable in mentioning past days of the month: whether by mentioning the elapsed thereof, or by mentioning the lesser of the elapsed and the remaining, through what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then companions. 5479 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus, known as al-Susi, narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Seek it for ten remaining of the month of Ramadan. So in this hadith is mention of the remainder of the month in seeking Laylat al-Qadr in one of the nights of the month sought therein. And some scholars, including Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Lului, would not date by the remainder of the month even if most had passed, since they do not know the amount remaining, arguing with what is narrated from Abdullah ibn Umar about it."
"6452","5480 - As Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah who said: I heard Sad ibn Ubaydah say: Ibn Umar heard a man say: Today is half the month, or tonight is half the month, and said: Woe to you, how do you know? The man said: Today is the fifteenth or tonight is the fifteenth. So Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The month is thus and thus and thus, and he clasped one in the third as if making nine. Abu Jafar said: This was a fine deduction by Abdullah ibn Umar. And the hadith of Aishah with which we began does not depart from this meaning, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, may have been informed by Allah Almighty before that of the remainder of the month, how much it was, so he said that seeking it in a particular month whose remainder was that amount. And this is indicated."
"6453","5481 - By what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muadh ibn Abdullah ibn Khubayb, from his brother Abdullah ibn Abdullah, a man in the time of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abdullah ibn Unays sat with us in the gathering of Juhaynah at the end of Ramadan, and we said to him: O Abu Yahya, did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about this blessed night? He said: Yes, we sat with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at the end of this month, and we said: O Prophet of Allah, when do we seek this blessed night? He said: Seek it tonight, the evening of twenty-three. A man of the people said: Then it is the first of eight. He said: It is not the first of eight, but the first of seven; what do you want with a month that is incomplete? Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is proof he meant a particular month in which he said that, by his saying: what do you want with a month that is incomplete, meaning: other months give the seven what they give in a complete month of thirty, not in months short of thirty. Then someone said: But there is narrated about that from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he mentioned."
"6454","5482 - What Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten: nine remaining, seven remaining, five remaining. So that shows that night is sought in nine remaining, refuting what you mentioned. Our answer to him was: That may have been aimed at a particular month whose exact number he knew, so he said that because of it; and its seeking in other times besides it may be in what may be nine remaining, seven remaining, five remaining, so whoever seeks it in all of them hits its reality in some, and we ask Allah for success."
"6455","Chapter 875: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in his saying to the one who said to him, I have a dinar: Spend it on yourself; and his saying when he said, I have another: Spend it on your child; and his saying when he said, I have another: Spend it on your servant; and his saying when he said, I have another: You know best. 5483 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered charity, and a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a dinar. He said: Spend it on yourself. He said: I have another. He said: Spend it on your wife. He said: I have another. He said: Spend it on your child. He said: I have another. He said: Spend it on your servant. He said: I have another. He said: You know best."
"6456","5484 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, urged charity one day and a man said: I have a dinar. He said: Give it in charity to yourself. He said: I have another. He said: Give it in charity to your child. He said: I have another. He said: Give it in charity to your wife. He said: I have another. He said: Give it in charity to your servant. He said: I have another. He said: You know best."
"6457","5485 - And al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah who said: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, then he mentioned the like of it, except he said in place of you know best, you know better. Then some said, among them Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam: In this is proof that whoever owns four dinars is rich, and charity to him is unlawful, as the Medinans say: whoever owns forty dirhams, charity to him is unlawful. They said: Do you not see he ordered him in the four what he ordered him therein, and ordered him nothing beyond them, referring their disposal to his judgment. We had mentioned this chapter and what is narrated therein earlier in this book, explaining that the worthiest reports for authentication therein is the hadith of Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar, from his father, from a man of Muzaynah, that he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asking him and found him preaching: Whoever seeks sufficiency, Allah suffices him; whoever seeks chastity, Allah keeps him chaste; and whoever asks people while having the equivalent of five uqiyahs asks importunately. We proved its soundness by what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wrote to Muadh ibn Jabal when he sent him to Yemen for charity, to take it from their rich and place it among their poor; so their rich were those taken from, and others not taken from were not rich since it was placed among them. And the hadith of Abu Hurayrah we mentioned in this chapter: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, urged charity on whom he urged it, and he may urge both the rich on whom zakahs are due and others with surplus beyond sustenance though not rich. And of that is what Abu Masud al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6458","5486 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Hurayth informed us, al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, from al-Husayn, that is Ibn Waqid, from Mansur, from Shaqiq, from Abu Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to order us to charity, and none of us would find anything to give until he went to the market and carried on his back, then bring a mudd and give it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. And I know today a man with a hundred thousand who then had not a dirham."
"6459","5487 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, Ghundar narrated to us, from Shubah, from Sulayman, meaning al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abu Masud who said: When we were ordered to charity, we would work as porters and give; Abu Aqil gave a sa, and another man brought more than him. The hypocrites said: Allah has no need of this one's charity, and the other only did it for show. So was revealed: Those who criticize the volunteers among the believers in charities and those who find only their effort."
"6460","5488 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Khalid informed us, Ghundar informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So that shows he used to urge charity on those neither liable to zakah nor rich. And his ordering in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah the man in each of his four dinars to what was worthiest, and referring him in his fifth dinar to what he referred him to, may be because he knew nothing to order him to spend it on, so referred it to himself since he knows his own affairs and obligations better than others. There is in that no proof of his richness by owning four, not being among its people before knowing he owns them; had what stopped him been his richness, informing him of owning four would have stopped him from ordering him in the fourth before informing him of a fifth. And when what those imagined about the hadith of Abu Hurayrah is negated, what we authenticated earlier in this book is established, namely the hadith of Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar, from his father, from al-Muzani, and success is from Allah."
"6461","Chapter 876: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in his saying: A whip's place in Paradise is better than the world and what is in it. 5489 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Mansur ibn Salamah al-Khuzai narrated to us, Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Yazid, Abu Jafar said: that is Ibn al-Had, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A whip's place in Paradise is better than the world and what is in it."
"6462","5490 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6463","5491 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A whip's place in Paradise, or a stick's place in Paradise, is better than the world and what is in it. Then someone said: What benefit is a whip's place in Paradise? Our answer to him was: What is meant, Allah knows best, is a whip's place in Paradise from what Allah Almighty gives His servants thereof in spaciousness; a whip's place of that is better than the world and what is in it. Like people's speech on their tongues, one saying: A span of my house is dearer to me than such-and-such, not meaning that measure alone without the rest of the house, but that measure as part of the house that is his. And Allah's gifts to Paradise's people are vaster; indeed it is narrated that the lowest of Paradise's people in rank is given the like of the world and ten times it."
"6464","5492 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I know the last of Hell's people to leave it and the last of Paradise's people to enter: a man leaves the Fire crawling, and Allah Almighty says: Go enter Paradise. He comes to it and imagines it full, returns and says: My Lord, I found it full. Allah Almighty says: Go enter Paradise, for you have the like of the world and ten times it, or: you have ten times the world. He says: Do You mock me or laugh at me while You are the King? I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, laugh until his molars showed; it used to be said: That man is the lowest of Paradise's people in rank. So we understood from this hadith the spaciousness of Allah's gift to His servants entering Paradise. So what is narrated from him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the two hadiths of Sahl and Abu Hurayrah, we found no interpretation worthier than the one we gave in this chapter, and Allah knows best the intent of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, therein and elsewhere, and success is from Allah."
"6465","Chapter 877: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then companions, about prayer after the Maghrib adhan, of permission and prohibition. 5493 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun and Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, they said: Kahmas ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun said in his hadith: and al-Jurayri, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from Abdullah ibn Mughaffal who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Between every two adhans is a prayer, between every two adhans is a prayer, between every two adhans is a prayer for whoever wishes. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, between every two adhans is a prayer for whoever wishes. Some inferred permission to pray between the Maghrib adhan and its iqamah. We considered whether this hadith proves what they say: we found it says between every two adhans is a prayer, not between every adhan and iqamah is a prayer. That applies to all prayers' calls: between the adhan for Fajr and for Zuhr is a prayer, namely the two rakahs of Fajr and voluntary prayers after prayer becomes lawful between sunrise and Zuhr; between Zuhr's adhan and Asr's is a prayer for whoever wishes; between Asr's adhan and Maghrib's is a prayer before Asr after its adhan; and between Maghrib's adhan and Isha's is a prayer for whoever wishes to volunteer between them. This is the apparent meaning, and whoever claims an inner meaning must prove it. Then we found al-Husayn al-Muallim narrated this hadith from Ibn Buraydah contrary to Kahmas and al-Jurayri."
"6466","5494 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Mamar Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us, Abd al-Warith, that is Ibn Said al-Tannuri, narrated to us, from Husayn, that is al-Muallim, from Ibn Buraydah, Abdullah al-Muzani narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Pray two rakahs before Maghrib, then said: Pray two rakahs before Maghrib, then said at the third: For whoever wishes, lest people take it as sunnah. So in that is the intent of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to order two rakahs after the Maghrib adhan. Then we found Hayyan ibn Ubaydullah, Abu Zuhayr, a man praised in narration, narrated this hadith from Ibn Buraydah, differing from Kahmas, al-Jurayri and al-Husayn al-Muallim in what they narrated from him."
"6467","5495 - As al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Said al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani Abu Salih narrated to us, Hayyan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Buraydah al-Aslami narrated to me, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: At every prayer are two rakahs except the Maghrib prayer. So Hayyan differed from Kahmas, al-Jurayri and al-Husayn in the chain, tracing it to Buraydah, and differed in the text as we mentioned. And the hadith of Hayyan is either about the prayer ordered in the first two hadiths, clarifying which prayer it is, namely other than Maghrib; or it is a different hadith forbidding what that hadith ordered. And when command and prohibition meet, prohibition is worthier, or it abrogates it, the abrogator being worthier. So when these reports are gathered and their meanings uncovered: prohibition of prayer after the Maghrib adhan, not permission. If someone says: But there is narrated from Anas ibn Malik about that."
"6468","5496 - So he mentioned what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad, al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful narrated to us, from Anas who said: We used to pray two rakahs before Maghrib in the life of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. So I said to Anas: Did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, see you? He said: Yes, he saw us and neither ordered nor forbade us."
"6469","5497 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Musab ibn Sulaym who said: I saw Anas ibn Malik pray two rakahs when the sun set before Maghrib. I said to him: Did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, pray them? He said: No. I said: Did he order you with them? He said: No, but the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would see who prayed them and not forbid him. He said: So in these two hadiths is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, seeing those who prayed them and not forbidding them. Our answer to him was that they may have been permitted until abrogated by what is in the hadith of Hayyan from Ibn Buraydah from his father. He said: And there is also narrated from Anas about that."
"6470","5498 - So he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Yala ibn Ata informed me, he said: I heard Abu Fazarah narrate from Anas who said: We used to pray two rakahs before Maghrib in the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6471","5499 - And what Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Amir al-Ansari who said: I heard Anas say: When the call for Maghrib was made, the elder Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would hasten to the pillars, praying two rakahs."
"6472","5500 - And what al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ali ibn Zayd who said: I heard Anas say: The muadhdhin would call in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and we thought it the iqamah from the many standing to pray two rakahs before Maghrib."
"6473","5501 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the muadhdhin called, the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would hasten to the pillars to pray behind them two rakahs before Maghrib. Abu Jafar said: The discussion here is like before. If someone says: But there is narrated from more than one Companion that they prayed them after the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6474","So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr, that Ubayy ibn Kab and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf used to pray two rakahs two rakahs before Maghrib. Our answer to him was that they may not have known the abrogation which Buraydah knew, so remained on the abrogated ruling they knew; and whoever knew otherwise is worthier than who fell short. If he says: Can knowledge of this escape such eminent men? Our answer: It may escape them despite eminence, as the abrogation of tatbiq in prayer escaped Abdullah ibn Masud despite eminence until he held to it till death, and as the permission of sacrificial meat after three days escaped Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, who held to the first ruling. We shall bring that later in this book, Allah willing. And some imagined it is narrated from Ali that he knew the abrogation through the hadith of al-Nabighah ibn Mukhariq, but that is nothing in our view, for it revolves on Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, from Rabiah ibn al-Nabighah, from his father, and Rabiah and his father are unknown, not known among transmitters. And what is sound from Ali is what he addressed when he led people while Uthman was besieged: he ordered them with this and forbade eating their sacrifices beyond three days. And the like is much; what we brought suffices. And there is narrated from Said ibn al-Musayyab, despite the many Companions he saw, his adherence to the mosque of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his eminence in knowledge, what contradicts the reports from the Companions about this prayer."
"6475","As Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Qatadah who said: I said to Said ibn al-Musayyab: Abu Said al-Khudri used to pray two rakahs before Maghrib. He said: He used to forbid them, and I knew none of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying them except Sad ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him. And in this: those he saw not praying them were most of the Companions in number, and the one he saw praying them was Sad. And the prohibition may have escaped him, so whoever knew it besides him is worthier in what he fell short of. And there is narrated from Ibrahim about that, whose place in knowledge is well known, and whose acquaintance with Abdullah's companions is well known."
"6476","What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim who said: The two rakahs before Maghrib are an innovation."
"6477","5502 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Abu Hanifah informed us, from Hammad who said: I asked Ibrahim about prayer before Maghrib and he forbade me, saying: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, never prayed it. Muhammad said: And by this we take, mentioning no disagreement from Abu Hanifah or Abu Yusuf. So practice after that in the mosques, the Sacred Mosque, the Mosque of the Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and al-Aqsa, was to leave it, and jurists of the cities likewise; departing from this to otherwise is clear to people of knowledge, and we ask Allah for success."
"6478","Chapter 878: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about calling sahur breakfast. 5503 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb ibn Ali narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Yusuf informed us, Abd al-Rahman, meaning Ibn Mahdi, narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Yunus ibn Sayf, from al-Harith ibn Ziyad, from Abu Ruhm, from al-Irbad ibn Sariyah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, calling to sahur in Ramadan, saying: Come to the blessed breakfast."
"6479","5504 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Nasr informed us, Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, informed us, from Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid, Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from al-Miqdam ibn Madi Karib, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: Keep to this sahur, for it is the blessed breakfast. Then someone said: How can sahur be called breakfast, when it is called sahur because done at dawn, and breakfast because done in the morning, each contrary to the other? Our answer was: Each of sahur and breakfast is as he mentioned, but one may be named by the other's name due to proximity, so named for that. He said: Why did you not interpret it as from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the time when fasting was from sunrise to sunset? So he mentioned about that."
"6480","5505 - What Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh who said: I took sahur then went to the mosque, passing by Hudhayfah's house; I entered and he ordered a milch-camel milked and a pot heated, then said: Eat. I said: I intend to fast. He said: And I intend to fast. He said: So we ate then drank, then went to the mosque and prayer was established. He said: Thus the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did with me, or: I did with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I said: After dawn? He said: Yes, after dawn, except the sun had not risen. He said: So in this hadith that food from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was after dawn, so he called it breakfast as in the first two hadiths. Our answer was: It may be as you mentioned, that food being breakfast; and the sound reconciliation of this hadith and the first two is that mention of sahur even after dawn is called sahur, though breakfast, due to nearness to sahur; and what in the other hadith of breakfast, if before dawn, is called breakfast due to nearness to breakfast. This is the worthiest interpretation so none refutes or contradicts another, and we ask Allah for success."
"6481","Chapter 879: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in his saying: Whoever of you sees the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah and intends to sacrifice should not take from his hair and nails until he sacrifices. 5506 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Kathir ibn Dirham narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When you see the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah and one of you intends to sacrifice, let him refrain from his hair and nails."
"6482","5507 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Thabit al-Bazzar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever of you sees the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah and intends to sacrifice should not take from his hair and nails until he sacrifices. Abu Jafar said: Thus Shubah narrated this hadith from Malik. Others narrated it from Malik, differing about the Ibn Muslim whom Malik narrated from, saying: Umar ibn Muslim, and stopping it at Umm Salamah without raising it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6483","5508 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Umar ibn Muslim al-Jundai, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yazid from Yahya ibn Kathir, without raising it."
"6484","5509 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Umar ibn Muslim, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah, then he mentioned the like of it without raising it. This was not in our view corrupting this hadith nor preventing unrestricted argument with it and attribution to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for though these two narrated it from Malik stopped, one nobler than them narrated it from Malik raised. And others besides Malik ibn Anas narrated it raised from Amr ibn Muslim, namely Said ibn Abi Hilal."
"6485","5510 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibrahim ibn Marzuq from Bishr ibn Thabit exactly."
"6486","5511 - And Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah as a narration: When the first ten enter and one of you intends to sacrifice, let him not touch anything of his hair or skin. Abu Jafar said: Thus we found this hadith with Ibn Abi Aqil in these words."
"6487","5512 - And Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated it to us, Ishaq ibn Ismail al-Talqani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Salamah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it exactly."
"6488","And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf who said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyab say: Umm Salamah said, then he mentioned the like of it without raising it. Abu Jafar said: This was not in our view contradicting this hadith nor lowering what Ibn Uyaynah narrated of it, for though Anas fell short without raising it, one not below him raised it from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, namely Sufyan ibn Uyaynah. Then we considered whether this hadith is narrated otherwise."
"6489","5513 - So we found Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti al-Hawzi had narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Umar ibn Muslim ibn Umarah ibn Ukaymah al-Laythi who said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyab say: I heard Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever has a sacrificial animal to slaughter, when the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah appears let him not take anything from his hair or nails until he sacrifices. So this hadith from Muhammad ibn Amr said Umar ibn Muslim in its chain, supporting what Ibn Wahb and Uthman ibn Umar narrated from Malik with Umar ibn Muslim, contrary to what Shubah said from Malik with Amr ibn Muslim; Allah knows best the reality of his name. And in the text of Muhammad ibn Amr's hadith is what differs from the texts we narrated before in this chapter: in it, whoever has a sacrificial animal, while the earlier reports are: when you see the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah and one of you intends to sacrifice, or: whoever of you sees the crescent and intends to sacrifice. Reconciling this hadith and those without contradiction is on the meaning of obligation, and what is in these reports is that whoever the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah enters upon, intending to sacrifice and having what to sacrifice with, is prevented from taking hair and trimming nails until he sacrifices. Then someone said: But you narrated from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what contradicts that, so he mentioned."
"6490","5514 - What Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us. 5515 - And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, then they agreed and each said: From al-Shabi, from Masruq who said: I said to Aishah: Some men here send sacrificial animals to the House and order the one sent with them with a mark of theirs to garland them that day, remaining in ihram until people become lawful. She clapped her hands, I heard that from behind the veil, and said: Glory be to Allah, I used to twist the garlands of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with my hands; he would send them to the Kabah and stay among us, leaving nothing a lawful man does until people return."
"6491","5516 - And what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah who said: I would often twist garlands for the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; he would garland them then send them, then stay, avoiding nothing a pilgrim in ihram avoids."
"6492","5517 - And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Mamar Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aishah who said: We would garland the sheep and he would send it, or she said: we would send it, while the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was lawful, nothing forbidden to him."
"6493","5518 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah who said: I would often twist garlands for the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; he would garland them then send them, then stay, avoiding nothing a pilgrim in ihram avoids."
"6494","5519 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6495","5520 - And what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6496","5521 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, the like of it."
"6497","5522 - And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah and Amrah, from Aishah, the like of it."
"6498","5523 - And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab who narrated to him, from Urwah, from Aishah, the like of it, without mentioning Amrah in its chain."
"6499","5524 - And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Hisham, from Urwah, from Aishah, the like of it."
"6500","5525 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, the like of it."
"6501","5526 - And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6502","5527 - And what al-Rabi also narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, adding: And we do not know anything making the pilgrim in ihram lawful except circumambulation of the House."
"6503","5528 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it, except he did not mention her saying: And we do not know anything making the pilgrim lawful except circumambulation. This objector said: So in these reports the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would send sacrificial animals then stay in Madinah, leaving nothing a lawful man does until people return from hajj. This contradicts the first reports, for these report that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would not avoid the things he orders those intending to sacrifice and having animals to avoid in the first reports, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to sacrifice. Our answer to him was that what is in these reports is narrated as it is, and some of their narrators narrated from Aishah in what they narrated from her an addition to what they narrated from her."
"6504","5529 - As al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Said narrated to us, Abu Salih Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah who said: I used to twist the garlands of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; then he would send the animals and stay with us, avoiding nothing a pilgrim in ihram avoids of his family until people return. So in this hadith the intent of what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would not avoid is what he would avoid of his family, which a pilgrim in ihram must avoid in ihram, not shaving hair or trimming nails. That does not preclude what is in the hadith of Umm Salamah which we narrated. Reconciling what we narrated from Umm Salamah and from Aishah is that the hadith of Umm Salamah forbids whoever intends to sacrifice and has an animal from shaving hair and trimming nails in the ten days until he sacrifices, while the hadith of Aishah permits everything besides nail-trimming and hair-shaving in those days, wherein he differs from the pilgrim in ihram in all those things, so all reports agree without contradiction. And this meaning we adopted, forbidding nail-trimming and hair-shaving for intending sacrificers with animals in the ten days, is supported by what is narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that they held to that."
"6505","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatadah: that Kathir ibn Abi Kathir asked Said ibn al-Musayyab that Yahya ibn Yamar gives fatwa in Khurasan, saying: When the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah enter and a man buys his sacrifice and designates it, he should not take from his hair and nails. Said said: He did well; the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to do or say that."
"6506","And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Kathir: that Yahya ibn Yamar used to give fatwa in Khurasan that when a man buys his sacrifice, designates it and the ten enter, he should refrain from his hair and nails until he sacrifices. Qatadah said: So I mentioned that to Said ibn al-Musayyab and he said: Yes. I said: From whom, O Abu Muhammad? He said: From the Companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. So this is our view in this chapter, contrary to Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions, and Malik and his companions, and success is from Allah."
"6507","Chapter 880: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about what one who sacrifices does on the day of sacrifice with his hair and nails. 5530 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Ayyub, Amr ibn al-Harith and Abdullah ibn Ayyash informed me, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from Isa ibn Hilal al-Sadafi, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to a man: And I am commanded with the day of Adha as a festival which Allah made for this nation. The man said: What if I find only a gifted milch-animal of my son, should I sacrifice it? He said: No, but take from your hair, trim your nails, take from your mustache and shave your pubic hair; that is the completion of your sacrifice with Allah."
"6508","5531 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yazid Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri al-Bakri narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, Ayyash ibn Abbas narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, urged the mentioned man about the day of Adha and ordered him to do with his hair and nails what he ordered therein. In that is proof he had before that not permitted him that act; so what is in this hadith supports and strengthens the hadith of Umm Salamah, and success is from Allah."
"6509","Chapter 881: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, indicating permission to spend counterfeit dirhams. 5532 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Ribi ibn Hirash, without mentioning anyone between them, who said: Hudhayfah and Abu Masud sat recalling and conversing, and one of them said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A man was reckoned and nothing good was found for him; his account was examined and he was told: Did you ever do any good? He said: No, except I used to lend to people, ordering my youths or servants to ease the solvent and grant respite to the insolvent. So Allah Almighty said: I am worthier to ease. He said: Then enter Paradise. Abu Jafar said: Thus Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated this hadith to us without mentioning anyone between al-Ajlah and Ribi."
"6510","5533 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Humayd ibn Zanjuyah narrated to us, Muhadir narrated to us, al-Ajlah narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, from Ribi ibn Hirash who said: I heard Abu Masud and Hudhayfah, one saying to his companion: Narrate what you heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: Rather you narrate. He said: So one of them narrated and the other confirmed, mentioning from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the story of the man who said to his family: When I die, burn me, then grind me, then scatter me. And this story is in the hadith we mentioned from Fahd, except I abridged what I wrote in this chapter. So that shows that between al-Ajlah and Ribi therein is Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, unless Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated it from al-Ajlah from Ribi without mentioning Nuaym, making it disconnected."
"6511","5534 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus and Abdullah ibn Harun Abu Shaykh al-Harrani narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Mansur ibn al-Mutamir narrated to us, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The angels received the soul of a man before you and it was said: Did you do any good? He said: No. They said: Remember. He said: I used to lend to people, ordering my youths to grant respite to the insolvent and overlook the solvent. Allah Almighty said: Then overlook him."
"6512","5535 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah looked into a man's deed and found nothing except that he overlooked people. So Allah Almighty said: Overlook him. So in this hadith is mention of overlooking people; we considered what that overlooking is."
"6513","5536 - So we found Abu Ubayd Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Harb had narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ghundar, from Shubah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ribi, from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A man died and was told: Remember; whether he remembered or was reminded, he said: I used to trade with people, granting respite to the insolvent and overlooking in cash and coinage, so he was forgiven. Abu Masud said: And I heard it from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abu Jafar said:"
"6514","5537 - Abu Ubayd said to us, and al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Fahm narrated it to me, from Bundar, from Abu Amir al-Aqadi, from Shubah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And in this hadith that overlooking mentioned in what we narrated before in this chapter was in cash and coinage. So in that is permission to spend counterfeit dirhams, Allah knows best, together with declaring its defect, not otherwise in which some people practice deceit on others, and success is from Allah."
"6515","Chapter 882: Explanation of the problem in what scholars differed over, how to face the qiblah at death. 5538 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Salih, meaning Ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Who is your chief, O Banu Salamah? They said: Our chief, O Messenger of Allah, is al-Jadd ibn Qays. He said: On what did you make him chief? They said: Because he is wealthiest among us, though we suspect him of miserliness. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: And what disease is more sickening than miserliness; that is not your chief. They said: Then who is our chief, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Your chief is Bishr ibn al-Bara. Kab said: Al-Bara ibn Marur was the first to face the qiblah alive and at the presence of his death before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, directed it. That reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who ordered him to face Bayt al-Maqdis while he was in Makkah; so he obeyed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until death approached, when he ordered his family to direct him toward the Sacred Mosque while the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was then in Makkah. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is al-Bara's order to direct him toward the Sacred Mosque at his death, that he was first to face the qiblah alive and at death, that it reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who did not object to that facing. Then someone said: In that is proof for those who say facing the qiblah at death is as in prayer. As for Abu Hanifah and his companions, they held facing the qiblah at death is otherwise, namely facing it on one's side as one faces it in the grave. This objector said: This hadith supports their opponents, for in the hadith of Kab which you narrated, facing the qiblah in prayer and at death is mentioned as one, proving their manner is the same. Our answer was: There is nothing in the hadith proving his interpretation, for in it is only mention of facing the Kabah in both things; each may face it as required though their manners differ. And when this disagreement occurred about facing the qiblah at death, we considered whether anything decides between the disputants and clarifies the preferable. We found what the dead must face in his grave toward the qiblah is facing on his side, which is a cause of death; so by analogy his facing at death's presence should be likewise, on his side not his back, so causes of death agree with one another, differing in totality from causes of life. This is our view in this chapter, and Allah is the Grantor of success."
"6516","Chapter 883: Explanation of the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, preferring one who withdraws from people's evils until thereby cut off from them over others who mix with people. 5539 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Abi Dhib informed us, from Said ibn Khalid, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to them while they sat in a gathering, and said: Shall I inform you of the best of people in station? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: One holding his horse's bridle in the path of Allah until killed or dying; and shall I inform you of the next? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: A man withdrawn in a mountain pass, establishing prayer, giving zakah and withdrawing from people's evils; and shall I inform you of the worst of people in station? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: One asked by Allah who does not give for His sake. Thus Yunus narrated this hadith to us, saying in its chain: from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Said ibn Khalid, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas; but others besides Ibn Wahb differed in its chain."
"6517","5540 - As Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Said ibn Khalid, from Ismail ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhuayb, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then he mentioned the like of it, except he did not mention at its end: and shall I inform you of the worst of people in station, to the end of the hadith."
"6518","5541 - And as Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Harawi narrated to us, Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhuayb, without mentioning Said ibn Khalid between Ibn Abi Dhib and Ismail, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to his Companions while they sat, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yunus; Allah knows best the reality of correctness therein. And Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrated this hadith from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, who said."
"6519","5542 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, Harun ibn Maruf narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr informed me that Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Shall I inform you of the best of people? A man holding his horse's bridle in the path of Allah; and shall I inform you of the next? A man withdrawn with a small flock, rendering Allah's due in it; and shall I inform you of the worst of people? A man asked by Allah who does not give for His sake. Then someone said: You narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what contradicts the preference of withdrawing from people over mixing with them in this hadith; you narrated from him its contrary."
"6520","5543 - So he mentioned what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Sulayman, meaning al-Amash, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: I think it is Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The believer who mixes with people and endures their harm is better than the believer who does not mix with people nor endure their harm."
"6521","5544 - And Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from al-Amash, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from an elder among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I thought he said Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: The Muslim who mixes with people and endures their harm is better than one who does not mix with people nor endure their harm."
"6522","5545 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath informed us, from al-Amash, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The believer who mixes with people and endures their harm is better than the believer who does not mix with people nor endure their harm. He said: So in this hadith is the opposite of the first hadith. Our answer to him was that there is no contradiction between this hadith and the first; for what is in the first hadith of the saying of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: the best of people in station is a man holding his horse's bridle in the path of Allah until killed or dying, came in general form while particular is meant, namely among the best of people; for he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, described others likewise, saying: The best of people is one whose life is long and deed good; and he said: The best of you are who learn the Quran and teach it. That is the latitude of language and Arab usage, mentioning in general what particular is meant by, as came in Allah's Book about the queen of Saba: And she was given of everything, though she was not given anything of what Sulayman, blessings of Allah upon him, was given of things Allah singled him out with among people. Likewise what in this hadith came in general is upon particular, as indicated by what we mentioned. And his saying, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, therein: Shall I inform you of the next? Is likewise, mentioning him as best of the people of his station; it may mean among the best of that station. And when that is allowed in specifying from the people of his station, the station he is of may have between it and the station mentioned before it another station, perhaps above the prior station too, as mentioned in the hadith; so the believer among those mixing with people and enduring their harm may be better than who does not mix nor endure, by withdrawing from their evils and cutting off in what his cutting off is mentioned therein. And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the hadith of Abu Dharr which we narrated earlier in this book about the three whom Allah loves, mentioning among them a man with a neighbor harming him, enduring his harm and counting it for reward until Allah relieves him, either by death or otherwise. And when one of such conduct is beloved to Allah Almighty for enduring a single man's harm, one who offers himself to people, mixes with them, endures their harm and counts it for reward is worthier of Allah's increase therein. And the preference in leaving mixing with people may have been meant for a particular time, not all times; and the time meant may be that in the hadith of Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, in the answer of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to his question about what is meant by Allah's saying: O you who believe, upon you is yourselves; he who strays harms you not when guided: He said: Rather enjoin good and forbid evil, until when you see obeyed avarice, followed desire, preferred worldliness, every opinionated admiring his opinion, and see a matter you must face; then upon you is your own affair, beware the commoners, for after you are days of patience, patience therein like grasping embers; the worker then among you has the reward of fifty men doing like his deed. We mentioned this hadith with its chains earlier in this book. So withdrawing from people in that time is better than mixing, and we seek refuge in Allah from that time; other times are otherwise. And what is meant by preferring mixing with people over leaving it is that time, so neither of these two hadiths we mentioned contradicts the other. And among what is narrated from Ibn Abbas is his hadith we mentioned in this chapter from another angle."
"6523","5546 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Habib ibn Shihab ibn Mudlij al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrate, he said: I came to Ibn Abbas with a companion of mine, and we met Abu Hurayrah at Ibn Abbas's door. He said: Who are you two? We informed him. He said: Go to people on dates and water; each valley flows according to its measure. We said: May your good increase; seek permission for us from Ibn Abbas. So he sought permission, and we heard Ibn Abbas narrate from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, preached on the day of Tabuk, saying: There is none among people like a man holding his horse's bridle to strive in the path of Allah and avoid people's evils, and like a man in the desert with his sheep, honoring his guest and rendering his due. I said: Did he say it? He said: He said it. I said: Did he say it? He said: He said it. I said: Did he say it? He said: He said it. He said: So I said Allahu Akbar, praised Allah Almighty and thanked. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is the Prophet's mention, peace be upon him, of the people of the two stations in the first hadith without preferring one over the other; in that is proof they would mention things in ranks, preferring some over others while in reality not preferred. And what confirms our interpretation of this hadith and our directing its meaning to what we mentioned is that it is about a particular time."
"6524","5547 - What Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Uthman al-Shahham, from Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There will be tribulations; indeed then will be a tribulation in which the lying one is better than the sitting, the sitting better than the standing, the standing better than the walking, the walking better than the hastening. When it occurs, whoever has land should join his land, whoever has camels should join his camels, whoever has sheep should join his sheep. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, whoever has neither land nor camels nor sheep? He said: Let him sheath his sword, blunt it, then escape if he can. Then he said: O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed? They said: Yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, if I am coerced until taken and placed between the two factions, and a man comes and kills me? He said: He bears your sin and his sin, and will be among the companions of the Fire."
"6525","5548 - And what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Uthman al-Shahham, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it up to his saying: And whoever has sheep should join his sheep, without mentioning what follows in the hadith of Bakkar to its end. Abu Jafar said: Withdrawing from people in the situation mentioned in this hadith is of high rank; so it may be the rank meant in the other hadith, and we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"6526","884 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to Bilal about prayer: ""Grant us rest through it, O Bilal."" 5549 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah who said: I entered with my father to visit an in-law of ours from the Ansar, then the prayer time came, so he said: O my slave-girl, bring me water for ablution, perhaps I will perform ablution and find rest. He saw that we disapproved of that, or it was as if he saw us disapproving of that, so he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Rise, O Bilal, and grant us rest through the prayer."" An objector objected to this hadith and said: How can you accept, ascribed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his commanding to be given rest from the prayer? Our answer to him regarding that is that there is nothing in the hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded to be given rest from the prayer; had the hadith been so, we would have objected to it as he objected. What is in the hadith is only his command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Bilal to grant him rest through the prayer from other things, since prayer was the delight of his eye, so he commanded to be given rest through it from what is besides it, whose rank is not like its rank. This is sound, reasonable speech, and Allah knows best what he intended by that, of what resembles what he was upon in the matters of Allah Almighty, in performing His obligations, holding fast to them, their predominance over his heart, and that nothing in his view is like it. And success is from Allah."
"6527","885 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what he described of woman, that she approaches in the image of a devil and turns her back in the image of a devil. 5550 - Abu Umayyah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Muslim narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah al-Dastuwai narrated to us, Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a woman, so he entered upon Zaynab bint Jahsh and fulfilled his need, then went out to his Companions and said to them: ""Indeed woman approaches in the image of a devil and turns back in the image of a devil, so whoever experiences that, let him go to his wife, for that will relieve what is in his soul."" Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith and found that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described woman in her approaching and turning back with what he described her. Devils are described in the Book of Allah Almighty in two senses. One of them: His likening, Almighty, the tree which is the food of the people of Hell, growing at the bottom of Hell, that its spathe is like the heads of devils, and that was to express the hideousness, horror and ugliness of what it is upon. We understood thereby that what woman was likened to of the devil is other than that, for in her form she is opposite to this description. And we found Him, Almighty, describing the devil which is of the highest of its ranks by His saying: ""O children of Adam, let not Satan tempt you as he removed your parents from Paradise,"" to the end of the verse, and that referred to what he casts into their hearts of what misguides them thereby and stirs them to disobedience of their Lord Almighty. That could plausibly be what woman was likened to in the hadith we mentioned, because there mixes with their hearts from her the like of what mixes with their hearts from what Satan casts into them. Then we found the like of that in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: ""Indeed Satan runs through the son of Adam like the running of blood."" Like that is what occurs from their seeing woman, of what falls into their hearts, of no hidden kind among their likes, of what is of disobedience of their Lord, and of what brings upon them of punishments in this world and the Hereafter, from what occurs from them at that, like what Satan casts into their hearts, until that becomes a cause for what that necessitates of punishment in their worldly life and punishment in their Hereafter. So the Prophet's command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to whoever sees that to do what he commanded him to do cuts off the cause which he fears has fallen into his heart, which would be a cause for those things. And success is from Allah."
"6528","886 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: ""None shall enter the Fire who has in his heart the weight of a mustard grain of faith."" 5551 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Harami ibn Hafs narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""None shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a mustard grain of arrogance, and none shall enter the Fire who has in his heart the weight of a mustard grain of faith."""
"6529","5552 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah, al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Kufi al-Hibri, and Muhammad ibn al-Ward ibn Zanjawayh al-Baghdadi narrated to us; they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6530","5553 - And Ismail ibn Hamdawayh al-Bikandi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus and Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us; they said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6531","5554 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Then a speaker said: In this hadith is that none shall enter the Fire who has in his heart the weight of a mustard grain of faith, yet you narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that there shall come out of the Fire whoever says ""There is no god but Allah,"" and whoever has in his heart good weighing an atom, and he mentioned regarding that:"
"6532","5555 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Sulayman al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and Abidah, from Abdullah, raising the hadith: He said: ""Indeed I surely know the last of the people of the Fire to come out of the Fire, and the last of the people of Paradise to enter Paradise: a man who comes out of the Fire crawling, and it is said to him: Enter Paradise. He enters though the people have taken their dwellings, so he comes out and says: O my Lord, I found no dwelling in it. Allah Almighty says to him: Enter, for We shall make a dwelling for you in it. He says: O my Lord, I found no dwelling in it. He enters then comes out and says: My Lord, I found no dwelling in it. Allah Almighty says to him: Then for you is the like of the world and ten times it; or he said: Are you pleased that We make for you the like of the world and ten times it? He says: O my Lord, do You mock me while You are the King?"" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared at that. And in this chapter there are other reports from Abdullah ibn Masud, whose mention we have delayed to a chapter other than this chapter, since what we mentioned of them in this chapter suffices for the rest."
"6533","5556 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Qatadah informed me, he said: I heard Anas saying: Indeed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There shall come out of the Fire whoever says 'There is no god but Allah' and has in his heart good weighing a grain of wheat, and there shall come out of the Fire whoever says 'There is no god but Allah' and has in his heart good weighing an atom. And every Prophet has a supplication he supplicated for his nation, and I have reserved my supplication as intercession for my nation on the Day of Resurrection."" This speaker said: This too is severe contradiction, for what is in the first hadith and what is in this hadith contradict each other with no obscurity to their hearer, since what is in one of them denies that one who has in his heart the weight of a mustard grain of faith shall enter the Fire, while in the other of them is that there shall come out of the Fire whoever has in his heart what weighs an atom of good, and none comes out of it except one who was made to enter it. Our answer to him regarding that, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that there is no contradiction in anything of these two hadiths, since each one of them is intended for other than what the other is intended for, even though the tongue addressed in is an Arabic tongue addressing an Arab people, the Quran having come down in their language, and with them understanding of what they are addressed with, and their addresser further informing them in his addressing them. The sense of what is in the first hadith is the entering accompanied by eternity in the Fire, and what is in the second hadith is upon the entering with which there is no eternity in the Fire."
"6534","And the proof of that is that Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Samad ibn Shuayb ibn Ishaq al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, my grandfather narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Za'ra, from Ibn Masud who said: Allah Almighty shall punish a people of the people of faith, then bring them out by the intercession of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until there remains in the Fire none but those whom Allah Almighty mentioned: ""What put you into Saqar? They will say: We were not of those who prayed, nor did we feed the poor, and we used to wade along with those who waded, and we used to deny the Day of Judgment, until certainty came to us. Then the intercession of the intercessors will not benefit them."""
"6535","And Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Sufyan informed us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Za'ra who said: We were with Abdullah ibn Masud when the Dajjal was mentioned in his presence, so he mentioned a long hadith, saying at its end: Then Allah Almighty shall permit intercession, and the first intercessor on the Day of Resurrection shall be the Holy Spirit, Jibril, then Ibrahim the intimate friend of Allah, then Musa and Isa, I do not know which of them he mentioned first, then your Prophet shall be fourth, not rejected in what he intercedes for, and it is the Praiseworthy Station which Allah Almighty mentioned, He said: ""It may be that your Lord will raise you to a praiseworthy station."" There is no soul but it looks at a house in Paradise and a house in the Fire, and it is the Day of Remorse. He said: The people of the Fire look at the house in Paradise and it is said: If only you had known; and the people of Paradise look at the house in the Fire and it is said: Had Allah not favored you. He said: Then the angels, the Prophets, the martyrs, the righteous and the believers intercede, and He accepts their intercession. He said: Then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: I am the Most Merciful, I am the Most Merciful of the merciful, and He brings out of the Fire more than all creation brought out, by His mercy. He said: Until He leaves no one in whom there is good. He said: Then Abdullah recited: ""What put you into Saqar? They will say: We were not of those who prayed,"" up to His saying: ""And we used to wade with those who waded, and we used to deny the Day of Judgment."" Then he knotted four with his hand and said: Do you see any good in these? Indeed no one in whom there is good shall be left. When Allah Almighty wills that none come out of it, He changes their faces and colors. A man of the believers comes and says: O Lord; He says: Whoever knows anyone, let him bring him out. A man comes to a man he knows and says: I do not know you; he says: I am so-and-so, I am so-and-so; he says: I do not know you. Thereupon the people of the Fire say: ""Our Lord, bring us out of it, then if we return, we are wrongdoers."" Thereupon He says: ""Remain despised in it and do not speak to Me."" He said: Then it closes upon them, and none comes out of it. And I heard Fahd ibn Sulayman saying: I heard Abu Nuaym saying: al-Firyabi wrote to me: You used to dictate for us from Sufyan the hadith of Abu al-Za'ra, meaning this hadith. Abu Nuaym said: And I do not know him, meaning al-Firyabi. In this hadith of Abu al-Za'ra is the confirmation of what we mentioned of the two intended senses in the first hadith we mentioned in this chapter, and in this hadith of Abu al-Za'ra is what indicates the two intended senses in the first hadith and in the second hadith. This speaker said: Is it then permissible to say: None shall enter the Fire who does enter? Our answer to him regarding that is that there has already passed from us in this chapter our description of the tongue in which the Quran came down, and how the addressed one was taught what is intended, and the addressed ones knew that from him. We found Allah Almighty mentioning the like of that in His Book by His saying: ""Indeed whoever associates partners with Allah, Allah has forbidden Paradise to him."" That was not upon everyone who associated partners with Allah Almighty; it was only upon whoever associated partners with Him and remained upon his association until he left the world, and it was not upon whoever associated partners with Him then repented of his association until he left the world believing in Him, because of what He clarified of that in His saying: ""And those who do not invoke with Allah another god, nor kill the soul which Allah made sacred except by right, nor commit adultery; and whoever does that shall meet punishment. The punishment shall be multiplied for him on the Day of Resurrection, and he shall abide in it humiliated, except whoever repents and believes and does righteous work, then those, Allah shall change their evil deeds into good deeds, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful."" We understood thereby that the people of the threat in the first verse are those from whom repentance and withdrawal from association with Him Almighty do not occur until they leave the world, and that whoever repents of his association, believes in Him and does righteous work is not included in the threat in the first verse. Since that is so in what we mentioned, like it is what is in the two hadiths we mentioned in this chapter from Ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the first of them upon the denial of an entering accompanied by eternity, and affirming eternity for others than them. It has become clear, by Allah's praise, that there is no contradiction in anything of what this ignorant one imagined in the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that Allah Exalted has undertaken them with what prevents there being in them what he imagined. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"6536","887 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: ""None shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of an atom of arrogance."" 5557 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from Fudayl al-Fuqaymi, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Alqamah ibn Qays, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""None shall enter the Fire who has in his heart the weight of an atom of faith, and none shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a grain of arrogance."" A man said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us loves that his garment be fine and his sandal be fine. He said: ""Arrogance is rejecting the truth and despising people."" Ibrahim said to us: And he narrated to us another time, saying: Disdaining the truth."
"6537","5558 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from Fudayl al-Fuqaymi, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Alqamah ibn Qays, from Abdullah ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""None shall enter the Fire who has in his heart the weight of a grain of faith, and none shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a grain of arrogance."" A man said: O Messenger of Allah, a man loves that his garment be fine and his sandal be fine. He said: ""Indeed Allah Exalted is Beautiful and loves beauty. Arrogance is rejecting the truth and despising people."" We do not know anyone who narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better than what the Kufans narrated from him in it, of this hadith, in the soundness of its chain and the excellence of its text's wording. They also narrated it from another angle of what may be of connected chain, and of what prevails in hearts that it is otherwise, and it is:"
"6538","5559 - What Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aishah narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Yahya ibn Jadhah, from Abdullah who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I love that my head be oiled, my garment washed, and the strap of my sandal new; is that of arrogance, O Messenger of Allah? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""No, but arrogance is whoever scorns the truth and despises people."" Yahya ibn Jadhah was early, except that we do not know, despite his early date, of any meeting of his with Abdullah ibn Masud. Some people mention that the Abdullah mentioned in this hadith is not Ibn Masud, but Abdullah ibn Umar; if so, its connection is established, and this becomes attached to the first hadith. They have regarding it another hadith, and it is:"
"6539","5560 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Isa, meaning his brother, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Thabit ibn Qays who said: Arrogance was mentioned in the presence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he stressed it severely. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Indeed Allah does not love whoever is arrogant, boastful."" A man of the people said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, my clothes are washed and I like their whiteness, and I like the strap of my sandal and the loop of my whip. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""That is not arrogance; arrogance is only that you scorn the truth and despise people."""
"6540","5561 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Imran narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. The Basrans have narrated in this chapter a hadith of fair chain, except that in its text there is shortcoming from what is in the text of this hadith, and it is:"
"6541","5562 - What Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, then he mentioned the story of the man who said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Beauty has been made beloved to me, is that of arrogance? He said: No. The Syrians narrated it with complete text."
"6542","5563 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us, Said ibn Marthad al-Rahabi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hawshab, from Thawban al-Ashari who said: I heard Kurayb ibn Abrahah, while he was sitting with Abd al-Malik on a roof at Dayr al-Murran, and they mentioned arrogance, so Kurayb said: I heard Abu Rayhanah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Indeed nothing of arrogance shall enter Paradise."" A speaker said: O Prophet of Allah, I love to beautify myself with the girth of my whip and the strap of my sandal. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""That is not arrogance. Indeed Allah Exalted is Beautiful and loves beauty. Arrogance is only whoever scorns the truth and despises people,"" and by al-jilaz he means the thong of the whip. In what we narrated was the clarification of the intended arrogance in these reports, what it is: exalting oneself over people, a man placing himself in a position Allah did not place him in, despising people by lowering them below the positions Allah placed them in, in contradiction to Allah Exalted's ruling regarding him and them. The threat from Allah Exalted is not objectionable in that, by what is in these reports. And success is from Allah."
"6543","888 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his forbidding one intending prayer from interlacing his fingers on his way to it. 5564 - Yunus narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Sad ibn Ishaq, meaning Ibn Kab ibn Ujrah, from Abu Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Thumamah who said: I met Kab ibn Ujrah while I intended Friday prayer and had interlaced my fingers, so he separated my fingers and said: We were forbidden that one of us interlace his fingers in prayer. I said: I am not in prayer. He said: Have you not performed ablution while intending Friday prayer? I said: Yes. He said: Then you are in prayer."
"6544","5565 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Sad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Thumamah, then he mentioned the like of it. This hadith has come through Abu Said al-Maqburi with no disagreement from him in it, and it has come through his son Said ibn Abi Said with disagreement from him in its chain. Among those who narrated it from him is Ibn Abi Dhib."
"6545","5566 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from a man of Banu Salim, from his father, from his grandfather, from Kab ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""No man purifies himself in his house then goes out intending prayer but he is in prayer until he completes the prayer, so let no one cross the fingers of his hand in prayer."" Abu Jafar said: This al-Maqburi from whom Ibn Abi Dhib narrated this hadith is Said, because he narrated nothing from his father. And among them is Ibn Ajlan."
"6546","5567 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said al-Maqburi, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Whoever goes out to prayer, let him not interlace his fingers, for he is in prayer."""
"6547","5568 - And as Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Said who said: One of the family of Kab ibn Ujrah narrated to us: that Kab ibn Ujrah used to narrate: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever interlaces his fingers in the mosque while praying, let him perform ablution."" This hadith was narrated by Dawud ibn Qays, mentioning it from Abu Thumamah, without mentioning anyone between him and him in it."
"6548","5569 - As Yunus narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Abu Thumamah al-Hannat, from Kab ibn Ujrah who said: Abu Thumamah said: Kab met me while I was at al-Balat having interlaced my fingers, and said: Where do you intend? I said: I intend the mosque. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""When one of you performs ablution and goes out intending the mosque, he is in prayer so long as he does not interlace his fingers."" This hadith was also narrated from Kab ibn Ujrah at Kufa."
"6549","5570 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Ubaydullah al-Raqqi narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Kab ibn Ujrah, when you perform ablution and perform ablution well, then go out to the mosque, do not interlace your fingers, for you are in prayer."" We know nothing in this chapter from Kab better than this hadith. We reflected on this hadith and found in it the Prophet's prohibition (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to a man against interlacing his fingers on his way to prayer. We understood thereby that he placed one intending prayer under the ruling of one who is in prayer, except what Allah permitted him of speech in that, and of walking to it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified in other than this hadith the meaning we mentioned, that there is intended for one intending prayer on his way to it that meaning, and that he be upon the calm demeanor in which there is no exceeding that to running, since running requires a sense which is not in walking with calm demeanor."
"6550","5571 - And it is what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, and Ishaq ibn Abdullah: that they heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you come to prayer, come to it walking, and do not come to it running. Come to it with tranquility upon you. What you catch, pray, and what you miss, complete, for one of you is in prayer so long as he heads toward prayer."""
"6551","5572 - And what al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, Malik informed us, from al-Ala, from his father, and Ishaq ibn Abdullah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Thus Ibn Wahb and al-Shafii narrated this hadith from Malik, while al-Qanabi narrated it from Malik."
"6552","5573 - As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, al-Qanabi narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When the call to prayer is made,"" then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: ""And what you miss, complete it,"" and he did not mention Ishaq ibn Abdullah in its chain. In this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to people to be in their coming to prayer upon the calm demeanor they come upon and which they are upon in it. Like that is what was narrated from Kab from him regarding the prohibition of interlacing fingers in the state of intending prayer; it is like the prohibition of the like of that for one who has entered it. And Allah we ask for success."
"6553","889 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: ""Circumambulation of the House is prayer, except that Allah Exalted permitted speech in it; so whoever speaks, meaning in it, let him speak only good."" 5574 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us; and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us; they said: al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Circumambulation of the House is prayer, except that Allah Exalted has permitted speech for you; so whoever speaks, let him speak only good."" In this hadith was proof that one circumambulating the House should be in his circumambulation upon the state the praying one is upon in his prayer, of covering nakedness, of purity, and of other than that of what the praying one is commanded in his prayer, and not depart from that except to what was permitted him of what makes him a circumambulator of that circumambulation, of what is prevented the like of in prayer. This meaning in this hadith supports the meaning upon which we interpreted the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this. And Allah we ask for success."
"6554","890 - Chapter explaining the problematic intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying to Abu Bakrah when he bowed before the row while breathless: ""May Allah increase you in eagerness, and do not repeat."" 5575 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, that Ziyad al-Alam informed them, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was bowing, and I was breathless, so I bowed before the row, then walked to the row. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed the prayer, he said: ""Which of you bowed before the row?"" Abu Bakrah said: I said, I. He said: ""May Allah increase you in eagerness, and do not repeat."""
"6555","5576 - And al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibri narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. We reflected on the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Abu Bakrah: Do not repeat, and found some people took it as: do not return to the running to prayer which was from him until he was breathless. And we found some of them took it as his forbidding him to bow before the row until he takes his place in the row. And we found of what narrated this very meaning."
"6556","5577 - Of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Umar ibn Ali narrated to me, Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When one of you comes to prayer, let him not bow before the row until he takes his place in the row."" Both meanings are of what it is permissible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended them both by his saying to Abu Bakrah: And do not repeat, and Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah's intent was in that. A speaker said: Do you dislike bowing before the row? It was narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit that he used to do that, and he mentioned."
"6557","What Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl who said: I saw Zayd ibn Thabit enter the mosque while people were bowing; when he could reach the row while bowing, he bowed, then shuffled while bowing until he reached the row."
"6558","And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik and Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6559","And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, my father informed me, from Kharijah, that Zayd ibn Thabit used to bow at the threshold of the mosque facing the qiblah, then walk sideways on his right side, then count it if he reached the row or did not reach it. We said to him: We dislike bowing before the row for one person, and do not dislike it for a group, for one person is thereby like one praying alone in a row, and that is of what has been said regarding it what was said, of corruption of prayer with it and of its permissibility with dislike for that. Thus Muhammad ibn al-Hasan used to say regarding that, of what he narrated no disagreement in it between him and any of his companions."
"6560","And as Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him. It was also narrated from Abdullah ibn Masud his bowing before the row with others than him."
"6561","As Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Zayd ibn Wahb who said: I entered the mosque, I and Ibn Masud, and we found the imam bowing, so we bowed, then walked until we stood level in the row. When the imam completed the prayer, I stood to make up, so Abdullah said: You have caught the prayer."
"6562","And as Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Bashir ibn Salman narrated to us, Sayyar Abu al-Hakam narrated to us, from Tariq who said: We were sitting with Ibn Masud when his caller came and said: Prayer has been established. He stood and we stood, and entered the mosque, and saw people bowing at the front of the mosque, so he said takbir and bowed and walked, and we did the like of what he did. What was in what we narrated from Abdullah is that they were a group, and it is possible that Zayd ibn Thabit in his doing what he did of what we narrated from him in this chapter was with others than him intending what he intended, and they were thereby a group, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that. It is not necessary to take what was from him as contrary to what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding prayer before the row. This is the best we found of interpretation of the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Abu Bakrah when there was from him what we narrated from him in his hadith which we narrated from him in this chapter. And success is from Allah."
"6563","891 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what refutes what some people narrated from Abu Hanifah regarding one for whom throat is cleared while he prays, so he waits for the one clearing his throat. Some people narrated from Abu Hanifah al-Khawarizmi, from Abu Hanifah al-Numan ibn Thabit: that whoever does that, his prayer is invalid, and I fear for him, the meaning being that he performed part of his prayer for other than Allah, so he is thereby a disbeliever. Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us this saying, from Muhammad ibn Shuja, from Abu Hanifah al-Khawarizmi by this chain, and this saying was not heard from Abu Hanifah al-Numan ibn Thabit, may Allah have mercy on him, from any other chain. We found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what refutes this saying."
"6564","5578 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan who said: I heard my father narrating from Abu Hurayrah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a boy's voice while in prayer and shortened it. In this is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shortened his prayer because of the crying of the boy he heard while in it. A speaker said: There is nothing in this hadith obligating for you against one who narrated the narration you mentioned from Abu Hanifah, for what is in this hadith is only of Abu Hurayrah's speech, supposing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that his shortening was because of him. That was indicated by."
"6565","5579 - What Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a boy crying while in prayer, so we supposed he shortened out of mercy for the boy's crying, since he knew his mother was with him in prayer. We looked: was there narrated in this chapter what establishes the ruling of what is obligatory in this act in prayer, what it is?"
"6566","5580 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Yaqub narrating, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us in one of the two evening prayers, carrying one of his two sons, al-Hasan or al-Husayn. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced and placed the boy at his right foot, then prostrated in the midst of his prayer a prostration he prolonged. My father said: So I raised my head from among the people, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrating with the boy riding on his back. I returned and prostrated. When he prayed, they said: O Messenger of Allah, you prostrated in the midst of your prayer a prostration you prolonged; was something commanded to you, or was revelation coming to you? He said: ""Neither of that was, but my son mounted me, and I disliked to hurry him until he fulfills his need of me."" In this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's waiting (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his son until he fulfills his need of him while in prayer, and that did not invalidate his prayer nor remove him from it. That indicates that whoever has the like of this in his prayer for a need calling to it, or a necessity befalling him, that is not invalidating his prayer nor disliked of him in it. How can the like of this invalidate his prayer or remove him from the like of it, when we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his permitting the praying one to kill snake and scorpion in his prayer?! We shall mention that later in this book of ours, Allah willing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not by his doing that in his prayer make him abandoning it nor exiting it. Like that, one who waits for another to enter it and attain of its merit what he sought by coming to it is not by his doing that invalidating it nor exiting his religion by his doing what he did in it. What we have of Abu Hanifah's saying in this chapter, of what we learned from those we sat with who profess his saying, is that this act in waiting for the throat-clearer is disliked, for another than him who preceded him to prayer is more entitled to do with him what follows his imam in, and to be thereby more entitled than one who fell short of coming to it and was slow in it. This too is Malik's school in this chapter, and it is also the import of al-Shafii in it, or narrated from him in it. The application of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that, its import with us, and Allah knows best, is upon what has no addition in it from the waited-for throat-clearer harming those behind him in his prayer to which he preceded and entered, and we say: there is no harm in doing that when there is no harm in it upon the praying ones with him, and he is not by what he does of that falling under the name of one occupied with other than his prayer; he is only by his doing in occupation with his prayer, and in rectifying it for another as he is in rectifying it for himself, of advancing from row to row to fill the gap in it. The like of that was narrated from Ibn Umar."
"6567","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Murrah informed me, he said: I heard Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman saying: I prayed beside Ibn Umar, and he saw a gap in the row, so he kept nudging me to advance, while I kept being prevented from advancing by the narrowness of my place, lest sitting I be distanced from him. When he saw that, he advanced himself. This is not of the prayer people are upon in it; they are only upon it when need calls to that, to rectify it and establish its sunnah, since of its sunnah is filling gaps in rows in it. When the like of this is permitted for the praying one in his own affair, it is permitted of him for another than him of what his doing for him is rectification of his prayer. And success is from Allah."
"6568","892 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: ""Take the Quran from four,"" mentioning four of those who gathered the Quran to the exclusion of others than them who gathered it. 5581 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir and Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Shubah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibrahim, from Masruq who said: Abdullah ibn Masud was mentioned in the presence of Abdullah ibn Amr, so he said: That is a man I shall ever love after I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Recite the Quran from four: Abdullah ibn Masud, Salim, freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, Ubayy ibn Kab, and Muadh ibn Jabal."""
"6569","5582 - And Abu Bishr Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Masruq, that Abdullah ibn Amr said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Take the Quran from four: from Abdullah ibn Masud, Ubayy ibn Kab, Muadh ibn Jabal, and Salim, freedman of Abu Hudhayfah."""
"6570","5583 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from al-Amash who said: I heard Abu Wail narrating, from Masruq who said: Abdullah ibn Amr said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Recite the Quran from four: from Abdullah, Salim freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, Ubayy ibn Kab, and Muadh ibn Jabal."""
"6571","5584 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Dawud ibn Shabur narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Take the Quran from four: two men of the Muhajirun and two men of the Ansar: Abdullah ibn Masud, Salim freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, Ubayy ibn Kab, and Muadh ibn Jabal."" And he singled out Abdullah ibn Masud, saying: ""Whoever loves to recite the Quran fresh as it was sent down, let him recite it as Ibn Umm Abd recites it."" Abdullah said: So I shall ever love him. A speaker said: In these reports you narrated is the Messenger of Allah's singling out (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) these four named men, so people take the Quran from them, though among his Companions besides them were those who gathered the Quran as they gathered it, namely Abu Zayd Thabit ibn Zayd, one of Banu al-Harith of the Khazraj, and Zayd ibn Thabit."
"6572","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatadah who said: I said to Anas: Who gathered the Quran in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Four, all of them of the Ansar: Ubayy ibn Kab, Zayd ibn Thabit, Muadh ibn Jabal, and Abu Zayd."
"6573","And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih al-Harithi narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6574","And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani informed us, from al-Husayn ibn Waqid, from Thumamah ibn Abdullah ibn Anas, from Anas who said: Four gathered the Quran in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Ubayy ibn Kab, Zayd ibn Thabit, Abu Zayd, and Muadh ibn Jabal."
"6575","And as Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zabalah al-Madini narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Anas who said: The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, boasted. Al-Aws said: Of us is the one washed by angels, Hanzalah ibn al-Rahib; of us is one for whom the Throne of the Merciful shook; of us is one protected by hornets, Asim ibn Thabit ibn al-Aqlah; and of us is one whose testimony was accepted as that of two men. The Khazrajis said: Of us are four who gathered the Quran, and none but them gathered it: Ubayy ibn Kab, Muadh ibn Jabal, Abu Zayd, and Zayd ibn Thabit. A speaker said: In these reports are of those who gathered the Quran some mentioned in them who were not mentioned in the first reports; since all are equal in gathering the Quran, it is impossible that some of them be more entitled to take it from than the rest. Our answer to him regarding that is that the Quran was gathered by one fit to be taken from, for his precision in it and excellence of taking from whoever recites to him; and it may be gathered by one who is not so in what one reciting to him needs of him, in his precision upon him, in correcting what needs correcting for him, and in stopping him upon what he must stop with of what the reciter needs of the recited-to. Since that is so, the four named in the first reports must be fit for that and able to it of themselves, and people are able to it from them, while others than them of those we mentioned in the other reports fall short of that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded people to take it from those with no shortcoming in this respect, to the exclusion of the others who fall short of it. And success is from Allah."
"6576","893 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him: ""I was commanded to recite to you."" 5585 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, al-Ajlah informed me, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Kab who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I was commanded to recite the Quran to you."" He said: I said: Did your Lord name me to you? He said: Yes. So he recited to me: ""Say: In the bounty of Allah and in His mercy, in that let them rejoice; it is better than what they gather,"" with ta."
"6577","5586 - And al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""I was commanded to recite the Quran to you."" A speaker said: How do you accept this, when the Quran is recited to one recited to only to be taken from him, and because he has in it a rank above what the reciter upon him has, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the furthest of people from that, and from needing the like of that from others than him of people? Our answer to him regarding that is that his reciting to Ubayy was to apprise him of what he recites to him of it, so that he thereby takes it from his mouth, although this hadith was narrated from Ubayy with other than this wording."
"6578","5587 - As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Aslam al-Minqari, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Kab who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""A surah was sent down to me, and I was commanded to teach it to you."" He said: I said to him: Then you rejoiced? He said: And what prevents me, while He says Almighty: ""Say: In the bounty of Allah and in His mercy, in that let them rejoice."" The one who narrated this first hadith with the wordings he narrated it. Then we looked: was this story narrated from other than this angle?"
"6579","5588 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ubayy and said to him: ""Allah Almighty commanded me to recite to you."" He said: Did He name me to you? He said: Allah named you to me. So Ubayy began weeping. Qatadah said: And he clarified that he recited to him: ""Those who disbelieved of the People of the Book were not..."""
"6580","5589 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Ali ibn Zayd informed us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar who said: I heard Abu Habbah al-Badri saying: When ""Those who disbelieved..."" was sent down to its end, Jibril, blessings of Allah upon him, said: O Messenger of Allah, your Lord commands you to teach it to Ubayy. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ubayy: ""Jibril commanded me to teach you this surah."" Ubayy said: Was I mentioned there, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. So Ubayy wept. The discussion of the Messenger of Allah's reciting (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Ubayy, and of his having him recite, is like the discussion in what preceded from us in this chapter. What we narrated in the first section of this chapter indicated that that was only in one who mentioned that it was in all the Quran; this is of what is permissible in language, to mention the Quran while part of it is intended, as a man says: I heard so-and-so reciting the Quran, when he heard him reciting something of it, even if he did not hear him recite all of it. Of that is Allah Almighty's saying: ""When you recite the Quran, seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan,"" and that was upon one intending recitation of the Quran, and upon one intending recitation of something of it, even if he does not recite all of it. If a speaker says: Did you find for the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who gathered the Quran, of rank in the Quran, the like of what Ubayy had in it? Our answer to him regarding that is that we found for Abdullah ibn Masud the like of what we found for Ubayy in it, and increase upon it. The recitation he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was of him upon Jibril, blessings of Allah upon him, and it was of the whole Quran. What Ubayy attended, of what we mentioned his attending of it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was not like that; he only taught him a surah of the Quran, not upon Jibril, blessings of Allah upon him."
"6581","5590 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah al-Nakhai, Abu Muawiyah and Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Zibyan who said: Ibn Abbas said: Which of the two readings do you recite? I said: The first reading, the reading of Ibn Umm Abd. He said to me: Rather it is the last. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to present the Quran to Jibril every year once; in the year he died, he presented it to him twice, and Abdullah ibn Masud attended that, so he knew what was abrogated and what was altered. In this hadith was Abdullah ibn Masud's attendance of the recitation which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to Jibril. We know with full knowledge that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not convey to Ibn Masud that rank except by Allah's command to him to convey it to him. Although we looked into the hadith narrated by Hammam, from Qatadah, from Anas, which we mentioned in the section before the section of this chapter, and found one above Hammam of Qatadah's companions, Said ibn Abi Arubah, disagreeing with Hammam in its chain."
"6582","5591 - As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata, from Said, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ubayy ibn Kab: ""Allah Exalted commanded me to teach you."" Ubayy said: Was I mentioned with Him? He said: Yes. He said: Then his eyes filled with tears, and he began weeping. In this was what indicated that there entered in its chain what has no obscurity. Then we looked into what was narrated from the companions of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what indicates the aspect in that and its reality."
"6583","5592 - We found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, from al-Amash. 5593 - And we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah who said: A man came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while at Arafat, and said: I came to you from Kufa, leaving therein a man dictating the codices from memory. He said: Umar became angry and swelled until he almost filled what is between the two wooden parts of the saddle, and said: Woe to you, who is he? He said: Abdullah ibn Masud. He said: By Allah, he kept calming down and anger left him until he returned to the state he was upon. Then he said: By Allah, I know no one of people more entitled to that than him. I shall inform you of that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to spend the evening with Abu Bakr at night, likewise in a matter of the Muslims' affairs. He spent an evening with him one night while I was with him. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and we went out with him. When he entered the mosque, there was a man standing praying. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood listening to his recitation, and we could hardly recognize the man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever is pleased to recite the Quran moist as it was sent down, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd."" Then the man sat supplicating, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Ask, you shall be given; ask, you shall be given."" I said: By Allah, I shall surely go to him in the morning and give him glad tidings. I went to him in the morning and found Abu Bakr had preceded me to him and given him tidings. By Allah, I never preceded him to any good but he preceded me to it."
"6584","5594 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, Shayban, meaning al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, and from Khaythamah, they said: Qays ibn Marwan set out to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while on pilgrimage upon a red camel of his. Abdullah ibn Masud hastened to him and said: O Commander of the Faithful, I left a man in Iraq dictating the codices. Then he mentioned the hadith, as Fahd narrated to us from the two men who narrated it to us from them. In this hadith Umar swore that he knows no one of people more entitled to what he mentioned of Ibn Masud than Ibn Masud. Among people in those days were Ubayy and others than him who had gathered the Quran, except Salim freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, for he had died before that, and except Abu Zayd, for it is also possible he had died before that, since his death was in Umar's days, and it is not known whether before that or after it."
"6585","And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail who said: Abdullah ibn Masud addressed us upon the pulpit and said: By Allah, by Allah, nothing of the Quran came down but I know in what it came down. No one is more knowledgeable of Allah Exalted's Book than me, yet I am not the best of you. Had I known anyone more knowledgeable of Allah Exalted's Book than me, I would have gone to him. Abu Wail said: When he came down from the pulpit, I sat in the circles and found no one denying what he said. In this hadith from Abdullah is what is in it from him. In it is the abandonment of denial by those who heard that of those he addressed of it. Among them were Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who were among them, and they did not deny that upon him, indicating their agreement with him upon it."
"6586","5595 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Sulayman al-Amash narrated to us, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah who said: When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded regarding the codices what he commanded, Abdullah ibn Masud stood as preacher and said: Do you command me to recite the Quran upon the recitation of Zayd ibn Thabit? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I took from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) some seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit at that time was playing with boys. Then he felt shy of what he said, saying: Yet I am not the best of them. Then he came down. Shaqiq said: I sat in the circles containing Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and others, and heard no one reject what he said. In this hadith is what is in it, in addition to what we narrated before it, of mention of those who were present in place of that, that he said: among them of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were those who were among them in those days, while Ubayy in those days was alive."
"6587","And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main informed us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Sufyan, Sulayman, meaning al-Amash, narrated to me, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Hurayth ibn Zuhayr who said: When the news of Abdullah's death came to Abu al-Darda, he said: He left none like him after him. Abu al-Darda joined, in what we mentioned from him in this hadith, others than him of those we mentioned from them regarding Abdullah ibn Masud what we mentioned from them. Ubayy's death was after that. And success is from Allah."
"6588","894 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated regarding one who recited His saying: ""And he is not, over the unseen, zanin (suspected)"" or ""danin (stingy)."" Abu Jafar said: We mentioned the transmission of Asim's reading earlier in this book of ours, and its return to Ali, Abdullah and Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them. We mentioned in Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash's narration his taking it from him letter by letter, and that he used to recite this letter with dad. We mentioned Hamzah's reading and its transmission, and to whom it returns in the chain by which we mentioned it, and that he used to recite this letter likewise. We mentioned Nafi's reading and his taking it from the group he took it from, that among them was Abu Jafar, Abu Jafar's taking it from the freedman of Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, and his freedman's taking it from Ubayy, and he used to recite this letter likewise too. As for Abdullah ibn Kathir, he used to recite it with za. As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us in recitation likewise. It was narrated in Khalaf's taking Abdullah ibn Kathir's reading in this narration from Utbah ibn Aqil, from Shibl al-Makki, from Abdullah ibn Kathir. As for Abu Amr ibn al-Ala, he used to recite it with za."
"6589","As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf Abu Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Amr: that he used to recite it likewise. Likewise Abd al-Rahman al-Araj and al-Layth ibn Sad used to recite it."
"6590","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr saying: I heard al-Layth ibn Sad saying to Abd al-Hakam ibn Ayan: How does your companion, meaning Nafi, recite this letter, ""And he is not, over the unseen, danin""? Ibn Bukayr said: al-Layth used to recite it ""zanin."""
"6591","And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to me, Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated to me: that he heard al-Araj reciting it ""zanin"" with za. As for what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that, of what."
"6592","Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Amr, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: that he used to recite it ""zanin."""
"6593","And what Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu al-Mualla, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: that he recited it likewise. Except that Mujahid narrated from Ibn Abbas that he used to recite it with dad."
"6594","As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Hushaym and Khalid, meaning Ibn Abdullah, narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: ""danin"" with dad."
"6595","And as Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Mujahid, the like of it. Then we looked into the first of these two readings by what the reports indicating that brought. Those who recited it with dad, its meaning is: he is stingy with the unseen. Those who recited it with za denied of him being accused in that. We found him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not accused among his people, until they used to call him the Trustworthy, for the truthfulness of his speech and the trustworthiness he was upon."
"6596","5596 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Abbad ibn Awwam narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, Mujahid narrated to me, my master Abdullah ibn al-Saib narrated to us, he said: I was among those who built the House. I took a stone and used to worship it; if there was something in the house, I would send it to be poured upon it. Good milk would be brought and I would send it to be poured upon it. Quraysh differed and quarreled over the Stone, where to place it, until fighting with swords almost occurred among them. He said: Look at the first man to enter through the mosque gate. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, so they said: This is trustworthy. They used to call him in Jahiliyyah ""trustworthy."" They said: This is Muhammad. He came, took a garment, spread it, placed the Stone in it, and said to this clan, this clan and this clan: Let each one of you take a side of the garment. They did, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it and placed it in its place. Likewise was Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, despite what was in his heart against him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in those days, in his answering Caesar when he asked him: Do you accuse him of lying before he said what he said, meaning prophethood?"
"6597","5597 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah informed me: that Ibn Abbas informed him, he said: Abu Sufyan ibn Harb ibn Umayyah informed us: that he was in Syria among men of Quraysh who came to it as traders during the truce between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Quraysh. Abu Sufyan said: Caesar's messenger found us in part of Syria, and took me and my companions until we came to Iliya. We entered upon him, and there he was sitting in his royal مجلس with a crown upon him and his chiefs around him. He said to his interpreter: Ask them, which of them is nearest in kinship to this man who claims he is a prophet? Abu Sufyan said: I am the nearest of them to him in kinship. He said: What kinship is between you and him? I said: He is my cousin, and there was in the company in those days no man of Banu Abd Manaf but me. Caesar said: Bring him near me. Then he commanded my companions, and they were placed behind my back. Then he said to his interpreter: Say to his companions: I shall ask this man about this man who claims he is a prophet; if he lies, belie him. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, had it not been shame lest my companions transmit lying from me, I would have spoken untruthfully of him when he asked me. But I was ashamed they transmit lying from me, so I told him truthfully of him. Of what he asked him was: Do you accuse him of lying before he said what he said? I said: No. In this, of Abu Sufyan's saying, is what indicates the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with them of truthfulness in the rank he was in. Those with him of Quraysh heard that of his saying and none of them opposed him in that. Likewise was of Amr ibn al-As and Abdullah ibn Abi Rabiah with al-Najashi, despite what was in their hearts in those days against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and despite what they came for to al-Najashi in attempting what they attempted regarding the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his people who followed him."
"6598","5598 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us; and as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, al-Zuhri informed me, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the story of their going out to al-Najashi: that Quraysh sent to al-Najashi Amr ibn al-As and Abdullah ibn Abi Rabiah, the more God-fearing of the two men among us being Abdullah ibn Abi Rabiah. Amr said: By Allah, I shall surely answer him with what destroys their green prosperity; I shall inform him they claim his god whom he worships is a servant. Abdullah said: Do not do it, for they have kinship ties, though they opposed us. He said: I swear by Allah I shall do it. He returned to him after a day in which he had entered upon him. He said: O king, they say about Isa, upon him be peace, a grave saying, so send for them and ask them of him. He sent to us. He said: What do you say about Isa? They said: We say of him what Allah Almighty said, and what our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: He is Allah's servant and His spirit, which He cast to Maryam, the virgin, the chaste. She said: He lowered his hand, took a stick from the ground and said: Isa, blessings of Allah upon him, did not exceed what you said of him. In this hadith the speaker to him of this speech is Jafar ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said at the first of his speaking to him: We were with our people in a Jahiliyyah affair, worshipping idols, then Allah sent to us a man whose lineage, truthfulness and loyalty we know. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Neither Amr nor Abdullah ibn Abi Rabiah refuted it; had they been able to refute that, they would have done so. But they left that for their knowledge that the proof would stand against them for Jafar by what he said of that, so they left opposing it for that. In this too, and in what we mentioned before it, is what indicated he, upon him be peace, was not suspected among his people, and he was with them of the people of truthfulness and trust. In that is what indicated that what Allah Almighty described him in the verse we recited, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not with them in contrary to it. What He described him was a defense of him, that he conceals nothing of what was sent down to him of what they might have supposed, because of what is in it of pity and gentleness for them. Allah Exalted sent down what negates that of him. Allah Exalted also sent down upon him with that: ""Proclaim what you are commanded, and turn away from the polytheists."" He also sent down upon him: ""O Messenger, convey what was sent down to you from your Lord,"" following that with what He sent down upon him: ""And if you do not, you have not conveyed His messages."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the most acting of people for what his Lord Almighty commands him, and the firmest of them in holding to it. For this was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"6599","5599 - What Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me: that Abd Rabbih ibn Said informed him, that Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to him, from Amir al-Shabi, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda: that he heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saying: The greatest fabrication against Allah Almighty is one who says three things: one who says Muhammad saw his Lord; Muhammad concealed something of revelation; and Muhammad knows what is in tomorrow. I said: O Mother of the Believers, what did he see? She said: No, that was only Jibril; he saw him twice: once in his form at the highest horizon, and once blocking the horizons of heaven."
"6600","5600 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, the like of it. Some people of knowledge said in interpreting His saying Almighty: ""And he is not, over the unseen, stingy,"" that every learned one with knowledge does not love to teach all his knowledge to another. Allah Almighty informed them that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what He taught him is contrary to that. In that is what indicated that with him in his knowledge is other than it of merit in that, by which he exceeds what all the learned knew. Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam used to say: The choice with me for reading this letter is with za. He said: Dad and za do not differ in script except by raising the head of one above the head of the other; this may resemble in codex script and be close. Abu Jafar said: We answer him regarding that by saying: You objected to Abu Amr in his reading ""These two are surely sorcerers,"" disputing him in that that the alif is fixed in the codex in that letter. It is possible to cut the alif and join it to the letter it is of, becoming ""these two."" What binds you in contradicting the codex in that letter is like what you bound Abu Amr with in his contradicting the codex in that letter. We never saw a codex at all but what is in it is ""danin"" with dad, not ""zanin"" with za. In what we mentioned in this chapter is sufficiency for what this letter is read with, namely ""danin."" And success is from Allah."
